is less than $4$. Hence, $1$ is the remainder.\n\nThe quotient is the sum of the numbers on top of the boxes:\n\n$$\n100+5=105\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n421 \\div 4 = 105 \\,\\text{R}\\, 1\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142491 1069 "e-10204" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142491 1071 "|  | $20$ | $0$ |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $6$ | $1$$4$$4$ |  |  |\n\nLaura paid $\\[math]144$ dollars for $6$ concert tickets. Using the box model above, find out how much Laura paid for each ticket." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142491 1072 #uuid "f983e4c1-4326-510a-842d-6fedc90448e5" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142491 1074 "To find out how much Laura paid for each ticket, we need to divide $144$ by $6$.\n\nFirst, we subtract\n\n$$\n6 \\times {\\color{blue}{20}}=120\n$$\n\nfrom $144$.\n\n|  | $\\color{blue}20$ |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $6$ | $1$$4$$4$$-\\!$$1$$2$$0$$\\color{red}$$\\color{red}2$$\\color{red}4$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we bring $\\color{red}24$ to the right.\n\n|  | $20$ |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $6$ | $1$$4$$4$$-\\!$$1$$2$$0$$\\color{red}$$\\color{red}2$$\\color{red}4$ | $\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!{\\color{red}2}$$\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\color{red}4$ |  |\n\nThen, we pick a multiple of $6$ that is easy to compute but is no larger than ${\\color{red}24}$. Let's pick\n\n$$\n6 \\times {\\color{blue}4} = 24\n$$\n\nWe write $\\color{blue}4$ above the box and subtract $6 \\times {\\color{blue}4}$ from $\\color{red}24$ inside the box.\n\n|  | $20$ | $\\color{blue}4$ |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $6$ | $1$$4$$4$$-\\!$$1$$2$$0$$\\color{red}2$$\\color{red}4$ | $\\color{red}2$$\\color{red}4$$-\\!$$2$$4$$\\fbox{0}$ |  |\n\nWe get $\\fbox{0}$, so the division is done.\n\nThe quotient is the sum of the numbers on top of the boxes:\n\n$$\n20+4=24\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n144\\div 6 =24\n$$\n\nTherefore, Laura paid $\\[math]24$ for each ticket." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142492 1069 "e-10205" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142492 1071 "|  | $100$ | $20$ | $0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $6$ | $7$$7$$8$ |  |  |\n\nUse the box model above to find the value of $778 \\div 6$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142492 1072 #uuid "1b6c4b88-50aa-51e2-a5d6-6b936114d127" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142492 1074 "First, we subtract\n\n$$\n6 \\times {\\color{blue}100} = 600\n$$\n\nfrom $778$.\n\n|  | $\\color{blue}100$ |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $6$ | $7$$7$$8$$-\\!\\!\\!$$6$$0$$0$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}7$$\\color{red}8$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we bring $\\color{red}178$ to the right and subtract\n\n$$\n6 \\times {\\color{blue}20} = 120\n$$\n\nfrom it.\n\n|  | $100$ | $\\color{blue}20$ |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $6$ | $7$$7$$8$$-\\!\\!\\!$$6$$0$$0$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}7$$\\color{red}8$ | $\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}7$$\\color{red}8$$-\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$0$$\\color{red}5$$\\color{red}8$ |  |\n\nNext, we bring ${\\color{red}58}$ to the right.\n\n|  | $100$ | $20$ |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $6$ | $7$$7$$8$$-\\!\\!\\!$$6$$0$$0$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}7$$\\color{red}8$ | $\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}7$$\\color{red}8$$-\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$0$$\\color{red}5$$\\color{red}8$ | $\\!\\!\\!$$\\color{red}5$$\\color{red}8$ |\n\nFinally, we pick a multiple of $6$ that is easy to compute but is no larger than ${\\color{red}58}$. Let's pick\n\n$$\n6 \\times {\\color{blue}9} = 54\n$$\n\nWe write $\\color{blue}9$ above the box and subtract $6 \\times {\\color{blue}9}$ from $\\color{red}58$ inside the box.\n\n|  | $100$ | $20$ | $\\color{blue}9$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $6$ | $7$$7$$8$$-\\!\\!\\!$$6$$0$$0$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}7$$\\color{red}8$ | $\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}7$$\\color{red}8$$-\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$0$$\\color{red}5$$\\color{red}8$ | $\\!\\!\\!$$\\color{red}5$$\\color{red}8$$-\\!\\!\\!$$5$$4$$\\fbox{4}$ |\n\nWe can't subtract any further since $\\fbox{4}$ is less than $6$. Hence, $4$ is the remainder.\n\nThe quotient is the sum of the numbers on top of the boxes:\n\n$$\n100+20+9=129\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n778\\div 6 = 129 \\,\\text{R}\\, 4\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142493 1069 "e-10317" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142493 1071 "Olivia has $93$ beads and would like to make $10$ bracelets. If each bracelet should have the same number of beads, and Olivia wants to use as many beads as possible, how many beads will be left over after she has finished making bracelets?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142493 1072 #uuid "880dda3b-dbb2-5d25-bdb2-c8a22b0c2717" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142493 1074 "To find the number of beads that will be left over, we need to divide $93$ by $10$:\n\n$$\n93 \\div 10 = 9 \\,\\text{R}\\, {\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\color{blue}3$ beads will be left over after Olivia has finished making bracelets." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142494 1069 "e-10318" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142494 1071 "Monica has made $98$ ounces of jam that she plans to scoop into jars. If one jar holds a maximum of $10$ ounces of jam, how many jars does Monica need?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142494 1072 #uuid "668009de-cd0a-59e2-b48d-1deab895abec" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142494 1074 "To find out the number of jars Monica requires, we first need to divide $98$ by $10$:\n\n$$\n98 \\div 10 = {\\color{blue}9} \\,\\text{R}\\, {\\color{red}8}\n$$\n\nThis means that Monica will fill $\\color{blue}9$ full jars, and she will need one more jar for the remaining $\\color{red}8$ ounces.\n\nTherefore, Monica needs\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}9} + 1 = 10\n$$\n\njars in total." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142495 1069 "e-10338" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142495 1071 "|  | $10$ | $0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $3$ | $5$$1$$-$$0$$0$$0$$0$ | $0$$0$$-$$0$$0$$\\fbox{0}$ |\n\nUse the box model above to compute the value of $51 \\div 3$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142495 1072 #uuid "a2dc59c4-04d5-5b67-bcb8-e80801f599df" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142495 1074 "**Step 1**: First, we subtract\n\n$$\n3 \\times {\\color{blue}10} = 30\n$$\n\nfrom $51$.\n\n|  | ${\\color{blue}10}$ | $0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $3$ | $5$$1$$-\\!\\!$$3$$0$$2$$1$ | $0$$0$$-\\!\\!$$0$$0$$\\fbox{0}$ |\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we bring $\\color{red}21$ to the right.\n\n|  | $10$ | $0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $3$ | $5$$1$$-\\!\\!$$3$$0$$2$$1$ | $2$$1$$-\\!\\!$$0$$0$$\\fbox{0}$ |\n\n**Step 3**: Pick a multiple of $3$ that is easy to compute but is no larger than ${\\color{red}21}$. Let's pick\n\n$$\n3 \\times {\\color{blue}7} = 21\n$$\n\nWe write $\\color{blue}7$ above the box and subtract $3 \\times {\\color{blue}7}$ from $\\color{red}21$ inside the box.\n\n|  | $10$ | ${\\color{blue}7}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $3$ | $5$$1$$-\\!\\!$$3$$0$$2$$1$ | $2$$1$$-\\!\\!$$2$$1$$\\fbox{0}$ |\n\nWe're left with $\\fbox{0}$, so the division is done.\n\n**Step 4**: The quotient is the sum of the numbers on top of the boxes:\n\n$$\n10+7=17\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n51 \\div 3 = 17\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142496 1069 "e-10534" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142496 1071 "Dorothy has $19\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of potatoes she would like to pack into bags. One bag holds a maximum of $5\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of potatoes. How many bags will Dorothy fill completely if all but one are filled?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142496 1072 #uuid "2d6278cb-127a-5bd0-a702-a092426aa458" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142496 1074 "To find the number of bags needed, we first need to divide $19$ by $5$:\n\n$$\n19 \\div 5= {\\color{blue}3} \\,\\text{R}\\, {\\color{red}4}\n$$\n\nThis means that Dorothy will fill $\\color{blue}3$ bags, and she will need one more bag for the extra ${\\color{red}4}\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of potatoes.\n\nTherefore, Dorothy will fill ${\\color{blue}3}$ bags completely." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142497 1069 "e-10563" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142497 1071 "What is the next shape in the sequence below?\n\n![](images/80/808585690bae17a6925ef1555f342c180d69a698769aca2ae9638f61132b227a.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142497 1072 #uuid "163628fe-d03d-5900-bc13-348b6b39d2c5" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142497 1074 "We can see that the rule for the pattern is \"triangle pointing left, circle, triangle pointing right, circle, and repeat.\"\n\n![](images/46/465dbdcf9e254cb14b6ebf01cbd16508d65ff9eeff266d8fe20f2efa1c0d0c3a.png)\n\nThe next shape comes after a circle, and the previous triangle points to the left. Therefore, the missing shape is a triangle pointing to the right." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142498 1069 "e-10564" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142498 1071 "Consider the following pattern:\n\n$14,\\,11,\\,8,\\,5$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The rule is \"subtract $2$.\"\n2. The next number in the pattern is $2$.\n3. The numbers in the pattern alternate between even and odd." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142498 1072 #uuid "d7df8abc-326b-56c2-84b6-35bf2299f2f5" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142498 1074 "Let's look at each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. We can see that the rule for the pattern is \"subtract $3$.\"![](images/eb/eb9fed376584e6f00614097adba767a64b630424a1528995082462b23a2179a0.png)\n- Statement II is true. The next number comes after $5$, and is therefore equal to\n$5-3 = 2$.\n- Statement III is true. When we subtract $3$ from an odd number we always obtain an even number, and when we subtract $3$ from an even number we always obtain an odd number. Therefore, the terms in the pattern alternate between even and odd.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142499 1069 "e-10722" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142499 1071 "A manufacturer of bias tape must package $9,252$ yards of tape into rolls containing $5$ yards each. After packaging, how many yards of tape will be left over?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142499 1072 #uuid "46fd744d-5c52-5866-821d-7acf7f39f6b1" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142499 1074 "To determine how many yards of the tape will be left, we need to find the remainder of $9,252\\div 5$.\n\nTo divide $9,252$ by $5$, we use long division:\n\n| $1$$8$$5$$0$$5$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$4$$2$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$0$$2$$5$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$5$$0$$2$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$0$$2$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n9,252 \\div 5 = 1,850 \\,\\text{R}\\, {\\color{blue}{2}}\n$$\n\nSo, $1,850$ rolls of tape will be packaged into rolls, and $\\color{blue}2$ yards will be left over." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142500 1069 "e-10723" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142500 1071 "Deborah, a clothing company employee, has to pack $112$ shirts into boxes. If each box can hold up to $6$ shirts, what is the smallest number of boxes that she needs to pack the shirts?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142500 1072 #uuid "667094b8-4cb0-59e4-92f9-f7562439af2d" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142500 1074 "To figure out the smallest number of boxes Deborah needs, we need to divide $112\\div 6$.\n\nTo divide $112$ by $6$, we use long division:\n\n|  |  | $1$ | $8$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $6$ | $\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2}$ |  |  |\n|  | $-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$ | $6$ |  |\n|  |  | $5$ | $2$ |\n|  | $-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n|  |  |  | $4$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n112 \\div 6 = 18 \\,\\text{R}\\, {\\color{blue}{4}}\n$$\n\nThis means that Deborah will fill $18$ boxes and have ${\\color{blue}{4}}$ shirts left over.\n\nTo hold the leftover shirts, Deborah will need an additional box. Therefore, she will need\n\n$$\n18+1 =19\n$$\n\nboxes in total." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142501 1069 "e-10735" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142501 1071 "An architect orders $563$ boards for a project. If the boards they need come in packs of $4$, how many fully-filled packs will they receive?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142501 1072 #uuid "2601ad66-1d73-5c2f-8be7-4184ef4c3d33" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142501 1074 "To find out the number of fully-filled packs the architect will receive, we need to compute the quotient of $563 \\div 4$.\n\nTo divide $563$ by $4$, we use long division:\n\n| $1$$4$$0$$4$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$1$$6$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$6$$0$$3$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$0$$0$${3}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n563 \\div 4 = {\\color{blue}{140}}\\,\\text{R}\\,3\n$$\n\nSo, the architect will receive ${\\color{blue}{140}}$ fully-filled packs." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142502 1069 "e-12541" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142502 1071 "![](images/26/26ebb64081e92e2b8370f5a940a1b3858161bd80245bc390740abef8310757af.png)\n\nConsider the comparison statement above. By putting both fractions over a denominator of $10$, this comparison statement becomes\n\n$0$\n\nWhat is the missing number?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142502 1072 #uuid "209c4b34-aea3-5541-bc1b-f111ce951094" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142502 1074 "The denominators are $2$ and $10$, and $10$ is a multiple of $2$. In fact\n\n$$\n10 = {\\color{purple}{5}}\\times 2\n$$\n\nWe can put both fractions over a common denominator of $10$ by splitting each of the parts on the left into $\\color{purple}5$ equal pieces.\n\n![](images/13/133c1cf0bfad1721bd77fb1aa74d92ac2d2b7d69f1b6c365f5e93e76db417c46.png)\n\nThe fractions now have the same denominator, $10$, and the comparison statement is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{5}}{10} < \\dfrac6{10}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $5$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142503 1069 "e-12551" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142503 1071 "![](images/d1/d1dc6ac30443523a083071238bfc44d49d9967cdb3c227148261df70256368ca.png)\n\nConsider the comparison statement above. By putting both fractions over a denominator of $10$, this comparison statement becomes\n$0$\n\nWhat is the missing number?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142503 1072 #uuid "d75f46bb-2da2-53f9-9222-7f08ddad5e8a" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142503 1074 "The left and right sides represent fractions with denominators of ${\\color{red}{2}}$ and ${\\color{blue}{5}}$, respectively.\nTo compare them, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{2}} \\times {\\color{blue}{5}} = 10\n$$\n\nWe put the left fraction over a denominator of $10$ by splitting each of its parts into ${\\color{blue}{5}}$ equal pieces.\n\n![](images/aa/aa84ccd2ca4643170580359fc7e4c39d06fbccce0c79aa3eea04afc4b1c99447.png)\n\nWe put the right side over a denominator of $10$ by splitting each of its parts into ${\\color{red}{2}}$ equal pieces.\n\n![](images/94/94dd224dad45fd8216e883bd98586918b6291c8e689739438c2b2c6332b8076b.png)\n\nThe fractions now have the same denominator, $10$, and the comparison statement is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{10} > \\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{2}}{10}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $2$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142504 1069 "e-12565" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142504 1071 "![](images/1b/1ba7df6d3e22669f0792e9070aad212e52a4539f5804ef34c7ce8c384886d8a5.png)\n\nConsider the comparison statement above. By putting the fractions over a common denominator, we can rewrite this comparison statement as follows:\n\n$0$\n\nWhat is the missing fraction?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142504 1072 #uuid "a9307dbd-1cbb-519f-8b12-d86b4ce6fb26" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142504 1074 "The denominators are $2$ and $8$, and $8$ is a multiple of $2$. In fact\n\n$$\n8 = {\\color{purple}{4}}\\times 2\n$$\n\nWe can put both fractions over a common denominator of $8$ by splitting each of the parts on the left into $\\color{purple}4$ equal pieces.\n\n![](images/9b/9ba06aec80cd4b225099b7c742e24194f5666d9a42822cd1f8bc6eff75ed7dad.png)\n\nThe fractions now have the same denominator, $8$, and the comparison statement is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{4}}{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{8}} > \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing fraction is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac48\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142505 1069 "e-12566" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142505 1071 "![](images/35/35e2bb6fb6da8cf3e8c43ad89c9f497d04ea03de68093191717c4299e5475cc2.png)\n\nConsider the fraction comparison above. By putting the fractions over a common denominator, we can rewrite this comparison statement as follows:\n$0$\n\nWhat number is missing from *both* boxes?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142505 1072 #uuid "40dc55d0-4610-5305-98d9-84701a7f29b3" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142505 1074 "The left and right sides represent fractions with denominators of ${\\color{red}{4}}$ and ${\\color{blue}{3}}$, respectively.\nTo compare them, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{4}} \\times {\\color{blue}{3}} = 12\n$$\n\nWe put the left fraction over a denominator of $12$ by splitting each of its parts into ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ equal pieces.\n\n![](images/df/df418278eb545bf14d477a350e1de733e0ed9fad373b7694bd678eedbca60750.png)\n\nWe put the right side over a denominator of $12$ by splitting each of its parts into ${\\color{red}{4}}$ equal pieces.\n\n![](images/ba/ba4beb7dae7ee7b36e70eb0bdcba2fd01f4fe3973108e678847443308cacae8c.png)\n\nThe fractions now have the same denominator, $12$, and the comparison statement is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{12}} \\lt \\dfrac{4}{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{12}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number missing from both boxes is $12$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142506 1069 "e-12601" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142506 1071 "What line plot represents the data set below?\n\n$\\dfrac{1}{3} \\quad \\dfrac{5}{6} \\quad \\dfrac{1}{6} \\quad \\dfrac{1}{3} \\quad \\dfrac{1}{6}$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142506 1072 #uuid "8a297440-3a03-577a-9d4e-b9dd7722c0ab" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142506 1074 "First, we identify any values that are the same:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{1}{3}}} \\quad {\\color{red}{\\dfrac{5}{6}}} \\quad \\dfrac{1}{6} \\quad {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{1}{3}}} \\quad \\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nThen, we order the data from the least to the greatest. To compare fractions with unlike denominators, we need to express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nHere, we can make a common denominator of $6$.\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{3}}\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $6$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}= \\dfrac{1 \\times 2}{3 \\times 2} = {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{2}{6}}\n$$\n\nSo our data set is equivalent to:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{2}{6}}} \\quad {\\color{red}{\\dfrac{5}{6}}} \\quad \\dfrac{1}{6} \\quad {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{2}{6}}} \\quad \\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nNow, we order the dataset:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{\\color{black}\\dfrac{1}{6}} \\quad {\\color{black}\\dfrac{1}{6}}}_{2} \\quad \\underbrace{{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{2}{6}} \\quad {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{2}{6}}}_{2} \\quad \\underbrace{{\\color{red}\\dfrac{5}{6}}}_{1}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can create a line plot. We write each fraction on the number line, and then place a cross above the fraction for each time it occurs in the dataset:\n\n![](images/bc/bc7ae5ddf801ade08d35200c74fbf4cfe99610af9f884efd7eef20d9d52794c1.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142507 1069 "e-12602" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142507 1071 "Mrs. Smith distributes pieces of half-apple to her students. These pieces are given to Margaret, Daniel, and Rose. The table below shows the number of half-apples each child received.\n\n| Child | Half-Apples |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Margaret | $4$ |\n| Daniel | $3$ |\n| Rose | $1$ |\n\nBy letting each cross represent a different child, create a line plot showing the frequency of *whole* apples handed out to the children." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142507 1072 #uuid "33820b60-e2de-537a-adc7-c55d4170c8f7" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142507 1074 "First, we find the number of whole apples given to each child.\n\n- $4$ halves is equivalent to $4 \\times \\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{4}{2} = 2$ apples.\n- $3$ halves is equivalent to $3 \\times \\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{3}{2} = 1\\, \\dfrac{1}{2}$ apples.\n- $1$ half is equivalent to $1 \\times \\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ of an apple.\n\nWe organize these results in the table:\n\n| Child | Half-Apples | Number of Apples |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Margaret | $4$ | $2$ |\n| Daniel | $3$ | $1 \\, \\dfrac{1}{2}$ |\n| Rose | $1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ |\n\nSo, we have the following dataset for the number of apples given to each child:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} \\qquad 1\\, \\dfrac{1}{2} \\qquad 2\n$$\n\nThis dataset has the following line plot:\n\n![](images/ca/ca5fe6bedfd44c1a0f3e3a7bc2ae858d922c74d1ecc815ee0ac7a17f8ab4b4e7.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142508 1069 "e-12603" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142508 1071 "The following line plot shows the distance traveled, in miles, by various professional cyclists as part of their morning training. Each cross represents a different cyclist. What is the most common distance the cyclists travel during training?\n\n![](images/6e/6e8c51700f6d1d7221add0a3d10f0957218ec2ada7aa901791eca1780bc4ca95.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142508 1072 #uuid "069ee100-5307-562b-801d-d594b6d2c624" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142508 1074 "Looking at the graph, the most common distance traveled by cyclists is\n\n$$\n8 \\, \\dfrac{1}{2}\\, \\textrm{mi}\n$$\n\nA total of $6$ cyclists traveled this far.\n\n![](images/e9/e94fa240a289039a4f168d4fcfa97d64ee4e95e4976b2b947a75902584d82867.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142509 1069 "e-12604" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142509 1071 "![](images/39/39b621655e29368ecc7af617dcd1dbf219455643f7dc8e5cc63ffe345b572313.png)\n\nJohn's mother made a chocolate cake for a charity day. She divided the cake into $12$ equal parts. The line plot above shows the fraction of cake each customer bought, where each cross represents a different person. How many people purchased at most $2$ pieces of this cake?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142509 1072 #uuid "5fd0a84d-c311-5b1f-bf95-1919b71475db" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142509 1074 "John's mother divided the cake into $12$ equal parts, so a piece of cake is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{12}\n$$\n\nof the whole cake.\n\nTo see how many people bought $2$ pieces or less, we focus on the portion of the graph that corresponds to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{12}\n$$\n\nor less:\n\n![](images/15/15b0f7d044ffcf5d5f288d5a0ded43e0f3a97749b5e8624ed2aae62b6a38b7c6.png)\n\nThe plot tells us the following information:\n\n- ${\\color{red}{3}}$ people bought $\\dfrac{1}{12}$ of cake.\n- ${\\color{red}{1}}$ person bought $\\dfrac{2}{12}$ of cake.\n\nTherefore, a total of\n\n$$\n3 + 1 = 4\n$$\n\npeople bought at most $2$ pieces of the cake." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142510 1069 "e-12772" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142510 1071 "What is the missing number in the following equality?\n\n$0$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142510 1072 #uuid "21bed255-c78a-5ea2-91e4-45738352f525" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142510 1074 "We need to factor the expression $18+24$.\n\nFirst, let's find the greatest common factor of $18$ and $24$. We list the factors of each number, and find the greatest number that appears in both lists:\n\n- Factors of $18$: $\\quad 1, 2, 3, {\\color{blue}6}, 9, 18$\n- Factors of $24$: $\\quad 1, 2, 3, 4, {\\color{blue}6}, 8, 12, 24$\n\nSo the greatest common factor of $18$ and $24$ is ${\\color{blue}{6}}$.\n\nNext, we write each number as a product of the greatest common factor and another factor:\n\n- $18 = {\\color{blue}6} \\times {\\color{red}3}$\n- $24 = {\\color{blue}6} \\times {\\color{red}4}$\n\nFinally, to factor the given expression, we place the greatest common factor ${\\color{blue}6}$ outside the parentheses and leave the sum of the other two factors inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n18+24=\\underbrace{{\\color{blue}{6}}\\times \\color{red}{3}}_{18} + \\underbrace{{\\color{blue}{6}}\\times \\color{red}{4}}_{24} = {\\color{blue}{6}}({\\color{red}{3}}+ {\\color{red}{4}})\n$$\n\nSo the missing number is $6$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142511 1069 "e-12773" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142511 1071 "Factor the expression $8+28$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142511 1072 #uuid "5c057ccd-f2a9-558b-8ca1-5db420c6c3a6" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142511 1074 "Let's find the greatest common factor of $8$ and $28$. We list the factors of each number. Then, we find the largest number that appears in both lists:\n\n- Factors of $8$: $\\quad 1, 2, {\\color{blue}4}, 8$\n- Factors of $28$: $\\quad 1, 2, {\\color{blue}4}, 7, 14, 28$\n\nSo the greatest common factor of $8$ and $28$ is ${\\color{blue}{4}}$.\n\nNext, we write each number as a product of the greatest common factor and another factor:\n\n- $8 = {\\color{blue}4} \\times {\\color{red}{2}}$\n- $28 = {\\color{blue}4} \\times {\\color{red}{7}}$\n\nFinally, to factor the given expression, we place the greatest common factor ${\\color{blue}4}$ outside the parentheses and leave the sum of the other two factors inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n8+28=\\underbrace{{\\color{blue}{4}}\\times {\\color{red}{2}}}_{8} + \\underbrace{{\\color{blue}{4}}\\times {\\color{red}{7}}}_{28} = {\\color{blue}{4}}({\\color{red}{2}}+{\\color{red}{7}})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression $8+28$ factors into $4(2+7)$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142512 1069 "e-12774" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142512 1071 "Factor the expression $12+21$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142512 1072 #uuid "bf9b1e12-2703-59a6-8f46-053a6b7c5736" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142512 1074 "First, we find the greatest common factor of $12$ and $21$. We list the factors of each number, and find the greatest number that appears in both lists:\n\n- Factors of $12$: $\\quad 1, 2, {\\color{blue}3}, 4, 6, 12$\n- Factors of $21$: $\\quad 1, {\\color{blue}3}, 7, 21$\n\nSo the greatest common factor of $12$ and $21$ is ${\\color{blue}{3}}$.\n\nNext, we write each number as a product of the greatest common factor and another factor:\n\n- $12 = {\\color{blue}3} \\times {\\color{red}{4}}$\n- $21 = {\\color{blue}3} \\times {\\color{red}{7}}$\n\nFinally, to factor the given expression, we place the greatest common factor $\\color{blue}3$ outside the parentheses and leave the sum of the other two factors inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n12+21=\\underbrace{{\\color{blue}{3}}\\times {\\color{red}{4}}}_{12} + \\underbrace{{\\color{blue}{3}}\\times {\\color{red}{7}}}_{21} = {\\color{blue}{3}}({\\color{red}{4}}+{\\color{red}{7}})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression $12+21$ factors into $3(4+7)$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142513 1069 "e-12777" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142513 1071 "What is $4$ multiplied by $21$?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142513 1072 #uuid "8945aebf-7914-5e12-bde9-960bfc5b78f6" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142513 1074 "We write our multiplication this way:\n\n$$\n2\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}4 \\mid 48 + 720 = 76800000000\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the ones:\n\n$$\n_(0); 2\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}4; 4 \\mid 1 \\cdot 4 = 40000000000000000; Write:4\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply the tens:\n\n$$\n_(0); 2\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}4; 8 \\mid 4 \\mid 2 \\cdot 4 = 80000000000000000; Write:8\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n21 \\times 4 = 84\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142514 1069 "e-12778" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142514 1071 "Calculate $37\\times 4$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142514 1072 #uuid "6f2bc7a1-7b22-54f0-a8dc-4a8a12113cca" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142514 1074 "We write it this way:\n\n$$\n3\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}4 \\mid 3 \\cdot 4 = 12and2 + 12 = 14\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the ones:\n\n$$\n_(2); 3\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}4; 8 \\mid 7 \\cdot 4 = 28and2 + 12 = 14; Carry:2; Write:8\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply the tens:\n\n$$\n_(2); 3\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}4; 1\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 8\\end{vmatrix}3 \\cdot 4 = 12and2 + 12 = 14; Carry:-; Write:14\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n37 \\times 4 = 148\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142515 1069 "e-12779" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142515 1071 "As part of a school assignment, each student in a class must interview $25$ people about their sports preferences. If there are $7$ students in the class, how many interviews will they need to conduct in total?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142515 1072 #uuid "e8887a29-b8d3-5be4-b396-52e3cc748236" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142515 1074 "To calculate the total number of interviews, we need to multiply $25$ by $7$.\n\n$$\n2\\begin{vmatrix}5 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}7 \\mid 14and3 + 14 = 170000\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the ones:\n\n$$\n_(3); 2\\begin{vmatrix}5 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}7; 5 \\mid 5 \\cdot 7 = 3500000000000000000; Carry:3; Write:5\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply the tens:\n\n$$\n_(3); 2\\begin{vmatrix}5 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}7; 1\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 5\\end{vmatrix}2 \\cdot 7 = 14and3 + 14 = 17; Carry:-; Write:17\n$$\n\nTherefore, there will be a total of $175$ interviews." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142516 1069 "e-13393" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142516 1071 "Find the value of $5\\times \\dfrac{1}{12}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142516 1072 #uuid "714ed22d-8268-502d-9d74-12620cb1c4fe" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142516 1074 "To multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n5\\times \\dfrac{1}{12}= \\dfrac{5\\times 1}{12} = \\dfrac{5}{12}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142517 1069 "e-13394" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142517 1071 "Multiply $\\dfrac{2}{3}\\times 3$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142517 1072 #uuid "499b112b-863b-568a-bd56-f32d66b619fc" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142517 1074 "To multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\times 3}{3} = \\dfrac{6}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify this fraction by interpreting\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 6 3\n$$\n\nas a division:\n\n$$\n6\\div 3 =2\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142518 1069 "e-13395" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142518 1071 "Multiply $\\dfrac{1}{6}\\times 4$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142518 1072 #uuid "da8475a1-d48e-5989-b1a9-a7c72a205fd2" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142518 1074 "To multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1\\times 4}{6} = \\dfrac{4}{6}\n$$\n\nTo simplify this fraction, we can divide the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4\\div 2}{6\\div 2} = \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142519 1069 "e-13396" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142519 1071 "Find the value $9\\times\\dfrac 3 {4}$ as a mixed number." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142519 1072 #uuid "494ad564-7a01-533e-887a-6c83a2fb4e78" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142519 1074 "To multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n9\\times \\dfrac{3}{4} = \\dfrac{9\\times 3}{4} = \\dfrac{27}{4}\n$$\n\nWe now convert this to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n27\\div 4 = 6\\,\\textrm{R} 3 = 6\\,\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142520 1069 "e-13420" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142520 1071 "Mary wishes to find the value of $4\\times748$. She proceeds as follows:\n\n$4 \\cdot 700|= [math]\\phantom{0000}[/math]; 4 \\cdot 40|= [math]\\phantom{000}[/math]; 4 \\cdot 8|= [math]\\phantom{00}[/math]$\n\nFind the missing numbers, and use your answers to find the value of $4\\times748$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142520 1072 #uuid "dec3a74e-6e8c-5dcf-a3e5-e81503207ab3" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142520 1074 "Notice that $748$ in expanded form is\n\n$$\n748 = 700+40+8\n$$\n\nSo, we can compute $4\\times 748$ by filling in the missing numbers and adding the results.\n\nFirst, let's find the missing values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4 \\cdot 700 &= [math]\\,2,800\\,[/math] \\\\\n4 \\cdot 40 &= [math]\\,160\\,[/math] \\\\\n4 \\cdot 8 &= [math]\\,32\\,[/math]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now add these results:\n\n$$\n2\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ + & 1 & 6\\end{vmatrix}0; +\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 9\\end{vmatrix}9 \\mid 2\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4\\times748 = 2,992\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142521 1069 "e-13421" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142521 1071 "What is the value of $745 \\times 8$?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142521 1072 #uuid "b0ffcab3-f3f0-569d-b21b-cb84cd713391" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142521 1074 "First, let's write the three-digit number $(745)$ in expanded form:\n\n$$\n745 = {\\color{blue}{700}} + {\\color{blue}{40}} + {\\color{blue}{5}}\n$$\n\nWe now multiply all numbers in our expanded form by ${\\color{red}{8}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n700 \\cdot 8 &= 5, 600 \\\\\n40 \\cdot 8 &= 320 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 8 &= 40\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now add these results:\n\n$$\n5\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ + & 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}0; +\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 9\\end{vmatrix}6 \\mid 0\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n745 \\times 8 = 5,960\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142522 1069 "e-13423" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142522 1071 "An adhesive tape has a length of $164$ feet. What is the total length of $6$ adhesive tapes?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142522 1072 #uuid "3bd43dd2-82c8-5fc7-9b1e-79118b74bbca" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142522 1074 "To figure out the total length of $6$ tapes, we need to compute the value of\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{6}} \\times 164\n$$\n\nFirst, let's write the three-digit number $(164)$ in expanded form:\n\n$$\n164 = {\\color{blue}{100}} + {\\color{blue}{60}} + {\\color{blue}{4}}\n$$\n\nWe now multiply all numbers in our expanded form by ${\\color{red}{6}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 \\cdot 100 &= 600 \\\\\n6 \\cdot 60 &= 360 \\\\\n6 \\cdot 4 &= 24\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now add these results:\n\n$$\n6\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ + & 3\\end{vmatrix}6\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}2\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ 9\\end{vmatrix}8 \\mid 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, six tapes cover $984$ feet." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142523 1069 "e-13426" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142523 1071 "You're given the following multiplication problem:\n$10 \\times 7 = 70$\nUse the equation above and the relationship between multiplication and division to determine which of the following is the solution to $98 \\div 7$.\n\n1. $11$\n2. $12$\n3. $13$\n4. $14$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142523 1072 #uuid "8fb07ccc-8fcc-5dfa-a8b6-78528e938331" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142523 1074 "If $0$ then by the relationship between multiplication and division, we have\n$0$\n\nWe start with the given multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n10 \\times 7 = {\\color{blue}{70}}\n$$\n\nLet's now try the numbers in the list, *starting with the smallest*. We can use repeated addition to compute the products.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n11 \\cdot 7 &= 70 + 7 = 77\\cdot \\\\\n12 \\cdot 7 &= 77 + 7 = 84\\cdot \\\\\n13 \\cdot 7 &= 84 + 7 = 91\\cdot \\\\\n14 \\cdot 7 &= 91 + 7 = 98✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n98 \\div 7 = 14\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142524 1069 "e-13427" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142524 1071 "You're given the following multiplication problem:\n$120 \\times 4 = 480$\nUse the equation above and the relationship between multiplication and division to find which of the following is the solution to $488 \\div 4$.\n\n1. $121$\n2. $122$\n3. $123$\n4. $124$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142524 1072 #uuid "c7500732-01a6-5f18-949e-d8698e39fa8c" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142524 1074 "If $0$ then by the relationship between multiplication and division, we have\n$0$\n\nWe start with the given multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n120 \\times 4 = {\\color{blue}{480}}\n$$\n\nLet's try the numbers in the list, *starting with the smallest*. We can use repeated addition to compute the products.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n121 \\cdot 4 &= 480 + 4 = 484\\cdot \\\\\n122 \\cdot 4 &= 484 + 4 = 488✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n488 \\div 4 = 122\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142525 1069 "e-13428" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142525 1071 "You're given the following multiplication problem:\n$70 \\times 4 = 280$\nUse the equation above and the relationship between multiplication and division to find which of the following is the solution to $268 \\div 4$.\n\n1. $69$\n2. $68$\n3. $67$\n4. $66$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142525 1072 #uuid "c9855999-2298-5cdc-86ad-a218494bf2c1" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142525 1074 "If $0$ then by the relationship between multiplication and division, we have\n$0$\n\nWe start with the given multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n70 \\times 4 = {\\color{blue}{280}}\n$$\n\nLet's now try the numbers in the list, starting with the *largest*. We can use repeated subtraction to compute the products.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n69 \\cdot 4 &= 280 - 4 = 276\\cdot \\\\\n68 \\cdot 4 &= 276 - 4 = 272\\cdot \\\\\n67 \\cdot 4 &= 272 - 4 = 268✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n268 \\div 4 = 67\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142526 1069 "e-13436" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142526 1071 "How many pints are in $15$ gallons?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142526 1072 #uuid "e0f7fc73-9993-5bbc-a881-ae0a3e99464e" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142526 1074 "We start with the unit conversion between gallons and pints:\n\n$$\n1\\, \\textrm{gallon} =8\\, \\textrm{pints}\n$$\n\nWe want to figure out how many pints are in $15$ gallons. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $15{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n15 \\cdot 1gallon &= 15 \\cdot 8pints \\\\\n15gallons &= 15 \\cdot 8pints\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute $15\\times 8$ using an area model:\n\n![](images/76/76a9b073c35415e2c63778a2f2ded1a1a81aac85ee01c9184a6b8e08e920b871.png)\n\nAccording to our model,\n\n$$\n15\\times 8 = 80+40 = 120\n$$\n\nTherefore, $15$ gallons equals $120$ pints." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142527 1069 "e-13437" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142527 1071 "A container can hold $64$ pints of water. How many cups of water is this?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142527 1072 #uuid "b24a6c93-7d93-5d22-8429-2279b0c3857a" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142527 1074 "We start with the unit conversion between pints and cups:\n\n$$\n1\\, \\textrm{pint} =2\\, \\textrm{cups}\n$$\n\nWe want to figure out how many cups are in $64$ pints. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $64{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n64 \\cdot 1pint &= 64 \\cdot 2cups \\\\\n64pints &= 64 \\cdot 2cups\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute $64\\times 2$ using an area model:\n\n![](images/b3/b30c4c720a3d1a44681821893257b8674d3afcb3d3f71b85e9c5fb0cb25f0718.png)\n\nAccording to our model,\n\n$$\n64\\times 2 = 120+8 = 128\n$$\n\nTherefore, the container can hold $128$ cups of water." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142528 1069 "e-13438" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142528 1071 "How many fluid ounces are in $21$ cups?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142528 1072 #uuid "f64603a9-0bd3-5177-81d3-63aeccbbbe9b" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142528 1074 "We start with the unit conversion between fluid ounces and cups:\n\n$$\n1 \\,\\textrm{cup}=8 \\,\\textrm{fluid ounces}\n$$\n\nWe want to figure out how many fluid ounces are in $21$ cups. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $21{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n21 \\cdot 1cup &= 21 \\cdot 8fluid ounces \\\\\n21cups &= 21 \\cdot 8fluid ounces\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute $21\\times 8$ using an area model:\n\n![](images/12/1215f0a2a7147e37ec486c45f3162f532f9d3938566e5845992c1c4476ab54bb.png)\n\nAccording to our model,\n\n$$\n21\\times 8 = 160+8 = 168\n$$\n\nTherefore, $21$ cups equals $168$ fluid ounces." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142529 1069 "e-13439" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142529 1071 "Dylan's car consumed $14.5$ liters of gasoline while traveling between two cities. How much is this in milliliters?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142529 1072 #uuid "5fc67e2c-11ca-58aa-b55e-36b1f39d7c4d" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142529 1074 "We start with the unit conversion between liters and milliliters:\n\n$$\n1\\, \\textrm{liter} =1,000\\, \\textrm{milliliters}\n$$\n\nWe want to figure out how many milliliters are in $14.5$ liters. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $14.5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n14.5 \\cdot 1liter &= 14.5 \\cdot 1, 000milliliters \\\\\n14.5liters &= 14, 500milliliters\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $14.5$ liters equals $14,500$ milliliters." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142530 1069 "e-13440" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142530 1071 "How many pounds are in $24$ tons?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142530 1072 #uuid "9f07cc42-ef07-5cf4-a97b-0ddabc3f01a4" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142530 1074 "We start with the unit conversion between tons and pounds:\n\n$$\n1\\, \\textrm{ton} =2,000\\, \\textrm{lb}\n$$\n\nRemember that $\\textrm{lb}$ is the unit symbol for pounds.\n\nWe want to figure out how many pounds are in ${\\color{blue}{24}}$ tons. So, we multiply *both* sides of the above equation by ${\\color{blue}{24}}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n24 \\cdot 1ton &= 24 \\cdot 2, 000lb \\\\\n24tons &= 48, 000lb\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $24$ tons is equal to $48,000\\,\\textrm{lb}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142531 1069 "e-13441" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142531 1071 "Luis wants to lose $26\\,\\textrm{lb}$ in weight over the next twelve months. How much is this in ounces?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142531 1072 #uuid "14be3128-4cdc-5d9f-b9bb-330395f1464f" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142531 1074 "We start with the unit conversion between pounds and ounces:\n\n$$\n1 \\,\\textrm{lb}=16 \\,\\textrm{oz}\n$$\n\nWe want to figure out how many ounces are in $26$ pounds. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $26{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n26 \\cdot 1lb &= 26 \\cdot 16oz \\\\\n26lb &= 26 \\cdot 16oz\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the value of $26\\times 16$ using an area model:\n\n![](images/8a/8a2b72e316779b9c3022779c46c956d7d835286e5c9b56580230d99548ff5071.png)\n\nAccording to our model,\n\n$$\n26\\times 16 = 200+60+120+36 = 416\n$$\n\nTherefore, $26\\,\\textrm{lb}$ is equivalent to $416\\,\\textrm{oz}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142532 1069 "e-13442" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142532 1071 "The weight of a watermelon is $8\\,\\textrm{kg}$. How much is this in grams?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142532 1072 #uuid "ec97bd0a-c9fa-5f3a-be98-ab516c082dd1" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142532 1074 "We start with the unit conversion between kilograms and grams:\n\n$$\n1 \\,\\textrm{kg}=1,000 \\,\\textrm{g}\n$$\n\nWe want to figure out how many grams are in $8$ kilograms. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $8{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8 \\cdot 1kg &= 8 \\cdot 1, 000g \\\\\n8kg &= 8, 000g\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the weight of the watermelon is $8,000\\,\\textrm{g}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142533 1069 "e-13443" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142533 1071 "How many milligrams are in $40\\,\\textrm{g}$?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142533 1072 #uuid "4c34f480-16fb-5c68-bc1b-744c9992c2b6" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142533 1074 "We need to convert $40$ grams into milligrams.\n\nWe start with the unit conversion between grams and milligrams:\n\n$$\n1\\, \\textrm{g} =1,000\\, \\textrm{mg}\n$$\n\nWe want to figure out how many milligrams are in $40$ grams. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $40{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n40 \\cdot 1g &= 40 \\cdot 1, 000mg \\\\\n40g &= 40, 000mg\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $40\\,\\textrm{g}$ equals $40,000\\, \\textrm{mg}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142534 1069 "e-13454" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142534 1071 "Find the least common multiple of $3$ and $7$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142534 1072 #uuid "67c29e35-a48b-54a3-b4e4-7109b6381af1" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142534 1074 "First, we list the multiples of each number. Then, we find the smallest number that appears in both lists.\n\n- Multiples of $3$: $\\quad 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 18, {\\color{blue}21}, \\ldots$\n- Multiples of $7$: $\\quad 7, 14, {\\color{blue}21}, 28, \\ldots$\n\nWe conclude that the least common multiple of $3$ and $7$ is ${\\color{blue}{21}}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142535 1069 "e-13456" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142535 1071 "Determine the least common multiple of $6$ and $10$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142535 1072 #uuid "dd4cce99-a9a4-502d-8aff-c363c318cd23" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142535 1074 "First, we list the multiples of each number. Then, we find the smallest number that appears in both lists.\n\n- Multiples of $6$: $\\quad 6, 12, 18, 24, {\\color{blue}30}, \\ldots$\n- Multiples of $10$: $\\quad 10, 20, {\\color{blue}30}, 40, \\ldots$\n\nWe conclude that the least common multiple of $6$ and $10$ is ${\\color{blue}{30}}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142536 1069 "e-13458" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142536 1071 "Mathias and Bill both washed their cars today. Mathias washes his car every $8$ days, while Bill washes his car every $10$ days. How often do Mathias and Bill both wash their cars on the same day?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142536 1072 #uuid "9fb3d065-77aa-50df-95fc-f061f1f4cfbd" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142536 1074 "We can solve this problem by finding the least common multiple of $8$ and $10$.\n\nFirst, we list the multiples of $8$ and $10$. Then, we find the smallest number that appears in both lists.\n\n- Multiples of $8$: $\\quad 8, 16, 24, 32, {\\color{blue}40}, \\ldots$\n- Multiples of $10$: $\\quad 10, 20, 30, {\\color{blue}40}, \\ldots$\n\nThe least common multiple of $8$ and $10$ is ${\\color{blue}{40}}$. Therefore, Mathias and Bill wash their cars on the same day every $40$ days." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142537 1069 "e-13460" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142537 1071 "Determine the least common multiple of $3$, $6$, and $8$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142537 1072 #uuid "d3bfca22-96bd-5f03-97c9-b33318abaa76" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142537 1074 "First, we list the multiples of each number. Then, we find the smallest number that appears in all three lists.\n\n- Multiples of $3$: $\\quad 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 18, 21, {\\color{blue}24}, 27 \\ldots$\n- Multiples of $6$: $\\quad 6, 12, 18, {\\color{blue}24}, 30 \\ldots$\n- Multiples of $8$: $\\quad 8, 16, {\\color{blue}24}, 32 \\ldots$\n\nWe conclude that the least common multiple of $3$, $6$, and $8$ is ${\\color{blue}{24}}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142538 1069 "e-13489" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142538 1071 "![](images/6d/6dac86fc5f131a9ec7de63ad906f308ac85ca86fac8a758f02caa2dfd2d92970.png)\n\nUse the model above to find the value of $\\dfrac{2}{8} + \\dfrac{4}{8}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142538 1072 #uuid "d398e593-50b8-5e7a-bdd8-91b6ee91e9cd" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142538 1074 "The shape to the left of the addition sign shows\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{\\color{blue}8}\n$$\n\nand the shape to the right shows\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{\\color{blue}8}\n$$\n\nCombined, both shapes give us\n\n$$\n2+4 = \\color{red}6\n$$\n\nshaded parts in total.\n\n![](images/af/afc51311e07819aef6c7a74969b912af496addcf2623da3f9bcce273cf728dd4.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{\\color{blue}8} + \\dfrac{4}{\\color{blue}8} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}6}{\\color{blue}8}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{8} =\\dfrac{6\\div 2}{8\\div 2} = \\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{8} + \\dfrac{4}{8} = \\dfrac34\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142539 1069 "e-13490" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142539 1071 "![](images/85/85211d00e6c439a042efa75e00ca7f51ce43b042e3f75e0d75d154aca6cf55a7.png)\n\nUse the model above to find the value of $\\dfrac{6}{7} - \\dfrac{4}{7}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142539 1072 #uuid "96f51882-dbd4-5019-b23f-bc10c06d6588" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142539 1074 "The shape to the left of the minus sign shows\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{\\color{blue}7}\n$$\n\nand the shape to the right shows\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{\\color{blue}7}\n$$\n\nWe subtract the number of shaded parts on the right from the number of the shaded parts on the left:\n\n$$\n6 - 4 = {\\color{red}2}\n$$\n\n![](images/45/453f9ca7b65272b9a55dc21d70bab967c90537737f740a80b529f8b49e6944d7.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{\\color{blue}7} - \\dfrac{4}{\\color{blue}7} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}7}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142540 1069 "e-13491" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142540 1071 "![](images/98/982f3cec69f0c061dd4c66c4c7f534731bd5cc1b81f843ee68c33cc3e53aae93.png)\n\nUse the number line above to find the value of $\\dfrac{7}{9} - \\dfrac{5}{9}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142540 1072 #uuid "a39f5ce4-cfbc-5257-9d72-62d7e2d63b07" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142540 1074 "The line segment between $0$ and $1$ is split into $9$ equal parts. So, each part gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{9}\n$$\n\nof a whole.\n\nTo find\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}\\dfrac{7}{9}} - \\dfrac{\\color{blue}5}{9}\n$$\n\nwe start at the point\n\n$$\n\\color{red}\\dfrac{7}{9}\n$$\n\nand move $\\color{blue}5$ steps to the left:\n\n![](images/74/74b92d76c29c9aca39c6695c5aa924465beeb3b91ba2ef7938a74f7b33f2eb04.png)\n\nWe have landed on\n\n$$\n\\dfrac29\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{9}-\\dfrac{5}{9} = \\dfrac{2}{9}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142541 1069 "e-13492" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142541 1071 "The length of a cross-country skiing course is $12$ kilometers. How long is the course in meters?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142541 1072 #uuid "73e88063-4501-5a9e-9b90-0fe20f69fae0" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142541 1074 "We need to convert $12$ kilometers into meters.\n\nWe start with the unit conversion between kilometers and meters:\n\n$$\n1\\, \\textrm{kilometer} = 1,000 \\, \\textrm{meters}\n$$\n\nThe left-hand side reads $1$ kilometer, and we want to determine how many meters there are in ${\\color{blue}{12}}$ kilometers. So, we multiply *both* sides of the above equation by ${\\color{blue}{12}}$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12 \\cdot 1kilometers &= 12 \\cdot 1, 000meters \\\\\n12kilometers &= 12, 000meters\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12$ kilometers equals $12,000$ meters." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142542 1069 "e-13494" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142542 1071 "How many centimeters are in $46$ meters?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142542 1072 #uuid "8c0c698c-589d-5c6f-bd6b-90f56e4df8c1" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142542 1074 "We start with the unit conversion between meters and centimeters:\n\n$$\n1\\, \\textrm{meter} =100\\, \\textrm{centimeters}\n$$\n\nThe left-hand side reads $1$ meter, and we want to determine how many centimeters there are in $46$ meters. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $46$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n46 \\cdot 1meter &= 46 \\cdot 100centimeters \\\\\n46meters &= 4, 600centimeters\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $46$ meters equals $4,600$ centimeters." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142543 1069 "e-13497" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142543 1071 "Express $78\\,\\textrm{cm}$ in millimeters." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142543 1072 #uuid "8e17d9f3-dff4-5b66-9ea9-08e614f2e80e" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142543 1074 "We need to convert $78$ centimeters into millimeters.\n\nWe start with the unit conversion between centimeters and millimeters:\n\n$$\n1 \\,\\textrm{cm} = 10 \\,\\textrm{mm}\n$$\n\nThe left-hand side reads $1$ centimeter, and we want to determine how many millimeters there are in $78$ centimeters. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $78$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n78 \\cdot 1cm &= 78 \\cdot 10mm \\\\\n78cm &= 780mm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $78\\,\\textrm{cm}$ equals $780\\,\\textrm{mm}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142544 1069 "e-13530" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142544 1071 "From left to right, find the missing numbers in the area model below.\n\n![](images/35/35e1a751c7f8705449b511075d0bef0aca9ad54bacdbefd6c3ee86523dc62724.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142544 1072 #uuid "cda9ca7f-247f-5137-9890-c000987e7ddf" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142544 1074 "We need to look at the blue and orange rectangles:\n\n- Let's first look at the blue rectangle (the one on the left-hand side). Its length is $200$, and its width is $2$. Therefore, the area of this rectangle must be\n$200 \\times 2 = {\\color{blue}400}$.\nLet's add this to our model.![](images/1c/1ce276727a86841151eb6c77d91c6c9d4512517d8ac9d11b20b4c96427734b3b.png)\n- Now, let's look at the orange rectangle (the one on the right-hand side). Its length is $3$, and its width is $2$. Therefore, the area of this rectangle must be\n$3 \\times 2 = {\\color{blue}6}$.\nLet's add this to our model:![](images/8f/8feb298fc4cf45374339477e7942430b1adcd89550875c9847b04c8617edd49a.png)\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the missing numbers are $400$ and $6$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142545 1069 "e-13533" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142545 1071 "![](images/89/893587d9ce1a7c4d4ddb2b267be76b87a1944bd29f4cbaf0c74e3ef3adca488b.png)\n\nThe area model above can be used to represent the following multiplication problem:\n$00$\n\nWhat is the missing number?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142545 1072 #uuid "ad34f5db-1b57-5557-b5db-dd33c0711d8b" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142545 1074 "Let's look at the big rectangle:\n\n- its length is $200 + 30 + 7 = 237$\n- its width is $6$\n- its area is $1,200 + 180 + 42 = 1,422$\n\nHence, this area model can be used to represent the following multiplication problem:\n$1, 422$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $1,422$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142546 1069 "e-13536" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142546 1071 "Using the area model below, find the value of $223 \\times 5$.\n\n![](images/e5/e5467a13b372a97782ad0563edc51613b4644befa104591c7f10f6ce1ea108da.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142546 1072 #uuid "98f71b8c-32a8-53ac-95c4-8f4d0f5a3b05" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142546 1074 "First, we compute the areas of the blue, pink, and orange rectangles. This gives the following picture:\n\n![](images/2f/2fc8ecb3c6ecbdcb633ffc7f8e85d16d39575cffdf536c22b70d017e60a3ed8f.png)\n\nNow, notice the following regarding the big rectangle:\n\n- its length is $200 + 20 + 3 = 223$\n- its width is $5$\n- its area is $1,000 + 100 + 15 = 1,115$\n\nTherefore, this area model can be used to represent the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n223 \\times 5 = 1,115\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142547 1069 "e-13540" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142547 1071 "Using the area model below, find the value of $754 \\times 3$.\n\n![](images/d3/d3f2cbee6b777cf215a9ab7a27c0925a4b9cfbdb81aa37465a77a081b996382c.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142547 1072 #uuid "1bbe19e1-59e0-5e0a-a4d3-3600f30f7a86" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142547 1074 "First, we set up the model. The number $754$ can be expressed in expanded form as ${\\color{blue}700} + {\\color{blue}50} + {\\color{blue}4}$. So, we place ${\\color{blue}700}$, ${\\color{blue}50}$, and ${\\color{blue}4}$ above the left, center, and right rectangles, respectively, and ${\\color{red}3}$ to the left of the big rectangle.\n\n![](images/eb/eb87c800d11e1d07c017d93be8c7165e5e913d1b6a964d5d45b4695f583374b7.png)\n\nNext, we compute the areas of the blue, pink, and orange rectangles. This gives the following picture:\n\n![](images/2f/2fcc5b95584ea2feffb0e647146f938d0c535e896794a88cfa67154cf95b1e39.png)\n\nNow, notice the following regarding the big rectangle:\n\n- its length is $700 + 50 + 4 = 754$\n- its width is $3$\n- its area is $2,100+150 + 12 = 2,262$\n\nTherefore, this area model can be used to represent the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n754 \\times 3 = 2,262\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142548 1069 "e-13541" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142548 1071 "What fraction of the shape is shaded?\n\n![](images/7b/7be754aee230bbbb39a172165e9fda99267b850b824dd844b3fda07b934961db.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142548 1072 #uuid "8c2f50cc-e8b0-545e-94a4-d20b47572617" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142548 1074 "There are $\\color{blue}5$ parts of the shape in total, and $\\color{red}4$ parts are shaded.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}4}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nof the shape is shaded." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142549 1069 "e-13543" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142549 1071 "Which of the following shapes represents the fraction $\\dfrac{3}{5}$?\n\n![](images/d2/d2efebd6e48922a60e5bcc3eed89c74e4344b838555ec4fdee65c4f134362187.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142549 1072 #uuid "69d4fbaa-6647-58e8-8e73-c595358a618c" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142549 1074 "Notice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nmeans we must have $\\color{blue}5$ parts in total, and only $\\color{red}3$ of them can be shaded.\n\nTherefore, the correct shape is the following:\n\n![](images/b5/b5d03c3ff68d86e9536e250d79614ad666691296d306c7c7e2fca84aec80ae53.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142550 1069 "e-13548" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142550 1071 "What is $\\dfrac{8}{32}$ written in lowest terms?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142550 1072 #uuid "d90fb571-7135-5bb4-a893-3fa50f7141c0" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142550 1074 "We start by finding the greatest common factor of $8$ and $32$:\n\n- Factors of $8:\\quad 1, 2, 4, {\\color{blue}{8}}$\n- Factors of $32:\\quad 1, 2, 4, {\\color{blue}{8}}, 16, 32$\n\nSo, the greatest common factor of $8$ and $32$ is ${\\color{blue}{8}}$.\n\nDividing both the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{blue}{8}}$ gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{32} = \\dfrac{8 \\div {\\color{blue}{8}}}{32 \\div {\\color{blue}{8}}}=\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{32}\n$$\n\nexpressed in lowest terms is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142551 1069 "e-13549" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142551 1071 "What is $\\dfrac{35}{63}$ written in lowest terms?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142551 1072 #uuid "9b263b61-efdf-5de0-b907-1076a8340152" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142551 1074 "We start by finding the greatest common factor of $35$ and $63$:\n\n- Factors of $35:\\quad 1, 5, {\\color{blue}{7}}, 35$\n- Factors of $63:\\quad 1, 3, {\\color{blue}{7}}, 9, 21, 63$\n\nSo, the greatest common factor of $35$ and $63$ is ${\\color{blue}{7}}$.\n\nDividing both the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{blue}{7}}$ gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{35}{63} = \\dfrac{35 \\div {\\color{blue}{7}}}{63 \\div {\\color{blue}{7}}} = \\dfrac{5}{9}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{35}{63}\n$$\n\nexpressed in lowest terms is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac59\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142552 1069 "e-13566" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142552 1071 "Determine the least common denominator of $\\dfrac{5}{8}$ and $\\dfrac{1}{4}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142552 1072 #uuid "a2cc410a-9e70-5cda-951b-338bf98c7e0b" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142552 1074 "The denominators of the given fractions are $8$ and $4$. To find the least common denominator, we find the least common multiple of $8$ and $4$.\n\nLet's list the multiples of each number.\n\n- Multiples of $8$: $\\quad {\\color{blue}8}, 16, 24, \\ldots$\n- Multiples of $4$: $\\quad 4, {\\color{blue}8}, 12, \\ldots$\n\nWe conclude that the least common multiple of $8$ and $4$ is ${\\color{blue}{8}}$.\n\nTherefore, the least common denominator of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{8}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nis $8$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142553 1069 "e-13568" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142553 1071 "What is the least common denominator of $\\dfrac{2}{9}$ and $\\dfrac{3}{4}$?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142553 1072 #uuid "bcfb57f3-0e32-5118-b045-fa76a2bf824f" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142553 1074 "The denominators of the given fractions are $9$ and $4$. To find the least common denominator, we find the least common multiple of $9$ and $4$.\n\nLet's list the multiples of each number.\n\n- Multiples of $9$: $\\quad 9, 18, 27, {\\color{blue}36}, \\ldots$\n- Multiples of $4$: $\\quad 4, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24, 28, 32, {\\color{blue}36}, \\ldots$\n\nWe conclude that the least common multiple of $9$ and $4$ is ${\\color{blue}{36}}$.\n\nTherefore, the least common denominator of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{9}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nis $36$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142554 1069 "e-13570" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142554 1071 "Kyle wishes to find the value of $8\\times 62$. He proceeds as follows:\n\n$8 \\cdot 60|= [math]\\phantom{000}[/math]; 8 \\cdot 2|= [math]\\phantom{00}[/math]$\n\nFind the missing numbers, and use your answers to find the value of $8\\times 62$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142554 1072 #uuid "e45ace09-1e70-5814-9bb4-e3ad4453b3fd" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142554 1074 "Notice that $62$ in expanded form is\n\n$$\n62 = 60+2\n$$\n\nSo, we can compute $8\\times 62$ by filling in the missing numbers and adding the results.\n\nFirst, let's find the missing values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8 \\cdot 60 &= [math]\\,480\\,[/math] \\\\\n8 \\cdot 2 &= [math]\\,16\\,[/math]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now add these results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ + & 1 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 9 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n8 \\times 62 = 496\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142555 1069 "e-13571" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142555 1071 "Determine the least common denominator of $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ and $\\dfrac{11}{12}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142555 1072 #uuid "a4ee8076-2424-53ab-bc9e-68c5c6fb3f21" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142555 1074 "The denominators of the given fractions are $8$ and $12$. To find the least common denominator, we find the least common multiple of $8$ and $12$.\n\nLet's list the multiples of each number.\n\n- Multiples of $8$: $\\quad 8, 16, {\\color{blue}24}, \\ldots$\n- Multiples of $12$: $\\quad 12, {\\color{blue}24}, 36, \\ldots$\n\nWe conclude that the least common multiple of $8$ and $12$ is ${\\color{blue}{24}}$.\n\nTherefore, the least common denominator of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{11}{12}\n$$\n\nis $24$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142556 1069 "e-13573" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142556 1071 "What is the value of $59 \\times 5$?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142556 1072 #uuid "30a1d2a0-8297-5217-8b65-b9c0d46a4252" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142556 1074 "First, let's write the two-digit number $(59)$ in expanded form:\n\n$$\n59 = {\\color{blue}{50}} + {\\color{blue}{9}}\n$$\n\nWe now multiply both numbers in our expanded form by ${\\color{red}{5}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n50 \\cdot 5 &= 250 \\\\\n9 \\cdot 5 &= 45\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now add these results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ + & 4 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 9 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n59 \\times 5 = 295\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142557 1069 "e-13574" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142557 1071 "Find the least common denominator of $\\dfrac{9}{16}$ and $\\dfrac{3}{24}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142557 1072 #uuid "8a4a16f0-062d-561d-a6e7-71be7fe05e5d" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142557 1074 "The denominators of the given fractions are $16$ and $24$. To find the least common denominator, we find the least common multiple of $16$ and $24$.\n\nLet's list the multiples of each number.\n\n- Multiples of $16$: $\\quad 16, 32, {\\color{blue}48}, \\ldots$\n- Multiples of $24$: $\\quad 24, {\\color{blue}48}, 72, \\ldots$\n\nWe conclude that the least common multiple of $16$ and $24$ is ${\\color{blue}{48}}$.\n\nTherefore, the least common denominator of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{16}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{24}\n$$\n\nis $48$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142558 1069 "e-13575" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142558 1071 "Ann wishes to find the value of $36\\times 19$. She starts by writing both factors in expanded form.\n\n$36 = {\\color{blue}{30}}+{\\color{blue}{6}},\\qquad 19 = {\\color{red}{10}}+{\\color{red}{9}}$\n\nThen, she proceeds as follows:\n\n$30 \\cdot 10|= [math]\\phantom{000}[/math]; 30 \\cdot 9|= [math]\\phantom{000}[/math]; 6 \\cdot 10|= [math]\\phantom{10}[/math]; 6 \\cdot 9|= [math]\\phantom{10}[/math]$\n\nFind the missing numbers, and use your answers to find the value of $36\\times 19$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142558 1072 #uuid "5043117a-68e9-5275-88a3-73a869d8bcc4" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142558 1074 "We can compute $36\\times 19$ by filling in the missing numbers and adding the results. So, let's find the missing values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n30 \\cdot 10 &= [math]\\,300\\,[/math] \\\\\n30 \\cdot 9 &= [math]\\,270\\,[/math] \\\\\n6 \\cdot 10 &= [math]\\,60\\,[/math] \\\\\n6 \\cdot 9 &= [math]\\,54\\,[/math]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now add these results:\n\n$$\n1; 3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ + & 2\\end{vmatrix}7\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}6\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}5\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ 6\\end{vmatrix}8 \\mid 4\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n36\\times 19 = 684\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142559 1069 "e-13577" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142559 1071 "A hospital has $5$ floors. On each floor, there are $96$ beds. How many beds does the hospital have in total?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142559 1072 #uuid "7c2b12f4-9287-5b9a-af99-d89c60ea8581" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142559 1074 "To determine the total number of beds in the hospital, we need to compute the value of\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{5}} \\times 96\n$$\n\nFirst, let's write the two-digit number $(96)$ in expanded form:\n\n$$\n96 = {\\color{blue}{90}} + {\\color{blue}{6}}\n$$\n\nWe now multiply both numbers in our expanded form by ${\\color{red}{5}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5 \\cdot 90 &= 450 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 6 &= 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now add these results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ + & 3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are $480$ beds in the hospital." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142560 1069 "e-13579" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142560 1071 "What is the value of $83 \\times 47$?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142560 1072 #uuid "36c8e3b0-976e-5af2-978e-0941f3b6bfe6" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142560 1074 "First, let's write our factors in expanded form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n83 &= 80 + 3, 47 = 40 + 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply *both* numbers in the first factor's expanded form by *both* numbers in the second factor's expanded form:\n\n- First, we multiply ${\\color{blue}{80}}$ by ${\\color{red}{40}}$ and ${\\color{red}{7}}$:\n$80 \\cdot 40|= 3, 200; 80 \\cdot 7|= 560$\n- Then, we multiply ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ by ${\\color{red}{40}}$ and ${\\color{red}{7}}$:\n$3 \\cdot 40|= 120; 3 \\cdot 7|= 21$\n\nLet's now add all our results:\n\n$$\n1; 3\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ + & 5 & 6\\end{vmatrix}0; +\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ + & 2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 9 & 0\\end{vmatrix}1\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n83 \\times 47 = 3,901\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142561 1069 "e-13581" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142561 1071 "A maximum of $68$ people can enter a particular gym every hour. What is the maximum number of people that can enter the gym over a $36$-hour period?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142561 1072 #uuid "554141c1-563f-5681-86a4-b3708a32a691" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142561 1074 "To determine the maximum number of people, we need to compute the value of\n\n$$\n68 \\times 36\n$$\n\nFirst, let's write our factors in expanded form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n68 &= 60 + 8, 36 = 30 + 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply *both* numbers in the first factor's expanded form by *both* numbers in the second factor's expanded form:\n\n- First, we multiply ${\\color{blue}{60}}$ by ${\\color{red}{30}}$ and ${\\color{red}{6}}$:\n$60 \\cdot 30|= 1, 800; 60 \\cdot 6|= 360$\n- Then, we multiply ${\\color{blue}{8}}$ by ${\\color{red}{30}}$ and ${\\color{red}{6}}$:\n$8 \\cdot 30|= 240; 8 \\cdot 6|= 48$\n\nLet's now add all our results:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}8\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ + & 3\\end{vmatrix}6\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}2\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ + & 4\\end{vmatrix}8; 2\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 4\\end{vmatrix}8\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum number of people that can enter the gym is $2,448$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142562 1069 "e-13592" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142562 1071 "Create a fraction equivalent to $\\dfrac{4}{3}$ with a denominator of $9$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142562 1072 #uuid "1f9d8816-bc93-576c-be53-b0518601ea51" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142562 1074 "To make an equivalent fraction with a denominator of $9$, we multiply the numerator and the denominator of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nby $3$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{3} = \\dfrac{4 \\times 3}{3 \\times 3}=\\dfrac{12}{9}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{3}=\\dfrac{12}{9}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142563 1069 "e-13594" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142563 1071 "Create a fraction equivalent to $\\dfrac{12}{8}$ with a denominator of $2$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142563 1072 #uuid "eb258e19-47bd-56fa-bc4b-0377ebeefb8b" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142563 1074 "To make a denominator of $2$, we divide the numerator and the denominator of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{12}{8}\n$$\n\nby $4$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{12}{8} = \\dfrac{12 \\div 4}{8 \\div 4} =\\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{12}{8} = \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142564 1069 "e-13595" 13194139533362 1