o our model:![](images/f8/f8d62c953300acf23e9e41f39d3fee017b8730fbc7404c297752d3a5b2067546.png)\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the missing numbers are $70$ and $6$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142572 1069 "e-13615" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142572 1071 "![](images/e0/e025b5e7fc2d5149128d068fbd3a9756c2ddf6ff5db64da3a7e45aa76a12775a.png)\n\nUsing the area model above, find the value of $76\\div 4$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142572 1072 #uuid "bd17ef6a-fdf6-5ac3-bb0b-0550273b1f28" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142572 1074 "First, we find the missing values. This gives the following:\n\n![](images/6b/6b6f90eaa80ceafd74bdc63d5799e7fe9dd4e35951e38d687e63b17e674b856a.png)\n\nNow, notice the following regarding the big rectangle:\n\n- its length is $10+9 = 19$\n- its width is $4$\n- its area $40+36 = 76$\n\nTherefore, this area model can be used to represent the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n76 = 19\\times 4\n$$\n\nWriting this as a division problem, we get\n\n$$\n76\\div 4 = 19\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142573 1069 "e-13616" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142573 1071 "![](images/25/252256ca391d2c709bc9707d5f58eba0271b3d53325799cb5f62183e306c2d1d.png)\n\nThe area model above can be used to represent the following division problem\n\n$00$\n\nWhat is the missing number?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142573 1072 #uuid "21f728ad-bedd-598b-a363-86aaaae42275" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142573 1074 "Let's look at the big rectangle:\n\n- its length is $40+7=47$\n- its width is $9$\n- its area is $360 + 63 = 423$\n\nTherefore, this area model can be used to represent the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n423 = 47 \\times 9\n$$\n\nWriting this as a division problem, we get\n\n$$\n423 \\div 9 = 47\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $423$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142574 1069 "e-13617" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142574 1071 "Leonard packed $812$ apples into bags. One bag holds $4$ apples. Using the area model below, find how many bags of apples Leonard packed.\n\n![](images/f8/f8d71f1a520d7c251ff60dd0120fb760d44f24f28b8557bb624452d42e534f85.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142574 1072 #uuid "c3e60866-a94f-5ce5-be15-593672f13d92" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142574 1074 "To determine the number of bags Leonard packed, we need to calculate $812\\div 4$.\n\n- Let's first look at the blue rectangle (the one on the left-hand side). Its area is $800$, and its width is $4$. Hence, the length of this rectangle must be\n$800 \\div 4$.\nTo carry out this division, we write $800$ as a product with $100$ as one of the factors and then swap the order of multiplication and division:\n$800 \\div 4|=; 8 \\cdot 100 \\div 4|=; 8 \\div 4 \\cdot 100|=; (8 \\div 4) \\cdot 100|=; 2 \\cdot 100|=; 200$\nLet's add this to our model:![](images/a5/a5cd3dd2fa5b35356cd8bb0853569adbb18a91903474cd14014a11bd2d0cbfd5.png)\n- Now, let's look at the pink rectangle (the one on the right-hand side). Its area is $12$, and its width is $4$. Hence, the length of this rectangle must be\n$12 \\div 4 = 3$.\nLet's add this to our model:![](images/f9/f9c0f5b07b1889ad10a560b2658b3491cb0ac399a42f89bccaeac83e86fb2bb6.png)\n\nNow, notice the following regarding the big rectangle:\n\n- its length is $200+3=203$\n- its width is $4$\n- its area is $800+12=812$\n\nTherefore, this area model can be used to represent the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n812 = 203 \\times 4\n$$\n\nWriting this as a division problem, we get\n\n$$\n812 \\div 4 = 203\n$$\n\nTherefore, Leonard packed $203$ bags of apples." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142575 1069 "e-13623" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142575 1071 "What is the value of $10 \\,\\dfrac{2}{9} - 9 \\, \\dfrac{4}{9}$?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142575 1072 #uuid "970b6a11-6754-58f4-bf7f-9d1e51eeed86" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142575 1074 "When subtracting the fractional parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{9} - \\dfrac{4}{9}\n$$\n\nHowever, we *cannot* perform this subtraction because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac49\n$$\n\nis larger than\n\n$$\n\\dfrac29\n$$\n\nTo get around this problem, we write each mixed number as an improper fraction.\n\n- Writing $10\\,\\dfrac 2 9$ as an improper fraction, we have\n$\\dfrac{(10\\times 9)+2}{9} = \\dfrac{92}{9}$.\n- Writing $9 \\, \\dfrac{4}{9}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n$\\dfrac{(9\\times 9) + 4}{9} = \\dfrac {85} 9$.\n\nWe can now carry out the subtraction in the usual way:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{92}{9} - \\dfrac {85} 9= \\dfrac{92-85}{9} = \\dfrac{7}{9}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142576 1069 "e-13625" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142576 1071 "Find the value of $7 \\,\\dfrac{2}{6} - 6 \\, \\dfrac{4}{6}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142576 1072 #uuid "dcd5d845-64dd-5548-afaf-af3b3961c4d1" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142576 1074 "When subtracting the fractional parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{6} - \\dfrac{4}{6}\n$$\n\nHowever, we *cannot* perform this subtraction because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{6}\n$$\n\nis larger than\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{6}\n$$\n\nTo get around this problem, we write each mixed number as an improper fraction.\n\n- Writing $7\\,\\dfrac{2}{6}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n$\\dfrac{(7\\times 6)+2}{6} = \\dfrac{44}{6}$.\n- Writing $6 \\, \\dfrac{4}{6}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n$\\dfrac{(6\\times 6) + 4}{6} = \\dfrac{40}{6}$.\n\nWe can now carry out the subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{44}{6} - \\dfrac {40}{6}= \\dfrac{44-40}{6} = \\dfrac{4}{6}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can simplify\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{6}\n$$\n\nby dividing the numerator and denominator by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{6} = \\dfrac{4\\div 2}{6\\div 2} = \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n7 \\,\\dfrac{2}{6} - 6 \\, \\dfrac{4}{6} = \\dfrac23\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142577 1069 "e-13627" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142577 1071 "What is the value of $7 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4} - 2 \\, \\dfrac{3}{4}$?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142577 1072 #uuid "471a935b-ae79-5ef8-aeac-568ef5f4d387" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142577 1074 "When subtracting the fractional parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} - \\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nHowever, we *cannot* perform this subtraction because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac34\n$$\n\nis larger than\n\n$$\n\\dfrac14\n$$\n\nTo get around this problem, we write each mixed number as an improper fraction.\n\n- Writing $7\\,\\dfrac 1 4$ as an improper fraction, we have\n$\\dfrac{(4\\times 7)+1}{4} = \\dfrac{29}{4}$.\n- Writing $2 \\, \\dfrac{3}{4}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n$\\dfrac{(2\\times 4) + 3}{4} = \\dfrac {11} 4$.\n\nWe can now carry out the subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{29}{4} - \\dfrac {11} 4 = \\dfrac{29-11}{4} = \\dfrac{18}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{18}{4}\n$$\n\nby dividing the numerator and denominator by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{18}{4} = \\dfrac{18\\div 2}{4\\div 2} = \\dfrac{9}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert back to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n9\\div 2 = 4\\,\\textrm{R}1\n$$\n\nSo, our final answer is\n\n$$\n4\\,\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142578 1069 "e-13630" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142578 1071 "Express $59\\div 4$ as a fraction." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142578 1072 #uuid "e4a51d82-84f8-59f0-b939-782b0ba4084d" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142578 1074 "A division problem may be interpreted as a fraction, where the dividend is the numerator, and the divisor is the denominator.\n\nTherefore, we can represent the given division problem as\n\n$$\n59\\div 4=\\dfrac{59}{4}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142579 1069 "e-13631" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142579 1071 "What is $20\\div 8$ as an improper fraction?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142579 1072 #uuid "3cd1f7ee-6a3f-57b1-837b-d5d96cf9296f" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142579 1074 "First, we can represent the given division problem as a fraction:\n\n$$\n20\\div 8 = \\dfrac{20}{8}\n$$\n\nThen, we can simplify the fraction by dividing both the numerator and denominator by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{20\\div 4}{8\\div 4} = \\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142580 1069 "e-13633" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142580 1071 "Express $\\dfrac{8}{19}$ as an equivalent division problem:" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142580 1072 #uuid "30e150f1-6d1b-5407-b1fd-5f37cd023b1f" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142580 1074 "A fraction may be interpreted as the division of the numerator by the denominator.\n\nTherefore, we can represent the given fraction as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{19}=8\\div 19\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142581 1069 "e-13638" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142581 1071 "From left to right, find the missing numbers in the area model below.\n\n![](images/a1/a1506e5c8f4fce256673a3bb26ad5e6a6d0f0ee5afdf4ce3e925baea221b41e5.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142581 1072 #uuid "20f24b51-3e1c-5ddd-b5f4-dcab21fef94a" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142581 1074 "We need to look at the blue and pink rectangles:\n\n- Let's first look at the blue rectangle on the top left. Its length is $80$, and its width is $40$. Therefore, the area of this rectangle must be\n$80 \\times 40 = {\\color{blue}3,200}$.\nLet's add this to our model:![](images/b1/b147d782ad23b4fa960d7f8714a61930feef309a7c2263071c131ce82048e71c.png)\n- Now, let's look at the pink rectangle (the one on the top right). Its length is $2$, and its width is $40$. Therefore, the area of this rectangle must be\n$2 \\times 40 = {\\color{blue}80}$.\nLet's add this to our model:![](images/a1/a14b266976c94b43c0fda4d0e66d098c5f05a5920301066f4a92ee0894930f3c.png)\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the missing numbers are $3,200$ and $80$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142582 1069 "e-13639" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142582 1071 "![](images/9d/9d6855a5e1df1d572888f804632b629a99af7a030a9437c4cccb3c99a766f076.png)\n\nThe area model above can be used to represent the following multiplication problem:\n\n$00$\n\nWhat is the missing number?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142582 1072 #uuid "b14e7e8b-579b-544f-8ffc-0b82bb63002f" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142582 1074 "Let's look at the big rectangle:\n\n- its length is $30+9=39$\n- its width is $20+3=23$\n- its area is $600 + 180 + 90 + 27 = 897$\n\nHence, this area model can be used to represent the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n39 \\times 23= \\fbox{897}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $897$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142583 1069 "e-13642" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142583 1071 "Using the area model below, find the value of $32 \\times 45$.\n\n![](images/da/dab2684b90e4d265d1d657cb1bfcf03be0421763e9f52320088822bc7cba0d12.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142583 1072 #uuid "69cf8369-2fd7-5fd6-956d-54a23fc07883" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142583 1074 "First, we compute the missing values. This gives the following picture:\n\n![](images/b0/b06caf2fec2f8013339564bfd255d59466ff816c4ccc6e089958ec2d95f612a1.png)\n\nNow, notice the following regarding the big rectangle:\n\n- its length is $30+2=32$\n- its width is $40+5=45$\n- its area is $1,200 + 80 + 150 + 10 = 1,440$\n\nTherefore, this area model can be used to represent the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n32 \\times 45 = 1,440\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142584 1069 "e-13643" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142584 1071 "Maria bought her cell phone $9$ weeks ago. How many days have passed since she purchased her cell phone?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142584 1072 #uuid "1898399e-b865-5524-bd1c-a24c5dc98b37" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142584 1074 "We need to convert $9$ weeks into days.\n\nWe start with the unit conversion between weeks and days:\n\n$$\n1 \\,\\textrm{week} = 7 \\,\\textrm{days}\n$$\n\nWe want to figure out how many days are in $9$ weeks. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $9{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9 \\cdot 1week &= 9 \\cdot 7days \\\\\n9weeks &= 63days\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $9$ weeks equals $63$ days.\n\nSo, Maria bought her cell phone $63$ days ago." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142585 1069 "e-13645" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142585 1071 "$9$ days is equal to how many hours?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142585 1072 #uuid "baf092f4-e733-5099-ab9b-d7e41f0e9e96" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142585 1074 "We start with the unit conversion between days and hours:\n\n$$\n1 \\, \\textrm{day} = 24 \\, \\textrm{hours}\n$$\n\nWe want to figure out how many hours are in $9$ days. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $9{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9 \\cdot 1day &= 9 \\cdot 24hours \\\\\n9days &= 9 \\cdot 24hours\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the value of $9\\times 24$ using an area model:\n\n![](images/74/748a681be93665cfef5cb4dede055ec9ba8c1b56bc6dbf5dad35cccdf8af98ce.png)\n\nAccording to our model,\n\n$$\n9\\times 24 = 180+36 = 216\n$$\n\nTherefore, $9$ days equals $216$ hours." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142586 1069 "e-13647" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142586 1071 "Using the area model below, find the value of $97 \\times 51$.\n\n![](images/16/16d84871e2e14a4c324573e2b9c7740a5d09469e2f64073f54578fef823fe9a9.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142586 1072 #uuid "f8602b9e-6a10-508c-853f-94456cd87605" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142586 1074 "First, we set up the model. The numbers $97$ and $51$ can be expressed in expanded form as ${\\color{blue}90}+{\\color{blue}7}$ and ${\\color{red}50}+{\\color{red}1}$.\nSo, we place ${\\color{blue}90}$ and ${\\color{blue}7}$ above the rectangles and ${\\color{red}50}$ and ${\\color{red}1}$ to the left of the rectangles.\n\n![](images/aa/aa247727581a0521ef339e1278efc896c2e347fe5b37ef4b5abe54a37d73978e.png)\n\nNext, we compute the missing values. This gives the following picture:\n\n![](images/26/2623fbb80d26c88eb1708e8e703cc4ca817b68ca52651de5c86a820db4de2980.png)\n\nNow, notice the following regarding the big rectangle:\n\n- its length is $90+7=97$\n- its width is $50+1=51$\n- its area is $4,500 + 350 + 90 + 7 = 4,947$\n\nTherefore, this area model can be used to represent the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n97 \\times 51 = 4,947\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142587 1069 "e-13648" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142587 1071 "A plane leaves Boston bound for Madrid. The captain estimates that the flight duration will be $7$ hours. How much is this time in minutes?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142587 1072 #uuid "9c5b9574-4a5a-5425-9a0b-eaaba3d30fbd" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142587 1074 "We need to convert $7$ hours into minutes.\n\nWe start with the unit conversion between hours and minutes:\n\n$$\n1 \\, \\textrm{hour} = 60 \\, \\textrm{minutes}\n$$\n\nWe want to figure out how many minutes are in $7$ hours. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $7{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7 \\cdot 1hour &= 7 \\cdot 60minutes \\\\\n7hours &= 420minutes\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $7$ hours equals $420$ minutes.\n\nSo, the estimated flight duration is $420$ minutes." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142588 1069 "e-13649" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142588 1071 "Express $9 \\, \\textrm{min}$ in seconds." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142588 1072 #uuid "1eb85a2a-ce28-5a8d-b40f-3a392bd3e5b0" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142588 1074 "We start with the unit conversion between minutes and seconds:\n\n$$\n1 \\, \\textrm{min} = 60 \\, \\textrm{s}\n$$\n\nWe want to figure out how many seconds are in $9$ minutes. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $9{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9 \\cdot 1min &= 9 \\cdot 60s \\\\\n9min &= 540s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $9 \\, \\textrm{min}$ is equal to $540 \\, \\textrm{s}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142589 1069 "e-13654" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142589 1071 "The rule for the pattern below is \"one circle, one square, one circle, two squares,...,\" where the number of squares increases by $1$ each time. What is the missing shape?\n\n![](images/47/47a381825619ec49e2329be0b9f7991cd52137b46ca59b0a1cdf5303c8e2a5f6.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142589 1072 #uuid "6be78249-e661-5cb4-959d-cd60d8452263" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142589 1074 "The rule is \"one circle, one square, one circle, two squares,...,\" where the number of squares increases by $1$ each time.\n\nFirst, we have a circle followed by ${\\color{blue}1}$ square.\n\n![](images/2e/2e60e629a165b1ea58913fdc66b94da21bc7af2ad9a1e2e6ad63d35f5acddb5c.png)\n\nThen, a circle followed by\n\n$$\n1+1={\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\nsquares.\n\n![](images/fe/fe20a7fc2bbc7fb2b26aa438d69ffdeb44699b1f84ed145c6790a88b92dce8de.png)\n\nSo next, we expect a circle followed by\n\n$$\n2 + 1 = {\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nsquares.\n\n![](images/15/15daf7f21c68c5d605c6774e59d3132086eb04c348bea8110bcdd5658b8c23d8.png)\n\nTherefore, the missing shape is a square." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142590 1069 "e-13656" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142590 1071 "The rule for the pattern below is \"multiply by $2$.\"\n$0$\n\nWhat is the missing number?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142590 1072 #uuid "0a7bd8ed-9801-5ceb-b527-0fbeaf3e6380" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142590 1074 "The rule is \"multiply by $2$\" and the missing number comes after $6$. Therefore, the missing number is\n\n$$\n6 \\times 2 = 12\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142591 1069 "e-13657" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142591 1071 "The rule for the pattern below is \"divide by $2$.\" What are the missing numbers?\n$0$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142591 1072 #uuid "d3891301-8d36-59d7-aa6e-e670a3e25d26" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142591 1074 "The rule is \"divide by $2$\" and the first missing number comes after $8$. Therefore, the first missing number is\n$8 \\div 2=4$.\n\nThe second missing number comes after $2$. Therefore, the second missing number is\n$2 \\div 2=1$.\n\nSo, we have the following pattern:\n$16,\\qquad 8,\\qquad \\bbox[3pt, lightgray]{4},\\qquad 2,\\qquad \\bbox[3pt, lightgray]{1}$\n\nThe missing numbers are $4$ and $1$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142592 1069 "e-13658" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142592 1071 "The rule for the pattern below is \"subtract $3$.\"\n$53,\\qquad 50,\\qquad 47,\\qquad 44,\\qquad 41$\n\nIf the pattern is continued, what will be the $7$th number?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142592 1072 #uuid "846916f5-de97-5244-b50d-41826884e2f3" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142592 1074 "The rule is \"subtract $3$\", and the last given number is $41$, which is the $5$th number.\n\nTo find the $6$th number, we subtract $3$ from $41$:\n$41 - 3 = 38$\n\nTo find the $7$th number, we subtract $3$ from $38$:\n$38 - 3 = 35$\n\nSo, the $7$th number is $35$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142593 1069 "e-13674" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142593 1071 "Estimate the sum $3.68 + 5.42$ using benchmarks with decimal parts of $0.0$ or $0.5$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142593 1072 #uuid "d9fdb891-9cf1-52f8-9a8f-4f2980adb45b" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142593 1074 "First, we round both numbers to the nearest benchmark:\n$3.68\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 5.42 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 3.5 & +\\end{vmatrix}5.5$\n\nNext, we calculate the sum:\n\n$$\n1; 3\\begin{vmatrix}. & 5 \\\\ + & 5\\end{vmatrix}.\\begin{vmatrix}5 \\\\ 9\\end{vmatrix}. \\mid 0\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3.68 + 5.42 \\approx 9\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142594 1069 "e-13675" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142594 1071 "To make a fruit juice drink, Maria mixes $0.73$ liters of fruit concentrate with $4.18$ liters of water. Using benchmarks with decimal parts of $0.0$ or $0.5$, estimate the total amount of fruit juice that Maria prepared." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142594 1072 #uuid "c30dac92-23c4-533f-a371-57226747b342" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142594 1074 "To estimate the amount of fruit juice that Maria prepared, we have to find an approximation for the sum\n$0.73 + 4.18$.\n\nFirst, we round both numbers to the nearest benchmark:\n$0.73\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 4.18 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 0.5 & +\\end{vmatrix}4.0$\n\nNext, we calculate the sum:\n\n$$\n0\\begin{vmatrix}. & 5 \\\\ + & 4\\end{vmatrix}.\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\\\ 4\\end{vmatrix}. \\mid 5\n$$\n\nTherefore, Maria prepared approximately $4.5$ liters of fruit juice." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142595 1069 "e-13699" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142595 1071 "Calculate the value of $4 \\times 1\\,\\dfrac{2}{7}$, expressing the result as a mixed number." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142595 1072 #uuid "54148f16-56b3-5ead-80fd-279c9b10ad29" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142595 1074 "First, we write $4$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n4 = \\dfrac{4}{1}\n$$\n\nWe also write\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac{2}{7}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac{2}{7} = \\dfrac{(1 \\times 7) + 2}{7} = \\dfrac{9}{7}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{1} \\times \\dfrac{9}{7} = \\dfrac{4 \\times 9}{1 \\times 7} = \\dfrac{36}{7}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{36}{7} = 5\\,\\textrm{R}\\,1 = 5\\,\\dfrac{1}{7}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142596 1069 "e-13701" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142596 1071 "Divide $4 \\div \\dfrac 1 {5}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142596 1072 #uuid "58c82929-55bc-5fcf-b4f1-d685a065efe4" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142596 1074 "Dividing a whole number by a fraction is equivalent to multiplying the whole number by the reciprocal of the fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nis $\\color{blue}5$.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n4\\div \\dfrac1{\\color{blue}5} = 4\\times {\\color{blue}5} =20\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142597 1069 "e-13703" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142597 1071 "Calculate the value of $2\\div \\dfrac67$, expressing the result as a mixed number." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142597 1072 #uuid "906c6d3e-4f1a-58e0-8f21-128bfc754d47" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142597 1074 "Dividing a whole number by a fraction is equivalent to multiplying the whole number by the reciprocal of the fraction.\n\nFirst, we write $2$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2=\\dfrac{2}{1}\n$$\n\nNext, we note that the reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}6}{\\color{red}7}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}7}{\\color{blue}6}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n2\\div \\dfrac 6 {7} = \\dfrac 2 1 \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}6}{\\color{red}7} = \\dfrac 2 1 \\times \\dfrac{\\color{red}7}{\\color{blue}6}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our fractions. Note that we can simplify the process by swapping the denominators first.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2}{1} \\cdot \\frac{7}{6} &= \\frac{2}{6} \\cdot \\frac{7}{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\frac{7}{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 \\cdot 7}{3 \\cdot 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert our result to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac73 = 2\\,\\textrm{R}\\,1 = 2\\,\\dfrac13\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142598 1069 "e-13706" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142598 1071 "Divide $8\\div \\dfrac 2 5$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142598 1072 #uuid "42efe817-2f21-57a9-9cfe-5973ac89f1f2" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142598 1074 "Dividing a whole number by a fraction is equivalent to multiplying the whole number by the reciprocal of the fraction.\n\nFirst, we write $8$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n8=\\dfrac{8}{1}\n$$\n\nNext, we note that the reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}5}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n8\\div \\dfrac 2 5 = \\dfrac {8} 1 \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}5} = \\dfrac {8} 1 \\times \\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our fractions. Note that we can simplify the process by swapping the denominators first.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{8}{1} \\cdot \\frac{5}{2} &= \\frac{8}{2} \\cdot \\frac{5}{1} \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142599 1069 "e-13708" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142599 1071 "Calculate the value of $4\\div \\dfrac35$, expressing the result as a mixed number." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142599 1072 #uuid "e86212dc-fc03-58a2-9a37-758e85a18073" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142599 1074 "Dividing a whole number by a fraction is equivalent to multiplying the whole number by the reciprocal of the fraction.\n\nFirst, we write $4$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n4=\\dfrac{4}{1}\n$$\n\nNext, we note that the reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}5}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n4\\div \\dfrac 3 5 = \\dfrac 4 1 \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}5} = \\dfrac 4 1 \\times \\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our fractions.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 4 {1} \\times \\dfrac{5}{3} = \\dfrac {4\\times 5} {1\\times 3} = \\dfrac{20}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{20}{3} =6\\,\\textrm{R} 2 = 6\\,\\dfrac 2 {3}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142600 1069 "e-13713" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142600 1071 "Which of the following fractions are written in lowest terms?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{11}{14}$\n2. $\\dfrac{10}{15}$\n3. $\\dfrac{15}{28}$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142600 1072 #uuid "3406a1b3-d5e1-5940-abc8-8eda915d3dd4" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142600 1074 "A fraction is written in lowest terms if the greatest common factor of the numerator and denominator equals $1$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our fractions.\n\n- The fraction $\\dfrac{11}{14}$ is written in lowest terms since the greatest common factor of $11$ and $14$ is $1$.\n- The fraction $\\dfrac{10}{15}$ is **not** written in lowest terms. Notice that the greatest common factor of $10$ and $15$ is $5$. So, we can divide both the numerator and denominator by $5$.\n$\\dfrac{10}{15} = \\dfrac{10\\div 5}{15\\div 5} = \\dfrac{2}{3}$\n- The fraction $\\dfrac{15}{28}$ is written in lowest terms since the greatest common factor of $15$ and $28$ is $1$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142601 1069 "e-13714" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142601 1071 "Calculate $12 + 30 \\div (6 + 9)$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142601 1072 #uuid "d2914b4d-bbb5-5433-b5bb-312caff3e2c0" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142601 1074 "To evaluate the given expression, we follow the order of operations with parentheses.\n\nFirst, we perform the operation inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12 + 30 \\div (6 + 9) &=  \\\\\n&= 12 + 30 \\div 15 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply and divide, from left to right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12 + 30 \\div 15 &=  \\\\\n&= 12 + 2 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add and subtract, from left to right:\n\n$$\n12 + 2 =14\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142602 1069 "e-13716" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142602 1071 "Evaluate $[10 - (1 + 3)] \\div 2$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142602 1072 #uuid "947ef370-3cdf-5566-b573-b0c74d2b1424" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142602 1074 "To evaluate the given expression, we follow the order of operations with parentheses.\n\nFirst, we perform the operation inside the inner parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[10 - (1 + 3)] \\div 2 &=  \\\\\n&= [10 - 4] \\div 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we perform the operation inside the square brackets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[10 - 4] \\div 2 &=  \\\\\n&= 6 \\div 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 \\div 2 &=  \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142603 1069 "e-13718" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142603 1071 "In the expression below, which part of the expression should be calculated first?\n\n$\\{[6 + (3 \\times 4)] \\div 9 \\} \\times 7$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142603 1072 #uuid "aa7f2efe-034e-54a9-85db-b01636f3b788" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142603 1074 "To evaluate the given expression, we must follow the order of operations with parentheses. Therefore, we should start with the operation that's inside the most inner parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\{[6 + ({\\color{blue}{3 \\times 4}})] \\div 9 \\} \\times 7\n$$\n\nSo we should calculate ${\\color{blue}{3 \\times 4}}$ first." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142604 1069 "e-13720" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142604 1071 "Evaluate $\\{[(4 \\times 2) - 5] + 3\\} \\div 3$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142604 1072 #uuid "d00f55c4-4a8f-5a1c-953b-007910f69203" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142604 1074 "To evaluate the given expression, we follow the order of operations with parentheses.\n\nFirst, we perform the operation inside the inner parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{[(4 \\cdot 2) - 5] + 3} \\div 3 &= {[(4 \\cdot 2) - 5] + 3} \\div 3 \\\\\n&= {[8 - 5] + 3} \\div 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we perform the operation inside the square brackets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{[8 - 5] + 3} \\div 3 &= {[8 - 5] + 3} \\div 3 \\\\\n&= {3 + 3} \\div 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we perform the operation inside the braces:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{3 + 3} \\div 3 &= {3 + 3} \\div 3 \\\\\n&= 6 \\div 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 \\div 3 &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142605 1069 "e-13731" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142605 1071 "What is the reciprocal of $\\dfrac{2}{7}$?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142605 1072 #uuid "0028c4d7-e672-5dd7-b951-3966be4266a1" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142605 1074 "To find the reciprocal of a fraction, we switch the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}7} \\quad\\rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}7}{\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\nSo the reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{7}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{2}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142606 1069 "e-13734" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142606 1071 "What is the reciprocal of $\\dfrac{3}{14}$ as a mixed number?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142606 1072 #uuid "bf0771a2-9eaa-5ad9-911e-18f529edcd35" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142606 1074 "To find the reciprocal of a fraction, we switch the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}14} \\quad\\rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}14}{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nNow, we convert the resulting fraction to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n14\\div 3 = 4\\,\\textrm{R}2=4\\,\\dfrac 2 3\n$$\n\nSo the reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{14}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n4\\,\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142607 1069 "e-13736" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142607 1071 "What is the reciprocal of $9$?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142607 1072 #uuid "769b40a0-d0c9-58c1-b317-a80cb4b6a4bb" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142607 1074 "First, we write $9$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n9= \\dfrac{9} 1\n$$\n\nTo find the reciprocal of a fraction, we switch the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}9}{\\color{red}1} \\quad\\rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}9}\n$$\n\nSo the reciprocal of $9$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{9}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142608 1069 "e-13737" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142608 1071 "What is the reciprocal of $2\\,\\dfrac{2}{5}$?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142608 1072 #uuid "9fc449fc-a8f9-5e70-b670-b28eab0ef4c8" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142608 1074 "First, we convert the given mixed number to an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac{2}{5} = \\dfrac{(2\\times 5)+2}{5}=\\dfrac{12}{5}\n$$\n\nTo find the reciprocal of a fraction, we switch the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}12}{\\color{red}5} \\quad\\rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{\\color{blue}12}\n$$\n\nSo the reciprocal of\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{12}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142609 1069 "e-13764" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142609 1071 "Using the benchmarks $0$ and $\\dfrac 1 2$, estimate the value of $\\dfrac{7}{13} + \\dfrac{1}{9}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142609 1072 #uuid "a2330ffb-51d8-5c11-b32b-78034b5b0e78" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142609 1074 "We round each fraction to its closest benchmark. Then, we add the benchmarks.\n\n- The fraction $\\dfrac{7}{13}$ is close to one-half because the numerator $(7)$ is approximately one-half of the denominator $(13)$. So, we round as follows:\n$\\dfrac{7}{13}\\rightarrow {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{1}{2}}}$\n- The fraction $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ is close to zero because the numerator $(1)$ is much smaller than the denominator $(9)$. So, we round as follows:\n$\\dfrac{1}{9}\\rightarrow {\\color{blue}{0}}$\n\nNext, we add the benchmarks:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{1}{2}}} + {\\color{blue}{0}} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{13} + \\dfrac{1}{9}\\approx \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nRemember that the symbol\n\n$$\n\\approx\n$$\n\nmeans \"is approximately equal to.\"" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142610 1069 "e-13766" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142610 1071 "Using the benchmarks $0, \\dfrac{1}{2}$, and $1$, estimate the value of $\\dfrac{7}{8} + \\dfrac{1}{15}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142610 1072 #uuid "6c1ecbce-af49-5ad7-b664-6eca000e6377" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142610 1074 "We round each fraction to its closest benchmark. Then, we add the benchmarks.\n\n- The fraction $\\dfrac{7}{8}$ is close to one because the numerator $(7)$ is close to the denominator $(8)$. So, we round as follows:\n$\\dfrac{7}{8}\\rightarrow {\\color{blue}{1}}$\n- The fraction $\\dfrac{1}{15}$ is close to zero because the numerator $(1)$ is much smaller than the denominator $(15)$. So, we round as follows:\n$\\dfrac{1}{15}\\rightarrow {\\color{blue}{0}}$\n\nNext, we add the benchmarks:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{1}} + {\\color{blue}{0}} = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{8} + \\dfrac{1}{15} \\approx \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}\n$$\n\nRemember that the symbol\n\n$$\n\\approx\n$$\n\nmeans \"is approximately equal to.\"" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142611 1069 "e-13768" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142611 1071 "Using the benchmarks $0,\\, \\dfrac{1}{2}$, and $1$, estimate the value of $\\dfrac{7}{12} - \\dfrac{9}{16}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142611 1072 #uuid "ba26c53b-184b-5edf-89c8-002607c3b040" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142611 1074 "We round each fraction to its closest benchmark. Then, we subtract the benchmarks.\n\n- The fraction $\\dfrac{7}{12}$ is close to one-half because the numerator $(7)$ is approximately one-half of the denominator $(12)$. So, we round as follows:\n$\\dfrac{7}{12}\\rightarrow {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{1}{2}}}$\n\n- The fraction $\\dfrac{9}{16}$ is close to one-half because the numerator $(9)$ is approximately one-half of the denominator $(16)$. So, we round as follows:\n$\\dfrac{9}{16}\\rightarrow {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{1}{2}}}$\n\nNext, we subtract the benchmarks:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{\\dfrac12}} - {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac12}} = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{12} - \\dfrac{9}{16} \\approx 0\n$$\n\nRemember that the symbol\n\n$$\n\\approx\n$$\n\nmeans \"is approximately equal to.\"" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142612 1069 "e-13780" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142612 1071 "Find the value of $\\dfrac{1}{4} \\div \\dfrac{1}{8}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142612 1072 #uuid "7e77df17-125b-5a13-9429-dc1fdc15cb26" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142612 1074 "Dividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}1}{\\color{red}8}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}8}{\\color{blue}1}\n$$\n\nSo, we have:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 4 \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}1}{\\color{red}8} = \\dfrac 1 4 \\times \\dfrac{\\color{red}8}{\\color{blue}1}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {4} \\times \\dfrac{8}{1} = \\dfrac {1\\times 8} {4\\times 1} = \\dfrac{8}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n$8\\div 4=2$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\div \\dfrac{1}{8} = 2 \\,\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142613 1069 "e-13781" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142613 1071 "What is the missing digit in the following equality?\n$0$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142613 1072 #uuid "ff77c815-b89c-5157-9c1c-cf52bddda0fb" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142613 1074 "Dividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}4}{\\color{red}3}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nSo, we have:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 6 {7} \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}4}{\\color{red}3} = \\dfrac 6 {7} \\times \\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 6 {7} \\times \\dfrac{3}{4} = \\dfrac {6\\times 3} {7\\times 4} = \\dfrac{18}{28}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{18}{28} = \\dfrac{18\\div 2}{28\\div 2} = \\dfrac{\\color{blue}9}{14}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 6 {7}\\div \\dfrac 4 {3} = \\dfrac{\\color{blue}9}{14} \\,\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the missing number is ${\\color{blue}{9}}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142614 1069 "e-13784" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142614 1071 "Calculate the value of $\\dfrac{1}{12} \\div \\dfrac{2}{3}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142614 1072 #uuid "4597d0ec-e04c-581a-ad9f-e78280182444" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142614 1074 "Dividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}3}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {12} \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}3} = \\dfrac 1 {12} \\times \\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {12} \\times \\dfrac{3}{2} = \\dfrac {1\\times 3} {12\\times 2} = \\dfrac{3}{24}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{24} = \\dfrac{3\\div 3}{24\\div 3} = \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{12} \\div \\dfrac{2}{3} = \\dfrac{1}{8} \\,\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142615 1069 "e-13786" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142615 1071 "Calculate the value of $\\dfrac{1}{3} \\div \\dfrac 2 {13}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142615 1072 #uuid "d361a96a-7abd-50a3-801d-9eb26f0c119c" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142615 1074 "Dividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}13}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}13}{\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {3} \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}13} = \\dfrac 1 {3} \\times \\dfrac{\\color{red}13}{\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {3} \\times \\dfrac{13}{2} = \\dfrac {1\\times 13} {3 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{13}{6}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{13}{6} =2\\,\\textrm{R} 1 = 2\\,\\dfrac 1 {6}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3} \\div \\dfrac 2 {13} = 2\\,\\dfrac 1 {6} \\,\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142616 1069 "e-13787" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142616 1071 "![](images/91/916d8a6c0c4ba6c249191c0ee0d85d20f3e14ebfbabbdb0a6a5be583e39be139.png)\n\nUse the model above to find the value of $\\dfrac{5}{8} \\div \\dfrac{3}{8}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142616 1072 #uuid "ae470aa4-327d-5b21-9e11-217710f1ef94" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142616 1074 "![](images/ea/ea208841c3276de2e8736add1ad5c6f4c343a9ec8d137f74f10e47b9243e87eb.png)\n\nWe can break down our division problem as follows:\n\n- Computing $\\dfrac{{\\color{red}5}}{8}\\div\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{8}$ means we want to determine how many times $\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{8}$ fits into $\\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{8}$.\n- Since both fractions have a *common denominator*, this is the *same* as determining how many times ${\\color{blue}3}$ fits into ${\\color{red}5}$.\n- Therefore, instead of $\\dfrac{{\\color{red}5}}{8}\\div\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{8}$, we can simply compute ${\\color{red}5}\\div {\\color{blue}3}$.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}5} \\div {\\color{blue}3} = \\dfrac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{8} \\div \\dfrac{3}{8} = \\dfrac{5}{3}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142617 1069 "e-13790" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142617 1071 "![](images/c5/c5e7367d6b3a53b513c2921d91579b1bd6588b850622ecf1c7b370695227a64d.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing number in the equation below.\n$6$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142617 1072 #uuid "0b51ee70-1d44-5427-8b9f-3930dc3a1962" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142617 1074 "The denominators are $5$ and $10$. We can make a common denominator of $10$ by splitting the shape on the left into $2$ parts vertically:\n\n![](images/3d/3d4839662bcac31bc197436b533da61e07ecf1ca301b3fa7613d433833e14028.png)\n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}6}{10} \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}7}{10}\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}6} \\div {\\color{blue}7} = \\dfrac{6}{7}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n6\n$$\n\nHence, the missing number is $6$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142618 1069 "e-13792" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142618 1071 "![](images/87/87ce51fed33e907d6acb7e213bec596ea97b16e739bb96b5446975dc6a593011.png)\n\nCalculate $\\dfrac{2}{3} \\div \\dfrac{3}{4}$ using the model above." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142618 1072 #uuid "daa61f81-4882-5af9-b86a-8b03a586e93b" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142618 1074 "The denominators are $4$ and $3$. We can make a common denominator of\n\n$$\n4\\times 3 = 12\n$$\n\nWe split the shape on the left of the division sign into $4$ parts vertically, and we split the shape on the right into $3$ parts horizontally:\n\n![](images/93/931f2220f4f5b4e8d3760bcf283d19ef565d2bd8910db6d95cd44e604782a0f0.png)\n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}8}{12} \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}9}{12}\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}8} \\div {\\color{blue}9} = \\dfrac{8}{9}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3} \\div \\dfrac{3}{4} = \\dfrac{8}{9} \\,\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142619 1069 "e-13793" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142619 1071 "![](images/93/93a73e4b7458251fdb96891e2b00af4fd5ecf6a442aed9fb7c289ca25ae0e389.png)\n\nUse the model above to find the value of $\\dfrac{11}{14} \\div \\dfrac{3}{7}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142619 1072 #uuid "066a9329-d63e-55d4-bc6b-bcdb5dd96cc3" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142619 1074 "The denominators are $7$ and $14$. We can make a common denominator of $14$.\n\nWe split the shape on the right of the division sign into $2$ parts horizontally:\n\n![](images/a9/a9867f3e62605ccbaad6fdc6a1925173a460f287e36b8cc563ab62bb3074a13c.png)\n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}11}{14} \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}6}{14}\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}11} \\div {\\color{blue}6} = 1\\,\\dfrac56\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{11}{14} \\div \\dfrac{3}{7} = 1\\,\\dfrac56 \\,\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142620 1069 "e-13797" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142620 1071 "![](images/5e/5eceab3e6842dfcdd27cf27aa46c624421fb9ede1d81cbf56d11f437be73f2e2.png)\n\nUse the fraction model above to find the value of $\\dfrac{1}{2}+\\dfrac{1}{4}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142620 1072 #uuid "be58d862-5a54-5e9b-83ef-c3b4db360aa9" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142620 1074 "We split the shape on the left into $4$ equal parts by drawing a horizontal line:\n\n![](images/44/44b4bf16288236f6d3bca18aaafa1767a988daf2cb55adbf342f221a04ab7254.png)\n\nNow that they have the same number of parts, we can add them:\n\n![](images/95/9587bb711324df8de8480a39c5698e572dd5f08ba8e0fa3d49b522b6e5ac50b2.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} + \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}4} = \\dfrac{2}{\\color{blue}4} + \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}4} = \\dfrac{3}{\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we made a common denominator of ${\\color{blue}{4}}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142621 1069 "e-13798" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142621 1071 "![](images/64/64689878e653ed169eb476f63899a97d4864a19d3018f4a9ac6c324621d9bf06.png)\n\nUse the model above to find the value of $\\dfrac{3}{4} - \\dfrac{4}{8}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142621 1072 #uuid "9bc033d8-daca-574b-b754-e300969bb37b" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142621 1074 "The denominators are $4$ and $8$. Since $8$ is a multiple of $4$, we can make a common denominator of $\\color{blue}8$.\n\nWe split the shape on the left of the minus sign into $2$ parts horizontally:\n\n![](images/a4/a4c84973f2f6d43ddcce8eab1b7a99a7bac13d18be961bb3138669a81355db49.png)\n\nNow, the shape to the left shows\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{\\color{blue}8}\n$$\n\nand the shape to the right shows\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{\\color{blue}8}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract the number of shaded parts on the right from the number on the left.\n\n$$\n6 - 4 = {\\color{red}{2}}\n$$\n\nThis gives the picture shown below.\n\n![](images/b3/b3bbacf875cade28d52160ac13355973a03f780c69b4fa7cce949b7944aed78a.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4} - \\dfrac{4}{8} = \\dfrac{6}{\\color{blue}8} - \\dfrac{4}{\\color{blue}8} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}8}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142622 1069 "e-13800" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142622 1071 "Use the model below to find the value of $\\dfrac{2}{9} + \\dfrac{2}{3}$.\n\n![](images/59/5916e76fa8dc00bb104b3008b7ffdabb85e349e98cfb13572e5683350f2462a3.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142622 1072 #uuid "5579fc27-53a3-5a36-8e98-577044239ede" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142622 1074 "The denominators are $9$ and $3$. Since $9$ is a multiple of $3$, we can make a common denominator of $\\color{blue}9$.\n\nWe split the shape on the right into $9$ parts by drawing two vertical lines:\n\n![](images/98/988d58bc1b3044b8471faf82be0e1cc6c9c1690bc86671cfadafa7418e899fd6.png)\n\nNow that both shapes have a common denominator of $\\color{blue}9$, we can add them:\n\n![](images/96/96207a70afcf58504cbff0acb9085bc0b7c5ed4832c52f9ce63faa3c533a4be7.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{9} + \\dfrac{2}{3} = \\dfrac{2}{\\color{blue}9} + \\dfrac{6}{\\color{blue}9} = \\dfrac{8}{\\color{blue}9}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142623 1069 "e-13802" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142623 1071 "![](images/06/06844e14b374bde0d3f2bb9ca654f2d651f1f9e360f463edf08d3f1b15622c59.png)\n\nUse the model above to find the value of $\\dfrac{3}{4} - \\dfrac{1}{12}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142623 1072 #uuid "21609521-71b8-5d6f-91b6-cdca3835da16" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142623 1074 "The denominators are $4$ and $12$. Since $12$ is a multiple of $4$, we can make a common denominator of $\\color{blue}12$.\n\nWe split the shape on the left of the minus sign into $3$ parts vertically.\n\n![](images/74/7482d068f30ed6d9e028eb0225013b61dc37fcbfcc481f11f070e03abc15fd8e.png)\n\nNow, the shape to the left shows\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{\\color{blue}12}\n$$\n\nand the shape to the right shows\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}12}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract the number of shaded parts on the right from the number on the left.\n\n$$\n9 - 1 = {\\color{red}{8}}\n$$\n\nThis gives the picture shown below.\n\n![](images/28/28a004bf5d830a1256551c7a2061742a588b00eabfff4890657e57cafe73936f.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4} - \\dfrac{1}{12} = \\dfrac{9}{\\color{blue}12} - \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}12} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}8}{\\color{blue}12}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142624 1069 "e-13807" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142624 1071 "![](images/2d/2d50e6ad9932567b034e4c0a89ee92f0d68f473208793de0fa5005c149446a7a.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the number that is missing in the statement below.\n$0$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142624 1072 #uuid "5e70ead0-c215-5471-9572-e8ba727f8fe2" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142624 1074 "The denominators are $5$ and $6$. So we can make a common denominator of\n\n$$\n5\\times 6 = \\color{blue}30\n$$\n\nWe split the shape on the left of the addition sign into $6$ parts vertically, and we split the shape on the right into $5$ parts horizontally:\n\n![](images/51/51cb48be18ed4f7ee98df8b7756c2dfc9c71a377dcac3acd61138ac5576237cb.png)\n\nNow, the shape to the left shows\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{\\color{blue}30}\n$$\n\nand the shape to the right shows\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{\\color{blue}30}\n$$\n\nWe add the number of shaded parts and get:\n\n$$\n6 + 5 = {\\color{red}11}\n$$\n\n![](images/32/322f8aec541b503f590e9ba35390460666752ea113e1f6d07f1fd7334432d931.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n$11$\n\nSo, the missing number is $\\color{red}11$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142625 1069 "e-13810" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142625 1071 "![](images/24/24137db0f5cb2d1eb57f9bf2d880097cf8cba471bf2eaa23e81016cab6d7b712.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the number that is missing in the statement below.\n$0$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142625 1072 #uuid "5be0f91b-8956-5f0e-a828-2ed9711b69bc" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142625 1074 "The denominators are $2$ and $3$. So we can make a common denominator of\n\n$$\n2\\times 3 = \\color{blue}6\n$$\n\nWe split the shape on the left of the minus sign into $3$ parts horizontally, and we split the shape on the right into $2$ parts vertically:\n\n![](images/0c/0cf8a37179ec5f2e1326f24329b2f2e179e4b89e463eb1504121946e7740217f.png)\n\nNow, the shape to the left shows\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{\\color{blue}6}\n$$\n\nand the shape to the right shows\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{\\color{blue}6}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract the number of shaded parts on the right from the number on the left.\n\n$$\n3-2 = {\\color{red}{1}}\n$$\n\nThis gives the picture shown below.\n\n![](images/95/95b6b863165b8d1c407f48cf70d91c5e64e75e19c783ed070cb60deed98cdda4.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n$1$\n\nSo, the missing number is $\\color{red}1$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142626 1069 "e-13826" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142626 1071 "What is the missing digit in the following equality?\n\n$0$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142626 1072 #uuid "68ab3d23-d9c7-5037-9ff5-1803bee39a02" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142626 1074 "First, we write\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 1 {2}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 1 {2}=\\dfrac{(2\\times 1)+1}{2}= \\dfrac {3} {2}\n$$\n\nWe also write\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 1 {3}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 1 {3}=\\dfrac{(3\\times 2)+1}{3}= \\dfrac {7} {3}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers.\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2} \\cdot \\frac{7}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this product by swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{2} \\cdot \\frac{7}{3} &= \\frac{3}{3} \\cdot \\frac{7}{2} \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot \\frac{7}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is ${\\color{blue}{7}}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142627 1069 "e-13829" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142627 1071 "What is the value of $1\\,\\dfrac 1 {2} \\times 1\\,\\dfrac 1 {3}$?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142627 1072 #uuid "1130bb19-8742-53c5-b07c-22c310f1d927" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142627 1074 "First, we write\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 1 {2}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 1 {2}=\\dfrac{(2\\times 1)+1}{2}= \\dfrac {3} {2}\n$$\n\nWe also write\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 1{3}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 1 {3}=\\dfrac{(1\\times 3)+1}{3}= \\dfrac {4}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers.\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2} \\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this product by swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{2} \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} &= \\frac{3}{3} \\cdot \\frac{4}{2} \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 1 {2} \\times 1\\,\\dfrac 1 {3} = 2\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142628 1069 "e-13831" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142628 1071 "What is the missing digit in the following equality?\n\n$0$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142628 1072 #uuid "294b83f4-2e38-5bf4-a521-01fe8ca13fe5" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142628 1074 "First, we write\n\n$$\n3 \\, \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3 \\,\\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{(3 \\times 2) + 1}{2}= \\dfrac{7}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{2} \\times \\dfrac{1}{4} = \\dfrac {7 \\times 1}{2 \\times 4} = \\dfrac{\\color{blue}7}{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is ${\\color{blue}{7}}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142629 1069 "e-13834" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142629 1071 "What is $3 \\, \\dfrac 1 {2} \\times 1 \\,\\dfrac 2 {5}$ expressed as a mixed number?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142629 1072 #uuid "b93f9bde-7033-529a-a8fa-63066c95d48e" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142629 1074 "First, we write\n\n$$\n3\\,\\dfrac 1{2}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3\\,\\dfrac 1 {2}=\\dfrac{(3\\times 2)+1}{2}= \\dfrac{7}{2}\n$$\n\nWe also write\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 2 {5}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 2 {5}=\\dfrac{(1\\times 5)+2}{5}= \\dfrac{7}{5}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 7 {2} \\times \\dfrac{7}{5} = \\dfrac {7\\times 7} {2\\times 5} = \\dfrac{49}{10}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{49}{10} =4\\,\\textrm{R} 9 = 4\\,\\dfrac 9 {10}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142630 1069 "e-13835" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142630 1071 "Find the value of $\\dfrac{1}{3} \\times 2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{2}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142630 1072 #uuid "a688fc95-6831-57c6-9fee-f3295412af05" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142630 1074 "First, we write\n\n$$\n2 \\,\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2 \\,\\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{(2 \\times 2) + 1}{2} = \\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3} \\times \\dfrac{5}{2} = \\dfrac{1 \\times 5}{3 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{5}{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3} \\times 2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{5}{6}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142631 1069 "e-13838" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142631 1071 "Express $2\\,\\dfrac 1 {3} \\times 2\\,\\dfrac 1 {4}$ as a mixed number in its lowest terms." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142631 1072 #uuid "1dcc8e5a-21ab-52af-b9ef-fccb97d74eb9" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142631 1074 "First, we write\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 1 {3}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 1 {3}=\\dfrac{(3\\times 2)+1}{3}= \\dfrac {7} {3}\n$$\n\nWe also write\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 1{4}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 1 {4}=\\dfrac{(2\\times 4)+1}{4}= \\dfrac {9}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers.\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{3} \\cdot \\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this product by swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{7}{3} \\cdot \\frac{9}{4} &= \\frac{7}{4} \\cdot \\frac{9}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{4} \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= \\frac{21}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{21}{4} = 5\\,\\textrm{R} 1= 5\\,\\dfrac 1 {4}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142632 1069 "e-13839" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142632 1071 "Determine the missing digit in the following equality.\n\n$0$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142632 1072 #uuid "689fd963-ba93-5b0a-b7e4-0b8fd1dc9101" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142632 1074 "First, we write\n\n$$\n3 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3 \\,\\dfrac{2}{3} = \\dfrac{(3 \\times 3) + 2}{3}= \\dfrac{11}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{11}{3} \\times \\dfrac{2}{5} = \\dfrac{11 \\times 2}{3 \\times 5} = \\dfrac{22}{15}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{22}{15} = 1 \\, \\textrm{R} 7 = 1 \\, \\dfrac{\\color{blue}7}{15}\n$$\n\nHence, the missing number is ${\\color{blue}{7}}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142633 1069 "e-13843" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142633 1071 "Multiply $1 \\, \\dfrac{7}{8} \\times \\dfrac{2}{3}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142633 1072 #uuid "db3701cf-af70-508a-a158-5e38ff68246f" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142633 1074 "First, we write\n\n$$\n1 \\,\\dfrac{7}{8}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1 \\,\\dfrac{7}{8} = \\dfrac{(1 \\times 8) + 7}{8} = \\dfrac{15}{8}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers.\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{8} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this product by swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{15}{8} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} &= \\frac{15}{3} \\cdot \\frac{2}{8} \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{4} = 1 \\, \\textrm{R} 1 = 1 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142634 1069 "e-13856" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142634 1071 "Evaluate $6+8-12$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142634 1072 #uuid "8848f5c1-767c-5cd3-815d-89a120e4eb03" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142634 1074 "To evaluate the given expression, we follow the order of operations.\n\nIn this case, we add and subtract from left to right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 + 8 - 12 &=  \\\\\n14 - 12 &=  \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142635 1069 "e-13858" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142635 1071 "Calculate $12 \\div 6 \\times 4$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142635 1072 #uuid "f1553aa8-81d4-5e53-8307-c64264d8a3ab" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142635 1074 "To calculate the given expression, we follow the order of operations.\n\nIn this case, we multiply and divide from left to right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12 \\div 6 \\cdot 4 &=  \\\\\n12 \\div 6 \\cdot 4 &=  \\\\\n2 \\cdot 4 &=  \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142636 1069 "e-13862" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142636 1071 "Evaluate $3 + 2 \\times 4$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142636 1072 #uuid "dc443bef-be76-5fba-bd54-6d0f678264b5" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142636 1074 "First, we multiply and divide, from left to right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 + 2 \\cdot 4 &=  \\\\\n3 + 2 \\cdot 4 &=  \\\\\n&= 3 + 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we add:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 + 8 &= 11\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142637 1069 "e-13864" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142637 1071 "What is the dividend in the division problem below?\n$20 \\div 5 = 4$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142637 1072 #uuid "8bcbc9fb-65b7-5a48-bdc3-0ef26adb3c33" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142637 1074 "![](images/cc/ccfc781ff2808834a1e725a0ebd95414c007a84ceecb2f3f3d709b7081b71674.png)\n\nIn a division problem,\n\n- the number we divide is called the *dividend*,\n- the number we divide by is called the *divisor*,\n- the result of the division is called the *quotient*.\n\nIn our example above, the dividend is ${\\color{blue}{20}}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142638 1069 "e-13865" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142638 1071 "Calculate $4 \\times 5 + 6 \\div 3$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142638 1072 #uuid "ad541341-5c04-5fce-a672-96655ce937ae" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142638 1074 "To evaluate the given expression, we follow the order of operations.\n\nFirst, we multiply and divide, from left to right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4 \\cdot 5 + 6 \\div 3 &=  \\\\\n4 \\cdot 5 + 6 \\div 3 &=  \\\\\n20 + 6 \\div 3 &=  \\\\\n20 + 6 \\div 3 &=  \\\\\n&= 20 + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we add:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n20 + 2 &= 22\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142639 1069 "e-13866" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142639 1071 "What is the divisor in the division problem below?\n$12 \\div 4 = 3$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142639 1072 #uuid "5bc83f0d-8166-532b-b2cc-f6a2d0fc1fca" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142639 1074 "![](images/bf/bf956aa35b9e56e63b9f5a26565898cb349f79052de2087d45b43b3e49eec17f.png)\n\nIn a division problem,\n\n- the number we divide is called the *dividend*,\n- the number we divide by is called the *divisor*,\n- the result of the division is called the *quotient*.\n\nIn our example above, the divisor is ${\\color{magenta}{4}}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142640 1069 "e-13869" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142640 1071 "What is the quotient in the division problem below?\n$18 \\div 2 = 9$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142640 1072 #uuid "0a2215d6-4e17-58fc-a42c-7f8b629c06f8" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142640 1074 "![](images/06/0670487d2d3458fa45fdea5f43744e9b27c6b3930f06780da3c1888cb0969a6a.png)\n\nIn a division problem,\n\n- the number we divide is called the *dividend*,\n- the number we divide by is called the *divisor*,\n- the result of the division is called the *quotient*.\n\nIn our example above, the quotient is ${\\color{red}{9}}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142641 1069 "e-13870" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142641 1071 "What is the remainder in the division problem below?\n$32 \\div 5 = 6 \\: \\textrm{R} \\: 2$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142641 1072 #uuid "136ca449-38dc-560d-a786-574e69804b3e" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142641 1074 "![](images/73/7384f6c79c158758e15214194df2ed4a7b85af0be8603379bcfc333eea172260.png)\n\nIn a division with remainder problem,\n\n- the number we divide is called the *dividend*,\n- the number we divide by is called the *divisor*,\n- the whole number part of the result is called the *quotient*,\n- the amount left over is called the *remainder*.\n\nIn our example above, the remainder is $2$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142642 1069 "e-14197" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142642 1071 "![](images/45/45d4e4ca4f1cdfddcb624dccf32cb053818a2fd70cbb7b5a02d1ff30f9784e2b.png)\n\nThe model above represents $3$ wholes. Use the model to determine the missing number in the following division problem.\n$0$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142642 1072 #uuid "23532ee6-f05b-5ac7-b722-84891afd3bc5" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142642 1074 "We proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we split each whole into fifths.\n\n![](images/9c/9c10c283125b23a553855e247149fba126c1977bf62a487b09121eb539dabfbf.png)\n\n- Next, we create groups of ${\\color{red}{\\dfrac3{5}}}$.\n\n![](images/a8/a8ee616adc072552e64182bba65bd74754b4a16857bfc7fe7e411ab9784bd331.png)\n\n- Then, we count the groups.\n\n![](images/ad/ad9c3bcb60e6277ae536b00489b3f4267a31fb9f2d0a7af368ee0cc43215287d.png)\n\nSo, if we divide $\\color{blue}3$ wholes into groups of\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{\\dfrac3{5}}}\n$$\n\nwe get ${\\color{purple}{5}}$ groups in total.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n3 \\div \\dfrac{3}{{5}} = \\fbox{5}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $\\bbox[3pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{purple}5}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142643 1069 "e-14203" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142643 1071 "![](images/0c/0c3f53204a0271ea9816eaddbdbeeb84648638918531ace2f06251af765fe05f.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing numbers in the following division problem.\n$0^{2}$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142643 1072 #uuid "be59814b-3823-57b9-9a2c-f678e1d34183" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142643 1074 "Let's start by interpreting our model:\n\n- The model shows $\\color{blue}2$ wholes. Each whole is split into *thirds*.\n\n![](images/d9/d9c692ed44d98c47b475dc18c2d28a01d0ab1f2f231ed24a9604f14000f599b1.png)\n\n- The thirds are grouped, and each group contains ${\\color{red}{\\dfrac23}}$.\n\n![](images/3c/3c9fd6222f68deffa02ce69a2f31dca220ce2285f71dbd8255d364126ea0d8c8.png)\n\n- There are $\\color{purple} 3$ groups in total.\n\n![](images/22/228c9e5eea2b5696d64d4423ecd927e12a11e6672d614cc30a947549943be0c4.png)\n\nSo, if we divide $\\color{blue}2$ wholes into groups of\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{\\dfrac23}}\n$$\n\nwe get ${\\color{purple}{3}}$ groups in total.\n\nTherefore, the model represents the following division problem:\n$2$\n\nTherefore, the missing numbers are $\\bbox[3pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}$ and $\\bbox[3pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{purple}3}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142644 1069 "e-14426" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142644 1071 "Find the value of $\\dfrac 3 {7} \\times \\dfrac 1 {4}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142644 1072 #uuid "cb9425fb-6e26-5706-ada1-105bd2dbf545" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142644 1074 "To multiply two fractions, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 3 {7} \\times \\dfrac 1 {4} = \\dfrac {3\\times 1} {7\\times 4} = \\dfrac{3}{28}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142645 1069 "e-14430" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142645 1071 "Multiply $\\dfrac 2 {9} \\times \\dfrac 4 {5}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142645 1072 #uuid "895012bb-d418-5720-92c1-771fa03ec8b6" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142645 1074 "To multiply two fractions, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 2 {9} \\times \\dfrac 4 {5} = \\dfrac {2\\times 4} {9\\times 5} = \\dfrac{8}{45}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142646 1069 "e-14431" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142646 1071 "Multiply $\\dfrac 5 {6} \\times \\dfrac 3 {4}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142646 1072 #uuid "c749c71d-16bc-5b50-b013-8c049618b073" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142646 1074 "To multiply two fractions, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 5 {6} \\times \\dfrac 3 {4} = \\dfrac {5\\times 3} {6\\times 4} = \\dfrac{15}{24}\n$$\n\nWe reduce this fraction to its simplest form by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {15} {24} = \\dfrac {15\\div 3} {24\\div 3} = \\dfrac{5}{8}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142647 1069 "e-14494" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142647 1071 "What improper fraction is shown in the picture below?\n\n![](images/1b/1b15a4eb78c0a011cd6ee9466b536f7dbebe3ecbb0da97fe1cb18150b7c16a6c.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142647 1072 #uuid "bef3c294-6473-50cf-b0c7-a4e00a214675" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142647 1074 "Each shape consists of $\\color{blue}4$ parts, and there are\n\n$$\n2\\times {\\color{blue}4} +2 = \\color{red}10\n$$\n\nshaded parts in total.\n\nTherefore, the picture represents the improper fraction\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}10}{\\color{blue}4}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142648 1069 "e-14496" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142648 1071 "Draw a fraction model that represents the improper fraction $\\dfrac{12}{7}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142648 1072 #uuid "a9b79a8d-903d-5480-b6c2-c4f5037f1fd9" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142648 1074 "The improper fraction\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}12}{\\color{blue}7}\n$$\n\nmeans\n\n- a \"whole\" is made up of $\\color{blue}7$ parts, and\n- $\\color{red}12$ parts must be shaded.\n\nTherefore, a correct picture is the following:\n\n![](images/9a/9af97d720fb868713b5757421f0f833a24953d515369fa5908cad1ade43ebe2a.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142649 1069 "e-14505" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142649 1071 "What improper fraction is shown on the number line?\n\n![](images/7d/7d6eba7b1cb3762ab5df20bf81bf4bf4e212e522c79f290f714fb0ef92a33fe0.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142649 1072 #uuid "e0d752d1-a12b-5b31-87ce-87f1eb3ad327" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142649 1074 "Notice that the line segment from $0$ to $1$ is split into $\\color{blue}4$ equal parts. Each part represents\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nof a whole.\n\n![](images/3a/3abe8ae5696deaa9fdfb7ad57a1690bce945acbeaa3ef57eb7aad2789093605b.png)\n\nThe number we are interested in is located\n\n$$\n2\\times {\\color{blue}4} +1 = \\color{red}9\n$$\n\nsteps from $0$ (left to right).\n\n![](images/0c/0cc68fcad034e32bae261faa668ed89cc199bff1bf42df86717fc78d81e053af.png)\n\nTherefore, the picture represents\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}9}{\\color{blue}4}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142650 1069 "e-14518" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142650 1071 "What is the value of $\\dfrac 1 6 \\div 5$?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142650 1072 #uuid "a2af42e6-fa21-5419-bc0b-dbcec4e73c5d" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142650 1074 "Dividing a fraction by a whole number is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of that whole number.\n\nThe reciprocal of $\\color{blue}5$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{{\\color{blue}{5}}}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 6\\, {\\color{red}\\div} \\, {\\color{blue}5} = \\dfrac 1 6\\, {\\color{red}\\times} \\,\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 6 \\times \\dfrac{1}{5} = \\dfrac {1\\times 1} {6\\times 5} = \\dfrac{1}{30}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142651 1069 "e-14519" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142651 1071 "What is the values of $\\dfrac 2 3 \\div 7$?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142651 1072 #uuid "42c81ef5-519f-5d53-9719-92cee05c8e87" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142651 1074 "Dividing a fraction by a whole number is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of that whole number.\n\nThe reciprocal of $\\color{blue}7$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{{\\color{blue}{7}}}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 2 3\\, {\\color{red}\\div} \\, {\\color{blue}7} = \\dfrac 2 3\\, {\\color{red}\\times} \\,\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}7}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 2 3 \\times \\dfrac{1}{7} = \\dfrac {2\\times 1} {3\\times 7} = \\dfrac{2}{21}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142652 1069 "e-14521" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142652 1071 "Find the value of $\\dfrac{6}{11} \\div 3$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142652 1072 #uuid "3f350570-ed4d-5f5b-8435-9f8c2af5b0fb" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142652 1074 "Dividing a fraction by a whole number is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of that whole number.\n\nThe reciprocal of $\\color{blue}3$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{{\\color{blue}{3}}}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {6}{11} \\, {\\color{red}\\div} \\, {\\color{blue}3} = \\dfrac {6}{11}\\, {\\color{red}\\times} \\,\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {6}{11} \\times \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac {6\\times 1} {11\\times 3} = \\dfrac{6}{33}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify our result by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{33} = \\dfrac{6\\div 3}{33\\div 3} = \\dfrac{2}{11}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142653 1069 "e-14537" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142653 1071 "What number is represented by the red dot on the number line below?\n\n![](images/7b/7bb205a466c9e38acd736aa3a5bf2e631cc8a779021cd92e1c851f56c94bf022.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142653 1072 #uuid "8a646314-4953-5b8c-94b3-8b64ea2409a8" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142653 1074 "The number is negative because it is to the *left* of zero.\n\nTo find the number, we count the number of jumps between zero and the red circle.\n\n![](images/4c/4ca11600e191c0fa9db4e26de8910b9526ab5b16c7474966c99946592085780e.png)\n\nThere is $1$ jump between zero and the red circle. So the number shown is $-1$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142654 1069 "e-14540" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142654 1071 "Represent the number $-6$ on a number line." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142654 1072 #uuid "7cf8a44d-7562-5b9d-aba9-45a9c94b5262" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142654 1074 "The number $-6$ is negative, so it is to the *left* of zero.\n\nTo plot the number $-6$, we start at zero and move $6$ places to the left.\n\n![](images/37/37a6f9e24c564217d192aaaee4471c5e4ef90a3864ba961279d91574990cf44d.png)\n\nSo the red dot on the number line below represents the number $-6$:\n\n![](images/ff/ff369c4973393d5f77da957e192d91d4b864e29e86db7dfa5689733bd98d37d2.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142655 1069 "e-14542" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142655 1071 "What number is represented by the red dot on the number line below?\n\n![](images/30/30379c84e08d1cfb0d91e6250a0af120b6b9cc779e75a99c4fcee1e4a2ffc95c.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142655 1072 #uuid "0a1c526a-589d-5bce-aa4c-05237347bdbe" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142655 1074 "The number is negative because it is to the *left* of zero.\n\nTo find the number, we count the number of jumps between zero and the red circle.\n\n![](images/e1/e108939884c8cc53f14bdd4aa1851910083885723af4543d8680de59b2fc45e8.png)\n\nThere are $2$ jumps between zero and the red circle, and each jump represents a distance of $3$. So the number shown is $-6$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142656 1069 "e-14543" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142656 1071 "From left to right, what are the missing numbers in the number line below?\n\n![](images/12/12ea070f2bb17822a139c8e812aafbcd23d1347af8bf67b10d657761fc6e5858.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142656 1072 #uuid "40f52dd9-b944-5ec7-bc15-9054e0e2ad8a" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142656 1074 "The first number is negative because it is to the *left* of zero. To find this number, we count the number of jumps between zero and the number.\n\n![](images/59/598df90ac77225ca3444de50ebf627aa78691c941d473670f5067ab830a55d3e.png)\n\nEach jump represents a distance of $5$, and we have $3$ jumps. Therefore, this number $\\color{blue}{-15}$.\n\nThe second number is also negative because it is to the *left* of zero. To find this number, we count the number of jumps between zero and the number.\n\n![](images/f6/f6f49f20c0620efb134e183d7c44947024469465a3502ef42adae9bf163bc0b0.png)\n\nEach jump represents a distance of $5$, and we have $1$ jump. Therefore, this number $\\color{red}{-5}$.\n\nTherefore, the numbers are $-15$ and $-5$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142657 1069 "e-14549" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142657 1071 "![](images/2a/2a7a8a236b9adb92b2e233777095c4e076013b304bc241211815a6590e8ede2f.png)\n\nThe number $4.82$ is shown on the number line above. Using benchmarks with decimal parts of $0.0$ and $0.5$, round $4.82$ to the nearest benchmark." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142657 1072 #uuid "38edca38-04e2-54f5-b36a-99135dba8202" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142657 1074 "Rounding to the nearest benchmark means finding the closest number with a decimal part of $0.0$ or $0.5$ to our number.\n\nThe number $\\color{blue}4.82$ falls between the benchmarks $4.5$ and $5.0$. Let's add these benchmarks to our diagram:\n\n![](images/fe/fe646c2f4691fe5d6b43dccda98380697c983f4d4dccdcfe00afa2263b83cb98.png)\n\nThe point that lies halfway between our two benchmarks is ${\\color{red}4.75}$.\n\n![](images/01/013d0ab42a5fe2e57604d381b355b73da7e5e29b005890f682e48e2802b3052b.png)\n\nSince $4.82$ is closest to $5.0$, this is the number we round to.\n\n![](images/86/86b0dfaca7823ed5810699aa50e6254765675ab01fc77e94081c2a1315b46b2c.png)\n\nTherefore, rounding $4.82$ to the nearest benchmark gives $5$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142658 1069 "e-14550" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142658 1071 "Using benchmarks with decimal parts of $0.0$ and $0.5$, round $9.3$ to the nearest benchmark." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142658 1072 #uuid "af63b527-a1a5-51b5-9db6-3ec39c76217a" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142658 1074 "Rounding to the nearest benchmark means finding the closest number with a decimal part of $0.0$ or $0.5$ to our number.\n\nLet's list the benchmarks with decimal parts $0.0$ and $0.5$ that are near $9.3{:}$\n\n$$\n9.0, \\qquad 9.5, \\qquad 10.0\n$$\n\nThe number $\\color{blue}9.3$ falls between the benchmarks $9.0$ and $9.5$, and the point that lies halfway between our two benchmarks is ${\\color{red}9.25}$.\n\n![](images/b7/b7fb8b36c97b3670273e46f0d4d77c477ddc3d1558a3e5c74ac3aeb98ebd2020.png)\n\nSince $9.3$ is closest to $9.5$, this is the number we round to.\n\nTherefore, rounding $9.3$ to the nearest benchmark gives $9.5$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142659 1069 "e-14569" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142659 1071 "Calculate the value of $2\\,\\dfrac 2 {9} \\times 3$, expressing the result as a mixed number." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142659 1072 #uuid "cba0777d-2ad9-5135-8b1f-bde135329d1e" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142659 1074 "First, we write\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac{2}{9}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 2 {9}=\\dfrac{(2\\times 9)+2}{9}= \\dfrac{20}{9}\n$$\n\nWe also write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3=\\dfrac{3}{1}\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{20}{\\color{blue}9} \\times \\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{1}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator ($\\color{blue}9$) and the second fraction's numerator ($\\color{red}3$) have a common factor of $3$.\n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{20}{9} \\cdot \\frac{3}{1} &= \\frac{20}{1} \\cdot \\frac{3}{9} \\\\\n&= 20 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{20 \\cdot 1}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{20}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{20}{3} =6\\,\\textrm{R} 2 = 6\\,\\dfrac 2 {3}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142660 1069 "e-14570" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142660 1071 "Multiply $4 \\times 2\\,\\dfrac{1}{4}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142660 1072 #uuid "2f9f1260-432a-56bc-b5e3-254b6f0fbad1" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142660 1074 "First, we write $4$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n4 = \\dfrac{4}{1}\n$$\n\nWe also write\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac{1}{4} = \\dfrac{(2 \\times 4) + 1}{4} = \\dfrac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{1} \\times \\dfrac{9}{4} = \\dfrac{4 \\times 9}{1 \\times 4} = \\dfrac{36}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we reduce the fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{36}{4} = \\dfrac{36 \\div 4}{4 \\div 4} = \\dfrac{9}{1} = 9\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142661 1069 "e-15654" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142661 1071 "![](images/7f/7fbc79b1d259f645a8296be12c2748e5abb05d02217f3628e24ef20781f06c71.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing number in the following division problem.\n$\\frac{0}{0}$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142661 1072 #uuid "7f3801c4-d25f-57e0-85b3-b52df08d7072" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142661 1074 "Let's start by interpreting our model:\n\n- The model shows $\\color{blue}4$ wholes. Each whole is split into *fifths*.\n\n![](images/20/2096f3451d19fc44bccca9ebe3033e9af29878aa928ad05b102e391be3f76105.png)\n\n- The fifths are grouped, and each group contains ${\\color{red}{\\dfrac{2}{5}}}$.\n\n![](images/96/96bc693ffe7fa65c90758e0d86a7176b19f7a279cfff12607926e0350448fe42.png)\n\n- There are $\\color{purple} 10$ groups in total.\n\n![](images/1e/1ebdc6bce6be33bbdd5fd7ff79a562c22e68103cebc8f0ded5ddac6833633989.png)\n\nSo, if we divide $\\color{blue}4$ wholes into groups of\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{\\dfrac{2}{5}}}\n$$\n\nwe get ${\\color{purple}{10}}$ groups in total.\n\nTherefore, the model represents the following division problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is\n\n$$\n\\bbox[3pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{red}\\dfrac{2}{5}}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142662 1069 "e-1674" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142662 1071 "What phrase represents the expression $(9 - 7) \\times 2$?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142662 1072 #uuid "9ef8b6bf-b244-5151-9217-5fd919f8e078" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142662 1074 "Since the expression uses parentheses, this operation comes first. So first, we should \"*Subtract $7$ from $9$*\".\n\nThe $\\times\\,2$ means that we should \"*then multiply by $2$*\".\n\nSo, the given expression is equivalent to \"*Subtract $7$ from $9$, then multiply by $2$.*\"" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142663 1069 "e-1675" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142663 1071 "What expression represents the phrase \"*Subtract $5$ from $7$, then multiply by $2$*\"?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142663 1072 #uuid "c7d3523d-4c21-5985-b0e8-22d4fcffa101" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142663 1074 "Checking our phrase carefully, we need to subtract $5$ from $7$ *before* multiplying by $2$.\n\nFor the phrase \"*Subtract $5$ from $7$*\", this means:\n$7-5$\n\nSince this operation comes first, we use parentheses:\n$(7 - 5)$\n\nFor the phrase \"*then multiply by $2$*\", this means that we should multiply the above expression by $2$. So we get:\n\n$$\n(7-5)\\times 2\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142664 1069 "e-1676" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142664 1071 "Two children, Charlie and Maria, have $3$ dogs and $2$ cats each. Write an expression represents the total number of pets that they have?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142664 1072 #uuid "3a5e404b-317e-50b5-9304-4b48297f96e9" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142664 1074 "We need to write an expression to represent the total number of pets.\n\nSince each of them has $3$ dogs and $2$ cats, the number of pets each child has is:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{3}_{\\text{dogs}}+\\underbrace{2}_{\\text{cats}}\n$$\n\nSince *each* child has this number of pets, and there are $2$ children, we must multiply the number of pets by $2$. Therefore, the total number of pets is:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(3 + 2)}_{\\text{pets per child}}\\times \\underbrace{2}_{\\text{children}}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142665 1069 "e-1681" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142665 1071 "Find an expression that is $8$ times smaller than the expression $5 + (2\\times 4)$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142665 1072 #uuid "fd992996-82eb-5a11-9503-6f77131ef34a" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142665 1074 "First, we note that \"${\\color{blue}8}$ *times smaller* than the expression\" means that we should *divide* the given expression by $8$.\n\nFirst, we add some brackets:\n\n$$\n\\left[5 + (2\\times 4)\\right]\n$$\n\nNext, we divide the expression by $\\color{blue}8$:\n\n$$\n\\left[5 + (2\\times 4)\\right]\\div {\\color{blue}8}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142666 1069 "e-1683" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142666 1071 "What expression is $7$ times larger than $5 + (3\\times 2)$?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142666 1072 #uuid "649d02eb-0cae-5f11-a25b-09307bf390cd" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142666 1074 "First, let's note the language. We want an expression that is \"$7$ times *larger*\" than the one above, which means that we should multiply the *entire* expression by $7$.\n\nFirst, since the expression already has parentheses, we add square brackets.\n\n$$\n[5 + (3\\times 2)]\n$$\n\nNow, we *multiply* the given expression by $7$. This gives:\n\n$$\n7\\times[5 + (3\\times 2)]\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142667 1069 "e-1753" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142667 1071 "What is missing in the following sentence?\n\n> In the coordinate plane, the vertical number line is called the $\\underline{\\phantom{{}^{0000000000000000000}}}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142667 1072 #uuid "0c8d2954-af35-5d97-9ee3-68f9274f423d" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142667 1074 "In the coordinate plane, the vertical number line is called the **$y$-axis**.\n\n![](images/4e/4ea7c731ba55f844b46d3866428c03c1e90117b350351e7a041943f2cfdf7ce3.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142668 1069 "e-1754" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142668 1071 "What is missing in the following sentence?\n\n> To graph the point $(0,2)$ in the coordinate plane, start at the origin and move $\\underline{\\phantom{{}^{0000000000000000000}}}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142668 1072 #uuid "ad84f44d-7b5d-5807-8c41-1e916b8e141d" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142668 1074 "The $x$-coordinate is zero. Therefore, we do not need to move along the $x$-axis to plot the point. We only need to move along the $y$-axis.\n\nSo, to graph the point $(0,2)$, we start at the origin and move **$2$ units up.**\n\n![](images/7f/7fb41e551628a1d02a8d676749a83476b3e0133735154e1fc609a76e361013cd.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142669 1069 "e-1756" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142669 1071 "What is the $v$-coordinate of the point $A$?\n\n![](images/18/18583fa1a22432395f7ecc26a322570734ce657ed227d24c3ee74ffd083d43d6.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142669 1072 #uuid "3ceca8f4-e308-59e7-bd0b-71f2767e89f5" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142669 1074 "To get to point $A$, we start at the origin, move $\\color{red}6$ units right (along the $\\color{red}t$-axis) and then $\\color{blue}4$ units up (along the $\\color{blue}v$-axis).\n\n![](images/e5/e55d6acec9c94e1b05839d2d433cb781bd8696f1cb5114cfe0cedd7ba7451786.png)\n\nSo the coordinates of $A$ are $({\\color{red}{6}},{\\color{blue}{4}})$. Therefore, the $v$-coordinate is ${\\color{blue}{4}}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142670 1069 "e-1830" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142670 1071 "What is $62 \\div 9$?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142670 1072 #uuid "be655630-0063-5e1b-b095-0d6825be0798" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142670 1074 "The whole number part of $62\\div 9$ must be ${\\color{blue}6}$, because\n\n- $9$ can go into $62$ a total of ${\\color{blue}6}$ times ($9 \\times {\\color{blue}6} = 54$), but\n- $9$ cannot go into $62$ a total of $7$ times ($9 \\times 7 = 63$).\n\nThe remainder is\n\n$$\n62 - 54 = {\\color{red}8}\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n62\\div 9 = {\\color{blue}{6}} \\,\\textrm{R}\\, {\\color{red}{8}}\n$$\n\nTo write the remainder as a fraction, we divide it by the original divisor ($9$). Therefore,\n\n$$\n62\\div 9 = {\\color{blue}6}\\,\\dfrac{\\color{red}8}{9}\\,\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142671 1069 "e-1831" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142671 1071 "A fisherman evenly distributed $61$ pounds of fish into $4$ boxes. How many pounds of fish did he put in each box?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142671 1072 #uuid "c503bf6e-cf3b-5ba2-a2f4-08229e9098d3" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142671 1074 "We need to work out $61\\div 4$. We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$\\color{blue}5$$4$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$2$$1$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$0$$\\color{red}1$ |\n| --- |\n\nFrom the long division, we conclude that\n\n$$\n61\\div 4 = {\\color{blue}{15}} \\,\\textrm{R}\\, {\\color{red}{1}}\n$$\n\nTo write the remainder as a fraction, we divide it by the original divisor ($4$). So, the mixed number is\n\n$$\n61 \\div 4 = {\\color{blue}15} \\, \\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fisherman put\n\n$$\n15 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\npounds of fish in each box." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142672 1069 "e-1832" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142672 1071 "What is $74 \\div 6$?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142672 1072 #uuid "d3ca57d3-fd57-573d-8e1b-ab71562c9bc9" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142672 1074 "We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$\\color{blue}2$$6$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$1$$4$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$\\color{red}2$ |\n| --- |\n\nFrom the long division, we conclude that\n\n$$\n74\\div 6 = {\\color{blue}{12}} \\,\\textrm{R}\\, {\\color{red}{2}}\n$$\n\nTo write the remainder as a fraction, we divide it by the original divisor ($6$). So, the mixed number is\n\n$$\n74\\div 6 = {\\color{blue}{12}} \\, \\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the fractional part by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{6} = \\dfrac{2 \\div 2}{6 \\div 2} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the simplified mixed number is\n\n$$\n74\\div 6 = 12 \\, \\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142673 1069 "e-1833" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142673 1071 "What is $942 \\div 9\\,$?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142673 1072 #uuid "061d6a29-9909-5abb-baf1-5fb250befeb5" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142673 1074 "We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$\\color{blue}0$$\\color{blue}4$$9$$\\!\\!\\phantom{\\mid}\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$0$$4$$2$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$6$$\\color{red}6$ |\n| --- |\n\nFrom the long division, we conclude that\n\n$$\n942 \\div 9 = {\\color{blue}{104}} \\,\\textrm{R}\\, {\\color{red}{6}}\n$$\n\nTo write the remainder as a fraction, we divide it by the original divisor ($9$). So, the mixed number is\n\n$$\n942 \\div 9 = {\\color{blue}{104}} \\, \\dfrac{\\color{red}6}{9}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the fractional part by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{9} = \\dfrac{6 \\div 3}{9 \\div 3} = \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the simplified mixed number is\n\n$$\n942 \\div 9 = 104 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142674 1069 "e-1834" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142674 1071 "A group of environmentalists planted $280$ trees along $14$ rows. If there are the same number of trees in each row, how many trees are in each row? Use the relationship between multiplication and division.\n\n1. $18$ trees\n2. $20$ trees\n3. $19$ trees" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142674 1072 #uuid "92eaa0ae-aee8-5ac0-9d7e-b61f9c96ccfc" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142674 1074 "To find out how many trees are in each row, we divide the number of trees ($280$) by the number of rows ($14$). So, we need to work out $280 \\div 14$.\n\nIf\n\n$$\n280 \\div 14 = \\square\n$$\n\nthen the corresponding multiplication equation is:\n\n$$\n\\square\\times 14 = 280\n$$\n\nWe can represent this multiplication problem using an area model:\n\n![](images/3d/3df101307774a2b4d4d87597a43a4f6e9c1ba00ca532b588a48e4afba4931cd3.png)\n\nFirst, let's list the possible answers, from smallest to largest:\n\n$$\n18,\\quad 19,\\quad 20\n$$\n\nWe'll try each of the given numbers until we find one that satisfies the multiplication equation.\n\n- $18 \\times 14 = 252 \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n- $19 \\times 14 = 252 + 14 = 266 \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n- $20 \\times 14 = 266 + 14 = 280 \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\nSo we have:\n\n![](images/f1/f1b9dcbab59bec3483df421220e919a92af2135f50a69c29ebfe25360aedb2d6.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n280 \\div 14 = 20\n$$\n\nSo there are $20$ trees in each row." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142675 1069 "e-1835" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142675 1071 "Given the following options, what is the result of calculating $1,335 \\div 15$ using the relationship between multiplication and division?\n\n1. $89$\n2. $88$\n3. $90$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142675 1072 #uuid "7b07359e-937b-5a0c-8cb2-f32bb0f0b651" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142675 1074 "If\n\n$$\n1,335 \\div 15 = \\square\n$$\n\nthen the corresponding multiplication equation is:\n\n$$\n\\square \\times 15 = 1,335\n$$\n\nWe can represent this multiplication problem using an area model:\n\n![](images/23/23472b4b3b9e08379b782e32561a46b46132aa4ee3f7c13df5711170d04c714a.png)\n\nFirst, let's list the possible answers, from smallest to largest:\n\n$$\n88,\\quad 89,\\quad 90\n$$\n\nWe'll try each of the given numbers until we find one that satisfies the multiplication equation.\n\n- $88 \\times 15 = 1,320 \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n- $89 \\times 15 = 1,320 + 15 = 1,335 \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\nSo we have:\n\n![](images/71/712ae12dcfb728e8a88dc3b4912bda3f01998feb44814d0d9c5e45934f686af1.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n1,335 \\div 15 = 89\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142676 1069 "e-1836" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142676 1071 "Given the following options, what is the result of calculating $96 \\div 16$ using the relationship between multiplication and division?\n\n1. $5$\n2. $6$\n3. $7$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142676 1072 #uuid "6c2adda6-1bb8-5e7f-acfd-efa13ee477d9" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142676 1074 "To find which choice is correct, we start by writing the corresponding multiplication equation. If\n\n$$\n96 \\div 16 = \\square\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\square \\times 16 = 96\n$$\n\nWe can represent this multiplication problem using an area model:\n\n![](images/ef/ef4c4260eb1f3cf09c6594ddadf15d6badb9956b82486128e5f602e26028aa7b.png)\n\nWe'll try each of the given numbers ($5,6,7$) until we find one that satisfies the multiplication equation.\n\n- $5 \\times 16 = 80 \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n- $6 \\times 16 = 80 + 16 = 96 \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\nSo we have:\n\n![](images/83/830c87378171135fb003d6359178d33b8229600c3caba0bc29f1732a745b7075.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n96 \\div 16 = 6\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142677 1069 "e-1845" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142677 1071 "A wooden slat was cut into two parts. One of the pieces is $\\dfrac{6}{5}\\,\\textrm{ft}$ long and the other is $\\dfrac{7}{4}\\,\\textrm{ft}$ long. How long was the original slat? Express your final answer as a mixed number." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142677 1072 #uuid "9d61ca3d-f117-5279-80d2-1ee1bd9a68b3" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142677 1074 "To find the length of the original slat, we need to calculate the sum\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{5} + \\dfrac{7}{4}\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with unlike denominators, we need to express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nLet's look at the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{\\color{red}5} + \\dfrac{7}{\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nIn this question, we can make a common denominator of\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}5} \\times {\\color{blue}4} = 20\n$$\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{5}\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $20$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{5} = \\dfrac{6 \\times 4}{5 \\times 4} = \\dfrac{24}{20}\n$$\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{4}\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $20$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $5$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{4} = \\dfrac{7 \\times 5}{4 \\times 5} = \\dfrac{35}{20}\n$$\n\nWe can now add the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we add the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{24}{20} + \\dfrac{35}{20} = \\dfrac{59}{20}\n$$\n\nConverting this to a mixed number, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{59}{20} = 2\\dfrac{19}{20}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slat was originally\n\n$$\n2\\dfrac{19}{20}\\,\\textrm{ft}\n$$\n\nlong." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142678 1069 "e-1846" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142678 1071 "John spends $\\dfrac{5}{4}$ of an hour in the gym. During this time, he's either on the exercise bike or lifting weights. One day, he spent $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ on the exercise bike. How much time did John spend lifting weights?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142678 1072 #uuid "6f1285e4-05b1-5f7b-8086-f3dd9c9b0daf" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142678 1074 "To find how much time John spent lifting weights, we need to calculate the difference\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{4} - \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with unlike denominators, we need to express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nSince $4$ is a multiple of $2$, we can make a common denominator of $4$.\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $4$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{1 \\times 2}{2 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{2}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can now subtract the fractions. We keep the denominator the same and we subtract the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{4} - \\frac{2}{4} = \\frac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, John spent\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nof an hour lifting weights." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142679 1069 "e-1848" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142679 1071 "Tara cuts a piece of fabric that is $\\dfrac{9}{4}\\,\\textrm{yd}$ long into two smaller pieces. If one of the pieces is $\\dfrac{15}{14}\\,\\textrm{yd}$ long, how long is the other piece? Express your final answer as a mixed number." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142679 1072 #uuid "6e667a13-c537-5006-b876-f1bf0b16c6f5" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142679 1074 "To determine the length of the other piece, we need to calculate the difference\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{4} - \\dfrac{15}{14}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with unlike denominators, we need to express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nLet's look at the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{\\color{red}4} - \\dfrac{15}{\\color{blue}14}\n$$\n\nThe least common denominator of $\\color{red}4$ and $\\color{blue}14$ is $28$.\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $28$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $7$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{4} = \\dfrac{9 \\times 7}{4 \\times 7} = \\dfrac{63}{28}\n$$\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{15}{14}\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $28$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{15}{14} = \\dfrac{15 \\times 2}{14 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{30}{28}\n$$\n\nWe can now subtract the fractions. We keep the denominator the same and subtract the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{63}{28} - \\frac{30}{28} = \\frac{33}{28}\n$$\n\nConverting this to a mixed number, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{33}{28} = 1\\dfrac{5}{28}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Tara's other piece is\n\n$$\n1\\dfrac{5}{28}\\,\\textrm{yd}\n$$\n\nlong." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142680 1069 "e-1849" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142680 1071 "Christian used $1\\dfrac{3}{4}\\,\\textrm{L}$ of milk to make pancakes. Before making the pancakes, he had $2\\,\\dfrac{6}{7}\\,\\textrm{L}$ of milk. How much milk does he have now?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142680 1072 #uuid "022717a6-c2f6-5346-b82c-ef8384b31e44" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142680 1074 "To determine the amount of milk Christian has left, we need to calculate the difference\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac{6}{7} - 1\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two mixed numbers, we subtract whole numbers from whole numbers and fractions from fractions. Subtracting the whole numbers, we get\n\n$$\n2-1 = {\\color{red}{1}}\n$$\n\nNow we subtract the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{7} - \\dfrac 3 4\n$$\n\nLet's look at the denominators. The least common denominator of $4$ and $7$ is $28$.\n\n- To put $\\dfrac 6 7$ over a denominator of $28$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $4$:\n$\\dfrac{6}{7} = \\dfrac{6 \\times 4}{7 \\times 4} = \\dfrac{24}{28}$\n- To put $\\dfrac 3 4$ over a denominator of $28$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $7$:\n$\\dfrac{3}{4} = \\dfrac{3 \\times 7}{4 \\times 7} = \\dfrac{21}{28}$\n\nWe can now subtract the fractions. We keep the denominator the same and we subtract the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{7} - \\frac{3}{4} = \\frac{24}{28} - \\frac{21}{28} = \\frac{3}{28}\n$$\n\nSubtracting, we get\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac{6}{7} - \\dfrac{3}{4} = {\\color{red}{1}}\\,{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{28}}\n$$\n\nSo, Christian has\n\n$$\n1\\dfrac{3}{28}\\,\\textrm{L}\n$$\n\nof milk remaining." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142681 1069 "e-1852" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142681 1071 "Nadia, a construction worker, cuts pieces of wood for her colleagues. She cuts $1\\,\\dfrac{3}{4}\\,\\textrm{yd}$ of wood for Louis and $2\\,\\dfrac{7}{10}\\,\\textrm{yd}$ of wood for Diana. What is the total length of wood she gave to her two colleagues?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142681 1072 #uuid "f71fd8b3-ebf3-5720-81a3-024027fdb3f4" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142681 1074 "To find the length of wood she gave to Louis and Diana, we need to calculate the sum\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac{3}{4} + 2\\,\\dfrac{7}{10}\n$$\n\nTo add two mixed numbers, we add whole numbers to whole numbers and fractions to fractions. Adding the whole numbers, we get\n\n$$\n1+2 = {\\color{red}{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, we add the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4} + \\dfrac{7}{10}\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with unlike denominators, we need to express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator. In this question, we can make a common denominator of $20$.\n\n- To put $\\dfrac{3}{4}$ over a denominator of $20$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $5$:\n$\\dfrac{3}{4} = \\dfrac{3 \\times 5}{4 \\times 5} = \\dfrac{15}{20}$\n- To put $\\dfrac{7}{10}$ over a denominator of $20$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n$\\dfrac{7}{10} = \\dfrac{7 \\times 2}{10 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{14}{20}$\n\nWe can now add the fractions. We keep the denominator the same and we add the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{20} + \\frac{14}{20} = \\frac{29}{20}\n$$\n\nWe can convert the fraction\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{29}{20}\n$$\n\nto a mixed number:\n\n$$\n29 \\div 20 = 1\\,\\textrm{R}\\,9 = {\\color{blue}{1\\,\\dfrac 9{20}}}\n$$\n\nAdding, we get\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac{3}{4} + 2\\,\\dfrac{7}{10} = {\\color{red}{3}} + {\\color{blue}{1\\,\\dfrac 9{20}}} = 4\\,\\dfrac{9}{20}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in total, Nadia cut\n\n$$\n4\\,\\dfrac{9}{20}\\,\\textrm{yd}\n$$\n\nof wood." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142682 1069 "e-1854" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142682 1071 "Find an expression that represents \"*add $7$ and $1$, then triple the result.*\"" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142682 1072 #uuid "2845309b-88e1-526c-9125-66be3a91d51f" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142682 1074 "Looking carefully, we see that \"*add $7$ and $1$*\" comes first.\n\n$$\n(7+1)\n$$\n\nNext, \"*then triple the result*\" means we need to multiply the above expression by $3$. This gives:\n\n$$\n(7+1)\\times 3\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142683 1071 "What fraction is represented on the number line depicted below?\n\n![](images/37/37211096a87ab368d3e2cabaed96ce64025c1b08d74d6d29183beba3a8094268.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142683 1072 #uuid "26ad978c-0724-50bb-9c41-3da55e7448cf" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142683 1074 "Notice that the line is split into $\\color{blue}8$ equal parts.\n\nThe number we are interested in is located $\\color{red}3$ steps from $0$ (left to right).\n\n![](images/4a/4a40b162045e143b5b741cc914185c7383962aee2738f8a52a61b0c70e585169.png)\n\nTherefore, the picture represents the fraction\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}8}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142684 1069 "e-2100" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142684 1071 "The line plot below shows the ages of the members in a judo class. Each cross represents a different member. What is the most common age of a member in this class?\n\n![](images/66/66fbb56281295814453191238292c42513b25e053b7721e070b724d74ee0d27f.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142684 1072 #uuid "0e188f39-a4e4-5af5-86f5-ca08bf4f9894" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142684 1074 "The plot tells us that:\n\n- there are ${\\color{blue}2}$ members who are $7$-years-old,\n- there are ${\\color{blue}4}$ members who are $8$-years-old,\n- there are ${\\color{blue}4}$ members who are $9$-years-old.\n- there are ${\\color{red}5}$ members who are $10$-years-old,\n- there are ${\\color{blue}2}$ members who are $11$-years-old,\n- there is ${\\color{blue}1}$ member who is $12$-years-old.\n\n![](images/03/034632e643d45b6906f8c1e1f44c926dad84261c94c765e1971c728323c0496a.png)\n\nTherefore, $10$ is the most common age of a member of the judo class." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142685 1069 "e-2101" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142685 1071 "A local meal delivery service kept track of the number of promotional pizzas delivered, at nights, in the last week. The line plot below shows\nthe results, where each cross represents one pizza. How many nights did they deliver $5$ or more pizzas?\n\n![](images/9e/9ee44156861fd3a80cca7f207cf838d3c42923cfb12dd376cd11df2845c55579.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142685 1072 #uuid "ca21f6a4-b432-5b81-8c69-4cba1968a6d6" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142685 1074 "The plot tells us that:\n\n- on the first night, ${\\color{blue}4}$ pizzas were delivered\n- on the second night, ${\\color{blue}2}$ pizzas were delivered\n- on the third night, ${\\color{blue}2}$ pizzas were delivered\n- on the fourth night, ${\\color{blue}3}$ pizzas were delivered\n- on the fifth night, ${\\color{red}5}$ pizzas were delivered\n- on the sixth night, ${\\color{red}6}$ pizzas were delivered\n- on the seventh night, ${\\color{blue}4}$ pizzas were delivered\n\nThey delivered five or more pizzas on the fifth night and the sixth night.\n\nTherefore, in total, they delivered five or more pizzas on $2$ nights.\n\n![](images/f7/f7f3b57564c9d5c7a82c9aa0edd7113b227a910253dbe918af9cc7a642f08bf8.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142686 1069 "e-2102" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142686 1071 "The following table reports the typing speed, in words per minute, of ten students after an online typing test.\n\nMake a line plot to represent the given table.\n\n| Name | Typing speed |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Elizabeth | $40$ |\n| Simon | $40$ |\n| Joseph | $40$ |\n| Emma | $41$ |\n| Edward | $42$ |\n| Lilly | $42$ |\n| Paul | $42$ |\n| Fiona | $43$ |\n| Gaby | $43$ |\n| John | $44$ |" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142686 1072 #uuid "c9ba6588-ae0d-547b-a755-02b2a4a3d212" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142686 1074 "From the table:\n\n- There are $3$ students with a typing speed of $40$ words per minute. So, there will be $3$ crosses over $40$.\n- There is $1$ student with a typing speed of $41$ words per minute. So, there will be $1$ cross over $41$.\n- There are $3$ students with a typing speed of $42$ words per minute. So, there will be $3$ crosses over $42$.\n- There are $2$ students with a typing speed of $43$ words per minute. So, there will be $2$ crosses over $43$.\n- There is $1$ student with a typing speed of $44$ words per minute. So, there will be $1$ crosses over $44$.\n\nTherefore, we get the line plot shown below:\n\n![](images/0c/0c899f9dc1bf74af90d2bd35a1ad694e1f8acf03a6dec702c0474a107d6fce8c.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142687 1069 "e-2103" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142687 1071 "During the last weekend, the number of hours spent doing homework by the students of a math class was\n\n$1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,\\,3,\\,1,\\,3,\\,1,\\,4,\\,4,\\,1,\\,0$.\n\nMake a line plot to represent the given data." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142687 1072 #uuid "e399f58a-27eb-5de7-8de9-91929b00ea5e" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142687 1074 "Let's look at the given scores:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}1}\\quad {0} \\quad{\\color{blue}1} \\quad {2} \\quad{\\color{red}3} \\quad{\\color{red}3}\\quad{\\color{blue}1} \\quad{\\color{red}3}\\quad{\\color{blue}1}\\quad {\\bf 4} \\quad {\\bf 4} \\quad{\\color{blue}1} \\quad {0}\n$$\n\nWe see that:\n\n- $2$ students spent ${\\ 0}$ hours doing homework. So, there will be $2$ crosses over $0$.\n- $5$ students spent ${\\color{blue}1}$ hour doing homework. So, there will be $5$ crosses over $1$.\n- $1$ student spent $2$ hours doing homework. So, there will be $1$ cross over $2$.\n- $3$ students spent ${\\color{red}3}$ hours doing homework. So, there will be $3$ crosses over $3$.\n- $2$ students spent ${\\bf 4}$ hours doing homework. So, there will be $2$ crosses over $4$.\n\nTherefore, we get the line plot shown below:\n\n![](images/b9/b935b7dc6413d036b0c8ffce3f086acf12c01039f8657875cfc02f80be637ca1.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142688 1069 "e-2108" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142688 1071 "The line plot shows the weight, in ounces, of some bags of fries in a restaurant. Each cross represents a different bag. If the chef redistributes the weight equally among each bag of fries, how much would each bag weigh?\n\n![](images/ae/aec0542f972c2e8f9012e5b80f8a169655b08a5e20ddb101ae3037eebadd77f7.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142688 1072 #uuid "3f37493b-da24-5785-aeb7-08a05cf4e124" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142688 1074 "The total weight of the bags (in ounces) is\n\n$$\n2 + 2 + 2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4} + 2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4} + 2 \\, \\dfrac{2}{4} \\,\n$$\n\nTo add mixed numbers, we add whole numbers to whole numbers and fractions to fractions.\n\n- Adding the whole numbers, we get\n$2 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 = 10$.\n- Adding the fractions, we get\n$\\dfrac{1}{4} +\\dfrac{1}{4} + \\dfrac{2}{4} = \\dfrac{4}{4} \\,$.\nSimplifying this fraction, we get\n$\\dfrac{4}{4} = 4 \\div 4 = 1$.\n\nTherefore, the total weight of the bags (in ounces) is\n\n$$\n2 + 2 + 2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4} + 2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4} + 2 \\, \\dfrac{2}{4} = 10 + 1 = 11 \\,\n$$\n\nTo redistribute this total weight equally among each bag of fries, we divide the total weight by the number of bags:\n\n$$\n11 \\div 5 = 2 \\,\\textrm{R} \\, 1 = 2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, each bag would weigh\n\n$$\n2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nounces." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142689 1069 "e-2109" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142689 1071 "![](images/a7/a78644645fc1974dab591c29f6a289a0ef8b955cca3f2c7b7b468e40059199c2.png)\n\nThe line plot above shows the amount of rain, in centimeters, that fell in Houston, Texas, over four days. Each cross represents a different day. Find the total amount of rain that fell in Houston during those four days." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142689 1072 #uuid "3c2859f5-f3f7-5325-98ee-05c6440ef67f" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142689 1074 "From the graph, we note the following:\n\n- There were $2$ days on which $\\dfrac{2}{3}$ centimeters of rain fell.\n- There were $2$ days on which $\\dfrac{3}{4}$ centimeters of rain fell.\n\nSo the total amount of rain that fell is given by\n\n$$\n2 \\times \\dfrac{2}{3} + 2 \\times \\dfrac{3}{4} = \\dfrac{4}{3} + \\dfrac{6}{4} \\,\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the fraction\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{4}\n$$\n\nby dividing the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6 \\div 2}{4 \\div 2} = \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to add the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{3} + \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with unlike denominators, we need to express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nHere, we can make a common denominator of $6$.\n\n- To put $\\dfrac{4}{3}$ over a denominator of $6$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n$\\dfrac{4}{3} = \\dfrac{4 \\times 2}{3 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{8}{6}$\n- To put $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ over a denominator of $6$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n$\\dfrac{3}{2} = \\dfrac{3 \\times 3}{2 \\times 3} = \\dfrac{9}{6}$\n\nWe can now add the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we add the numerators.\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{3} + \\frac{3}{2} = \\frac{8}{6} + \\frac{9}{6} = \\frac{17}{6}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{17}{6}\n$$\n\nto a mixed number.\n\n$$\n17 \\div 6 = 2 \\,\\textrm{R} \\,5 = 2 \\, \\dfrac{5}{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2 \\, \\dfrac{5}{6}\n$$\n\ncentimeters of rain fell during this period." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142690 1069 "e-2110" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142690 1071 "![](images/3a/3aefd6a3fd2d15525fdf778ec2f9592f96ad54a0c901cfabc1d94e2dd647623f.png)\n\nThe line plot above gives the weight of four vehicles in a scrapyard. Each cross represents a different vehicle. What is the difference between the weights of the heaviest and the lightest vehicles?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142690 1072 #uuid "077fbd38-f86e-57ad-a59c-9358a6137467" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142690 1074 "The plot tells us that the lightest vehicle weighs\n\n$$\n\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\ntons, and the heaviest vehicle weighs\n\n$$\n\\color{red}\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\ntons.\n\n![](images/04/0487fc01404e499201b7e9a39383059d9cb7f9ae291539b1271f6cf8797eedaf.png)\n\nThe difference between the weights is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} - \\dfrac{1}{8} \\,\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with unlike denominators, we need to express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nHere, we can make a common denominator of $8$.\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $8$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{1 \\times 4}{2 \\times 4} = \\dfrac{4}{8}\n$$\n\nWe can now subtract the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we subtract the numerators.\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{8} = \\frac{4}{8} - \\frac{1}{8} = \\frac{3}{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the difference between the weights of the heaviest and lightest vehicles is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{8}\n$$\n\ntons." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142691 1069 "e-2111" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142691 1071 "What is the $x$-coordinate of the point $A$?\n\n![](images/de/de4fa466dd9f9dc97691852e2749fdb39ee26cdfa76b1a6f20090ccdbfd1a24a.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142691 1072 #uuid "fc9b06e2-e0cb-55e0-9b6a-553e8ef00009" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142691 1074 "To find the $x$-coordinate of the point $A$, we start at the origin, move $\\color{red}5$ units right (along the $\\color{red}x$-axis) and then $\\color{blue}3$ units up (along the $\\color{blue}y$-axis). Therefore, the $x$-coordinate is $5$.\n\n![](images/10/10a68c87c788b3cdc5b1e9b72595294f90026258b56bfe17c4c816cb7124afc6.png)\n\nSo the coordinates of $A$ are $({\\color{red}5}, {\\color{blue}3})$. Therefore, the $x$-coordinate is $5$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142692 1069 "e-2112" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142692 1071 "The graph below shows the distances from students' houses to their school, in meters, and the total time required to get to school, in minutes. Which student requires the longest time to get to school?\n\n![](images/76/76604e5f4ee752bce67666d24e29ccce3163ee9f432b54b25758922235abb3b1.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142692 1072 #uuid "7a4c2e2e-89ee-5ce6-8fe6-4b00978f04d5" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142692 1074 "\"Time\" is represented by the $x$-axis. So, we need to find the point with the greatest $x$-coordinate.\n\n![](images/f8/f8a42642665a3bc164c72a6b36db039c42afd392e5761cdcb8a0731dea1b4f07.png)\n\nTherefore, Lily requires the longest time to get to school." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142693 1069 "e-2113" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142693 1071 "The diagram below shows the number of candies and the number of sandwiches that $5$ friends ate during a holiday party. How many sandwiches did Diane eat?\n\n![](images/9a/9ace16681aaa33b30fbff8f1a0a6396b957635088f483896efc5496e9f998ed5.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142693 1072 #uuid "80a9bf9d-e7f8-5f60-8b96-4171878de4eb" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142693 1074 "In the given coordinate plane, the horizontal $\\color{red}x$-axis corresponds to the number of candies, and the vertical $\\color{blue}y$-axis corresponds to the number of sandwiches.\n\n![](images/c7/c75b46ead0385fadc2e488275b34c62f9d314ee57946e5ca08ad5f45241e801e.png)\n\nThe point that represents Diane has coordinates $({\\color{red}4},{\\color{blue}2})$. The $\\color{blue}y$-coordinate is $\\color{blue}2$, which means that Diane ate $2$ sandwiches." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142694 1069 "e-2114" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142694 1071 "Jack, a locomotive driver, recorded the distance traveled by a train every hour. The following ordered pairs are recorded:\n$(0,0), \\quad (1,150),\\quad (2,300),\\quad (3,450)$\n\nGiven that the first number in each ordered pair is the time, and the second is the distance, which plot corresponds to the given data?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142694 1072 #uuid "4b751d30-4cf3-5364-b095-8ac526cbc59a" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142694 1074 "We have $4$ points with the following coordinates:\n\n| $x$-coordinate | $y$-coordinate |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $150$ |\n| $2$ | $300$ |\n| $3$ | $450$ |\n\nPlotting these, we get the following graph:\n\n![](images/a8/a8a401f480c8f24c7c8c78bebd05c86f64233c88676af6fc5ce04036dff8bdf6.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142695 1069 "e-2115" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142695 1071 "Linda took a walk around Paris. She started at the Eiffel Tower, and she walked for $5$ hours. Using a GPS navigator, she recorded her distance from the Eiffel Tower after every hour. The results are shown below.\n\n![](images/13/1350d41b475142c9bb0affb2530b5825c62804348c5c12f50a6c7347f2e52923.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding Linda's walk?\n\n1. After $5$ hours, her distance from the Eiffel Tower was $2$ kilometers.\n2. After $4$ hours, she was at her maximum distance from the Eiffel Tower.\n3. Her distance from the Eiffel Tower after $1$ hour and after $3$ hours was the same." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142695 1072 #uuid "3d99d84d-694d-598d-806e-2399f9ac4c71" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142695 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- From the graph, the distance to the Eiffel Tower after $\\color{red}5$ hours was $\\color{blue}4$ kilometers, not $2$ kilometers. So, statement I is false.![](images/62/626730072f9f4e9303813c76dc5a992dd3bfbd7d5b5972fa290a49adee9d7e8a.png)\n\n- From the graph, the largest distance from the Eiffel Tower was after $\\color{red}4$ hours, and this distance was $\\color{blue}5$ kilometers. So, statement II is true.![](images/e9/e9c8d326b8d0b405cfaaeecbab4c3f88b5ad6d394073e1c05fa397a07702e5b3.png)\n\n- From the graph, the distance to the Eiffel tower after $\\color{red}1$ hour and after $\\color{red}3$ hours was $\\color{blue}2$ kilometers. Therefore, statement III is true.![](images/4d/4dd782deee4f8e03533a67d0aa7b6c452b4f451d3c2ae3f25148a93b30bbe34e.png)\n\nIn conclusion, only statements II and III are true." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142696 1069 "e-2116" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142696 1071 "The diagram shows a map of a town. What is at $(5,2)$?\n\n![](images/c8/c8d5e7778d61fb7ec130ca058d0f289e4c64d8204858c79aab1e0559ba1f673d.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142696 1072 #uuid "1ab5f96e-4c1c-5e6b-bcf2-a0e19159fc48" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142696 1074 "The ordered pair $(5,2)$ means that the $x$-coordinate of the location is $5$ and the $y$-coordinate is $2$.\n\n![](images/8f/8f400e605d312a80c97375984cf4fe4da4273bd447b5dc4213c00ef067830a92.png)\n\nWe see that, at $(5,2)$, there is a school." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142697 1069 "e-2117" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142697 1071 "The diagram shows the location of $4$ students around their school. How far away from the school does the furthest student live?\n\n![](images/0c/0c96d658016b87f4ae2ea98491e9b9e227edfcf1fb79d4755c596a11b17c37a4.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142697 1072 #uuid "386506b1-06bd-5148-b0bd-96e6c4bda0ff" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142697 1074 "From the map, we see that:\n\n- The distance from Francis to the school (parallel to the $x$-axis) is $5\\:\\textrm{km}$.\n- The distance from Helen to the school (parallel to the $y$-axis) is $3\\:\\textrm{km}$.\n- The distance from Jose to the school (parallel to the $y$-axis) is $4\\:\\textrm{km}$.\n- The distance from Ruth to the school (parallel to the $x$-axis) is $4\\:\\textrm{km}$.\n\nSo we conclude that Francis lives furthest away from the school, at a distance of $5\\:\\textrm{km}$ from the school." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142698 1069 "e-2118" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142698 1071 "![](images/62/624f299af8ed572cc3c28b6d6830272a7f5bc4de4eae361afc341ce27f864850.png)\n\nKen maps his friends' houses on the coordinate plane. Which of the following are true?\n\n1. Ken lives furthest from Julia\n2. Ken lives at an equal distance from Judy and Joyce\n3. Ken lives $1\\:\\textrm{mile}$ from Ann" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142698 1072 #uuid "929df8ab-6057-570a-8a67-1564ae3b3e96" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142698 1074 "From the map, we see that:\n\n- The distance from Ken's house to Julia's house is $3\\:\\textrm{miles}$.\n- The distance from Ken's house to Joyce's house is $4\\:\\textrm{miles}$.\n- The distance from Ken's house to Judy's house is $4\\:\\textrm{miles}$.\n- The distance from Ken's house to Ann's house is $2\\:\\textrm{miles}$.\n\nNow, we can analyze each statement in turn:\n\n- We conclude that Ken lives furthest from Joyce and Judy, not Julia. So statement I is false.\n- Ken lives at an equal distance of $4\\:\\textrm{miles}$ from both Judy and Joyce, so statement II is true.\n- Ken lives $2\\:\\textrm{miles}$ from Ann, not $1\\:\\textrm{mile}$, so statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142699 1069 "e-2119" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142699 1071 "Peter is going for a walk. Starting from his house, Peter travels $3$ kilometers east, and then $3$ kilometers north. He arrives at the home of one of his friends. Who is this friend?\n\n![](images/2f/2f8912926f5b2a229874cb57e6a123456232f58f09bbaed9b83f4f05f4e529a0.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142699 1072 #uuid "2f09de8f-08c7-5188-9de9-b8ff44cfd45d" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142699 1074 "Moving $3\\:\\textrm{km}$ east means a shift of $3$ unit right, parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nMoving $3\\:\\textrm{km}$ north means a shift of $3$ units up, parallel to the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/2a/2a94bdda6304017bd906c1d4420bd32063a20a95f55020cf53b3f233008ea937.png)\n\nConsequently, Peter arrives at John's house." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142700 1069 "e-2124" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142700 1071 "What rule describes the pattern below?\n$56,\\,52,\\,48,\\,44,\\,40$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142700 1072 #uuid "875074df-1e1b-55ee-bc6e-fe9bca9f13a7" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142700 1074 "The pattern starts from $56$ and subtracts $4$ on each step.\n\n![](images/56/56071b2477f019e636ee516c47e0c7e91a0730a71191412ee21a2854a8094c1c.png)\n\nTherefore, the rule is \"subtract $4$\"." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142701 1069 "e-2125" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142701 1071 "Find the missing number in the pattern below.\n\n$0$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142701 1072 #uuid "1b109f57-c2f8-50af-aa67-40c3271aeffd" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142701 1074 "We see that the rule for the pattern is \"multiply by $3$\".\n\n![](images/31/3131cc7bb8267bfa60990d2a8085dbb4ad323bdcee56f6641fa64fc6ac1cea95.png)\n\nThe missing number comes after $54$, so we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\n54\\times 3 = 162\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $162$.\n\nLet's check our answer. Since $486$ comes after the missing number, if we multiply the missing number by $3$, then we must get $486$.\n\n$$\n162\\times 3 =486\\, \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142702 1069 "e-2127" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142702 1071 "Consider patterns A and B, shown in the table below.\n\n| Pattern A: subtract $2$ | $12$ | $10$ | $8$ | $6$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Pattern B: subtract $6$ | $36$ | $30$ | $24$ | $18$ | $12$ |\n\nWhat is missing in the following sentence?\n\n- Each term in pattern A is $\\underline{\\phantom{{}^{000000000000000000000}}}$ than the corresponding term in pattern B." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142702 1072 #uuid "83f544ad-c8cf-5fdc-bf1c-5218b1e2a6a4" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142702 1074 "Note that we can divide each term in pattern B by $3$ to get the corresponding term in pattern A:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n36 \\div 3 &= 12 \\\\\n30 \\div 3 &= 10 \\\\\n24 \\div 3 &= 8 \\\\\n18 \\div 3 &= 6 \\\\\n12 \\div 3 &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct statement is:\n\n- Each term in pattern A is **$3$ times smaller** than the corresponding term in pattern B." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142703 1069 "e-2128" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142703 1071 "The leftmost column of the table below defines two patterns, Pattern A and Pattern B. You're also given a single number that appears in each pattern.\n\n| Pattern A: subtract $5$ |  | $16$ |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Pattern B: subtract $15$ | $63$ |  |  |  |\n\nBy finding the missing numbers in each pattern, complete the following sentence:\n\n- Each term in pattern B is $\\underline{\\phantom{{}^{000000000000000000000}}}$ than the corresponding term in pattern A." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142703 1072 #uuid "d7d94d76-0cde-5249-be45-5a5aa4babeb3" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142703 1074 "First, we compute the missing numbers in the two patterns.\n\n- The rule of pattern A is \"subtract $5$\", and the second number is $16$. So, we have the following pattern:![](images/dc/dc077278e9fc5cd56591c7d0f16758e13c6b7296dfe49b38520cc2411a9185ed.png)\n- The rule of pattern B is \"subtract $15$\", and the first number is $63$. So, we have the following pattern:![](images/09/0962caa71326f7bb9d32ea8bcd487a8be1b8a2c56a33e4b8d2fb08f7debf33fd.png)\n\nSo we can fill in the table:\n\n| Pattern A: subtract $5$ | $21$ | $16$ | $11$ | $6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Pattern B: subtract $15$ | $63$ | $48$ | $33$ | $18$ |\n\nNote that we can multiply each term in pattern A by $3$ to get the corresponding term in pattern B:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n21 \\cdot 3 &= 63 \\\\\n16 \\cdot 3 &= 48 \\\\\n11 \\cdot 3 &= 33 \\\\\n6 \\cdot 3 &= 18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct statement is:\n\n- Each term in pattern B is **$3$ times larger** than the corresponding term in pattern A." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142704 1069 "e-2129" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142704 1071 "The leftmost column of the table below defines two patterns, Pattern A and Pattern B. You're also given the first number in each pattern.\n\n| Pattern A: add $3$ | $0$ |  |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Pattern B: add $18$ | $0$ |  |  |  |\n\nBy finding the missing numbers in each pattern, complete the following sentence:\n\n- Each term in pattern A is $\\underline{\\phantom{{}^{000000000000000000000}}}$ than the corresponding term in pattern B." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142704 1072 #uuid "d8e877c5-39b1-5287-be21-3ff4055efd5f" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142704 1074 "First, we compute the missing numbers in the two patterns.\n\n- The rule of pattern A is \"add $3$\", and the first number is $0$. So, we have the following pattern:![](images/fb/fbaa7ceb815c926a39dadbded56310fa4dae533391b621826082b838055d513d.png)\n- The rule of pattern B is \"add $18$\", and the first number is $0$. So, we have the following pattern:![](images/2b/2b41bc6d8aae1a484c01f995a69c5da094dc3564cbb878a90ce22ec2bfa5dba1.png)\n\nSo we can fill in the table:\n\n| Pattern A: add $3$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Pattern B: add $18$ | $0$ | $18$ | $36$ | $54$ |\n\nNotice that we can divide each term in pattern B by $6$ to get the corresponding term in pattern A:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0 \\div 6 &= 0 \\\\\n18 \\div 6 &= 3 \\\\\n36 \\div 6 &= 6 \\\\\n54 \\div 6 &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct statement is:\n\n- Each term in pattern A is **$6$ times smaller** than the corresponding term in pattern B." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142705 1069 "e-2130" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142705 1071 "Stephanie wants to find the relationship between the terms in patterns A and B below. She wants to do this without calculating all of the values.\n\n| Pattern A: add $7$ | $0$ |  |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Pattern B: add $21$ | $0$ |  |  |  |\n\nShe writes the following down in her notebook.\n\n- *Both patterns start from zero*\n- *The rule for pattern A is \"add $7$\".*\n- *The rule for pattern B is \"add $21$\".*\n- *Therefore, the terms in pattern B are $\\underline{\\phantom{{}^{0000000000000}}}$ times larger than the corresponding terms in pattern A.*\n\nWhat should be inserted into the blank space to make Stephanie's answer correct?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142705 1072 #uuid "93bc944b-6e38-5be0-9e02-0b4893d06fe6" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142705 1074 "Pattern A is \"add $\\color{red}7$\" and pattern B is \"add ${\\color{blue}21}$.\" Therefore, pattern B is growing faster, by a factor of\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{21}}\\div {\\color{red}{7}} =3 \\,\n$$\n\nSo the final point should be:\n\n- Therefore, the terms in pattern B are $3$ times larger than the corresponding terms in pattern A." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142706 1069 "e-2131" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142706 1071 "Find the numbers for the patterns shown in the table below and plot the corresponding graph.\n\n| Pattern A: add $2$ | $1$ |  |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Pattern B: multiply by $2$ | $1$ |  |  |  |" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142706 1072 #uuid "4826479c-fbbc-5577-ae84-e7f10f7f1702" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142706 1074 "The rule of pattern A is \"add $2$\", and the first number is $1$. So, we have the following pattern:\n\n![](images/11/11004332da0b3d1ff785c451b2d05962835cfa1251ba4eabe7583e5b18d861b9.png)\n\nThe rule of pattern B is \"multiply by $2$\", and the first number is $1$. So, we have the following pattern:\n\n![](images/69/69483006f1634704954bf3aa5d55b2b1a7aef6690686550d42c40b6e5b915d48.png)\n\nLet's fill in the table:\n\n| Pattern A: add $2$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Pattern B: multiply by $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n\nWe choose the horizontal axis ($x$-axis) to correspond to pattern A and the vertical axis ($y$-axis) to correspond to pattern B. We add one more row to the table with ordered pairs corresponding to each point.\n\n| Pattern A: add $2$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Pattern B: multiply by $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n| Ordered pair | $(1,1)$ | $(3,2)$ | $(5,4)$ | $(7,8)$ |\n\nPlotting each ordered pair on the coordinate plane, we get the following graph:\n\n![](images/e6/e6b3f5ac6218b16db0f72df02f6215d5d42254e31caa935d4cecf91c45b78b10.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142707 1069 "e-2132" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142707 1071 "What is the rule for pattern B, shown in the graph below?\n\n![](images/df/dfb9689fbd14a0f1f2482c24e8437cf7ee1ea830f045d9e9b0a7d2f7904a44dd.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142707 1072 #uuid "8a56329b-fff2-578a-b7e2-d9cd9aa4c5d4" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142707 1074 "The horizontal axis ($x$-axis) corresponds to pattern A, and the vertical axis ($y$-axis) corresponds to pattern B. We have the following coordinates of points:\n\n$$\n(1,10),\\, (2,20),\\, (3,40),\\, (4,80)\n$$\n\nSo, we can fill the table:\n\n| Ordered pair | $(1,10)$ | $(2,20)$ | $(3,40)$ | $(4,80)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Pattern A | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| Pattern B | $10$ | $20$ | $40$ | $80$ |\n\nLooking at pattern B only, we see that the rule is \"multiply by $2$\":\n\n![](images/45/45c1e55b3e08c0b756f8644e9045592f9366b7d072eb7aa6730dba6b064f71ea.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142708 1069 "e-2133" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142708 1071 "![](images/f7/f78e409e58a20bc8e8eff342dfbe3acd0e84d0675448fd4af362b90a33c56091.png)\n\nCreate a table that gives the pattern shown in the graph above." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142708 1072 #uuid "fe3a325b-b9e0-5f62-b0cf-b0534c28b08e" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142708 1074 "The horizontal axis ($x$-axis) corresponds to pattern A, and the vertical axis ($y$-axis) corresponds to pattern B. We have the following coordinates of points:\n$(1,10),\\, (3,8),\\, (5,6),\\, (7,4),\\, (9,2)$\n\nSo, we can fill the table:\n\n| Ordered pair | $(1,10)$ | $(3,8)$ | $(5,6)$ | $(7,4)$ | $(9,2)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Pattern A | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ | $9$ |\n| Pattern B | $10$ | $8$ | $6$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n\nRemoving the first row, we get:\n\n| Pattern A | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ | $9$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Pattern B | $10$ | $8$ | $6$ | $4$ | $2$ |" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142709 1069 "e-2134" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142709 1071 "What is missing from the following statement?\n\n$\\qquad$ Each term in pattern B is $\\underline{\\phantom{{}^{000000000000000000000}}}$ than the corresponding term in pattern A.\n\n![](images/1d/1de8040c785a651787eaf0b6898bf2af207ca4357b66f446db8267d8dbf33c23.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142709 1072 #uuid "e7f61e9d-90b4-587c-9b68-d077ff23a7b7" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142709 1074 "The horizontal axis ($x$-axis) corresponds to pattern A, and the vertical axis ($y$-axis) corresponds to pattern B. We have the following coordinates of points:\n\n$$\n(2,0),\\, (3,1),\\, (4,2),\\, (5,3)\n$$\n\nSo, we can fill the table:\n\n| Ordered pair | $(2,0)$ | $(3,1)$ | $(4,2)$ | $(5,3)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Pattern A | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| Pattern B | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n\nComparing the terms in both patterns, we note that we can add $2$ to each term of pattern B to get the corresponding term in pattern A:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0 + 2 &= 2 \\\\\n1 + 2 &= 3 \\\\\n2 + 2 &= 4 \\\\\n3 + 2 &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct statement is:\n\n$\\qquad$ Each term in pattern B is **$2$ smaller** than the corresponding term in pattern A." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142710 1069 "e-2147" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142710 1071 "If each cube represents one cubic unit, what is the volume of the figure?\n\n![](images/54/544b5a74ce1d8c5545d32df665df7d10ba4853cdf166ca02a3a03b46e2a97259.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142710 1072 #uuid "c041baf7-07b4-5c1a-969d-4324a3417f92" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142710 1074 "The figure consists of $10$ cubes.\n\n![](images/df/dfa571cbacb1f3e06ce70e05c127239cb159fdbc9adbd937df6120a5f214b120.png)\n\nTherefore, the volume is $10$ cubic units." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142711 1069 "e-2148" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142711 1071 "If each cube represents $1$ cubic unit, what is the volume of the figure?\n\n![](images/2e/2e9da7bdc1b3e20a197be4c7e2ab3a64abcbdd78dc16274bc314e73bdc6bd1ce.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142711 1072 #uuid "40f45207-a212-5fb2-8c5c-5625e367301f" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142711 1074 "The figure consists of $3$ rows, and each row has $5$ cubes.\n\n![](images/ba/baa53ae50449e9d56821925d9fbe5d2ebf694badd4e1e5e3c604fdcbf698a16f.png)\n\nSo, there are\n\n$$\n5 \\times 3 = 15\n$$\n\ncubes in total.\n\nTherefore, the volume of the figure is $15$ cubic units." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142712 1069 "e-2149" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142712 1071 "An object is assembled as shown below. Given that each small cube represents one cubic unit, what is the volume of the object?\n\n![](images/53/5304eed09b6d029958fbfcce95001c79a8a9dbdd91e7c8ae6eefa7d54db61a75.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142712 1072 #uuid "5316b5a3-dec6-5e2c-a14e-ee19c1040916" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142712 1074 "The figure consists of two layers:\n\n- The bottom layer consists of $4$ cubes.\n- The top layer consists of $1$ cube.\n\n![](images/4c/4c6372a05b21e7aa9f26717fdbb2cfe47e17d2d17215e9b7411e876b0e6473f5.png)\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\n4+1=5\n$$\n\ncubic units." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142713 1069 "e-2152" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142713 1071 "A rectangular prism has a length of $7 \\, \\text{ft}$, a width of $12 \\, \\text{ft}$, and a height of $2 \\, \\text{ft}$. What is the volume of the prism?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142713 1072 #uuid "8ad6541e-b295-5603-bca3-fb2dd33d90e8" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142713 1074 "The volume of the rectangular prism can be found as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= length \\cdot width \\cdot height \\\\\n&= 7 \\cdot 12 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 168\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, note the following:\n\n- The lengths of the prism are measured in feet $(\\text{ft})$.\n- Therefore, the volume is measured in **cubic feet** $(\\text{ft}^3)$.\n\nSo, we conclude that the prism's volume is $168\\, \\text{ft}^3$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142714 1069 "e-2153" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142714 1071 "What is the volume of the rectangular prism given below?\n\n![](images/08/084cd5db7b94dd4d1049d1dbbeb47bf3f04c58c5a78b700089951b15c85256f1.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142714 1072 #uuid "ea2d5e5c-a5d1-544c-bf08-a49b1bd995a5" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142714 1074 "In the picture, we have a rectangular prism with a length of $7 \\, \\text{m}$, a width of $3 \\, \\text{m}$, and a height of $6 \\, \\text{m}$.\n\nThe volume of the rectangular prism can be found as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= length \\cdot width \\cdot height \\\\\n&= 7 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 6 \\\\\n&= 126\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, note the following:\n\n- The lengths of the prism are measured in meters $(\\text{m})$.\n- Therefore, the volume is measured in cubic meters $(\\text{m}^3)$.\n\nSo, the volume is $126 \\, \\text{m}^3$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142715 1069 "e-2154" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142715 1071 "A rectangular prism has a base of area $15$ square units and a height of $5$ units. What is the volume of the prism?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142715 1072 #uuid "40573f2d-4f15-5fc0-9e97-6c424ec2c68d" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142715 1074 "The volume of the rectangular prism can be found as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= area of base \\cdot height \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&= 75\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the volume is $75$ cubic units." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142716 1069 "e-2155" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142716 1071 "![](images/f3/f36565da0c93fa7fd9e7320307b1f1f42638cd47bd3e333bb6a33f7e22e3c1c4.png)\n\nFind the volume of the rectangular prism shown above." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142716 1072 #uuid "78e0bd21-e2a1-542f-81be-6eb7422a617f" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142716 1074 "Notice that the given prism will have the same volume as the prism shown below.\n\n![](images/94/94ccd2f55cff4d0f4e2297ba2c6858aa8f4aab5c5fcfcbbec6bcf1ebce1ba4e1.png)\n\nThe rectangular prism above has a base of area $15 \\, \\text{cm}^2$ and a height of $6 \\, \\text{cm}$.\n\nThe volume of the rectangular prism can be found as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= area of base \\cdot height \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot 6 \\\\\n&= 90\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, note the following:\n\n- The lengths of the prism are measured in centimeters $(\\text{cm})$.\n- Therefore, the volume is measured in cubic centimeters $(\\text{cm}^3)$.\n\nSo, the volume is $90 \\, \\text{cm}^3$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142717 1069 "e-2159" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142717 1071 "A statue is mounted on a pedestal consisting of a concrete block. It is $3.5$ feet long, $3$ feet wide, and $2$ feet high. If a cubic foot of concrete requires $40$ pounds of gravel, how much gravel was used to build the pedestal? You may assume the pedestal takes the form of a rectangular prism." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142717 1072 #uuid "e0a5ba07-9967-5012-897e-2b8fcfc4d6fa" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142717 1074 "The volume of our pedestal is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= length \\cdot width \\cdot height = 3.5 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 = 21ft^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/00/0044f747f609704a1e711d8cf71ee5147af6922d0ba593cd785a12baffe32a12.png)\n\nSince each cubic foot of concrete requires $40$ pounds of gravel, we get\n\n$$\n40 \\times 21 = 840\n$$\n\nTherefore, $840$ pounds of gravel were used to make the pedestal." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142718 1069 "e-2160" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142718 1071 "Denise sends a package to her father. The total volume of the package is $75$ cubic inches, and it is $6$ inches wide and $2.5$ inches high. By modeling the package as a rectangular prism, what is the length of the package according to this model?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142718 1072 #uuid "24d4fbd9-9a1b-5736-ae95-b5cb844d887d" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142718 1074 "First, recall that the volume of a rectangular package can be calculated as\n\n$$\nV = \\text{length} \\times \\text{width} \\times \\text{height}\n$$\n\nand we are told that this volume is equal to $75 \\, \\text{in}^3$.\n\n![](images/32/32798500f85c9bc6dfc97b588d787ec9befd8a4f5b52aa0bc482588f71ca989f.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n$0$\n\nSo, since\n\n$$\n6 \\times 2.5 = 15\n$$\n\n, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n75 &= [math]\\,\\phantom{0}\\,[/math] \\cdot 6 \\cdot 2.5_(⏟)_(15) \\\\\n75 &= [math]\\,\\phantom{0}\\,[/math] \\cdot 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the relationship between multiplication and division, we obtain that the length of the package must be\n$0$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142719 1069 "e-2165" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142719 1071 "Place the following words into the sentence below.\n\n$\\qquad\\qquad$ (*Polygon*, *Quadrilateral*)\n\n$\\qquad$ A $\\text{___________}$ is a $\\text{___________}$ that has exactly $4$ sides." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142719 1072 #uuid "321c180e-7d06-598e-9c4c-983178ac9892" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142719 1074 "By the definition of a quadrilateral, the correct answer is\n\n$\\qquad$ A **quadrilateral** is a **polygon** that has exactly $4$ sides." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142720 1069 "e-2166" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142720 1071 "Which of the shapes below is a parallelogram?\n\n![](images/19/19b0cd25445bb3b065a0c80650e18bfe9d397101c54a1f431cf9678d38eec656.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142720 1072 #uuid "2579e471-9e0a-50ba-96bb-3f8ac4386b25" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142720 1074 "Let's examine the shapes one by one:\n\n- Shape I is a triangle since it has only $3$ sides.\n- Shape II is a trapezoid. It has $4$ sides and only one pair of parallel sides.\n- Shape III represents a parallelogram since it has $4$ sides and $2$ pairs of parallel sides.\n\n![](images/5b/5b451d1d67639a3c5cd74605b080bde9ae17cfbd8b3c60fc99945f7134f814c7.png)\n\nTherefore, only III is a parallelogram." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142721 1069 "e-2167" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142721 1071 "What word is missing from the sentence below?\n\n$\\qquad$ *Any $\\text{__________}$ has exactly $1$ pair of parallel sides.*\n\n1. parallelogram\n2. trapezoid\n3. quadrilateral" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142721 1072 #uuid "4c78ccfb-ae8c-5d3e-8b26-94b35930f829" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142721 1074 "The correct answer is II: **trapezoid**. The correct sentence is:\n\n$\\qquad$ *Any **trapezoid** has exactly $1$ pair of parallel sides.*\n\n![](images/db/dbfe53a441e6a3d1e2b9931b7cd14bcfd4aa2d3e388445ded6c1f0c557db4f3e.png)\n\nBy contrast, any parallelogram has exactly $2$ pairs of parallel sides, and a quadrilateral can have $0$, $1$, or $2$ pairs of parallel sides." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142722 1069 "e-22024" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142722 1071 "In a bank account, deposits are presented by positive numbers and withdrawals by negative numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- A transaction of $-\\[math]4$ means that $\\[/math]4$ were withdrawn.\n- If the balance changes from $-\\[math]3$ to $\\[/math]2$, it increases by $\\[math]5$.\n- A balance cannot be less than $\\[/math]0$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142722 1072 #uuid "52fead30-9f54-5141-990e-f0dd41f88749" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142722 1074 "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- A negative transaction means the number is on the left side of zero on the number line. The number $-4$ is $4$ units to the left of zero, which represents removing $4$ dollars from the account.![](images/aa/aaa17c446b687ba522a8cf12c6cd14d62fb71f80a4d5c3b23debf5144f595917.png)\nTherefore, this statement is *True.*\n\n- To find the increase, we count how many units the balance moves upward on the number line. From $-3$ to $0$ is an increase of $3$ dollars. From $0$ to $2$ is another increase of $2$ dollars.![](images/bf/bf02b81be6acc08de7d4aee41e97c8a73bba7364a49f6df434c9d7850c3da1e3.png)\nSo, the total increase is $3 + 2 = 5$ dollars.Therefore, this statement is *True.*\n- The number $0$ is on the number line exactly between positive and negative numbers. It represents a balance that is not below zero and not above zero. Since there could be negative balances, a balance could be less than $\\[math]0$.![](images/ce/ce3df042347c3d502d9b631e1c8978eaf1c5fd2348c1d8d1352af19058082a5e.png)Therefore, this statement is *False.*" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142723 1069 "e-2207" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142723 1071 "What division problem is represented by the model below?\n\n![](images/b1/b1cea7679d63c16d43e1f9b2f156cbd69057a6668fca2a4ee8bd868716631857.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142723 1072 #uuid "ea196aed-62c9-50a9-aeab-2332278befad" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142723 1074 "We have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{7}\n$$\n\non the left and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{21}\n$$\n\non the right.\n\n![](images/84/84cba43457adc42afc433238410fefc9fe98b042d326c10ea3fce6221e7e620c.png)\n\nTherefore, the division problem shown is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{7} \\div 3 = \\dfrac{5}{21}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142724 1069 "e-2208" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142724 1071 "What picture is missing from the division model below?\n\n![](images/bb/bb9bf65df4eb34dac86c30479de00123c796c82d3137b69d20a1d21eecae67c3.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142724 1072 #uuid "386c19cf-1f88-551b-b1bc-6f4baa142d2a" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142724 1074 "In the model, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{6}\n$$\n\non the left.\n\nSince we need to divide by $\\color{blue}2$, we split the whole into $\\color{blue}2$ equal parts horizontally.\n\n![](images/84/84f5fc54aa28636fd587cc712d039bdb984edec3dc3f5f22f891dbc1776fbd3e.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we obtain that the shaded part represents\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{12}\n$$\n\nof the whole.\n\nTherefore, the missing picture is the following:\n\n![](images/a6/a68a88f55f3f06c1f238927ae53df6a64348e2ab190673e445568cd1161d46c7.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142725 1069 "e-2209" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142725 1071 "What number is missing from the division problem below?\n\n![](images/0b/0b119e18348ad6f0ee31f2ad3e57cad4fc66403302df5590a8526ce39e930c78.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142725 1072 #uuid "e85814d8-b93e-573e-aca5-2c64e601d05a" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142725 1074 "We have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\non the left and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{20}\n$$\n\non the right.\n\n![](images/eb/eb114471d69ebd728a176b521ab410ab2b16d2bccc039e003faacd9c5d445e96.png)\n\nThe division operation splits each vertical line into $\\color{blue}5$ equal parts. So the shaded parts on the left are $\\color{blue}5$ times larger than the shaded parts on the right.\n\nTherefore, the division problem shown is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 3 4 \\div {\\color{blue}{5}} = \\dfrac{3}{20}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $\\color{blue}5$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142726 1069 "e-2210" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142726 1071 "Use the model below to calculate $\\dfrac{5}{6} \\div 4$.\n\n![](images/d1/d1c1c3842b04174137bfe8a938c70493493b2c9a0e74bee619d965446c9326a6.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142726 1072 #uuid "c0f9c9b1-08f9-5ae6-8583-0b7f93904a18" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142726 1074 "Since we need to divide by $\\color{blue}4$, we split the whole into $\\color{blue}4$ equal parts horizontally.\n\n![](images/24/24bffed596b77ba53d57e04ebb6baede437d40f825c03a794ae7689f11c3c250.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we obtain that the shaded part represents\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{24}\n$$\n\nof the whole.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{6} \\div 4 = \\dfrac{5}{24} \\,\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142727 1069 "e-2305" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142727 1071 "What is $9.888 \\div 24$?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142727 1072 #uuid "d9824a14-6214-5753-b312-7a25e8dbcd19" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142727 1074 "First, we write down the problem using long division notation. We keep the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $24$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe also bring the decimal point up into the quotient, placing it directly above the dot in the dividend:\n\n| $\\:\\: \\phantom{0} \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}$$24$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow, сarrying out the long division process as usual, we get:\n\n| $0$$\\!\\!. 4$$1$$2$$24$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\,. \\,} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$6$$2$$8$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$4$$4$$8$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$8$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n9.888 \\div 24 = 0.412\\,\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142728 1069 "e-2306" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142728 1071 "A pump delivered $367.5$ liters of water in $3.5$ hours. How many liters of water would the pump deliver every hour if it worked at a steady pace?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142728 1072 #uuid "38730f23-7bf6-5e1f-9536-c26ebefded46" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142728 1074 "To determine the number of liters of water the pump would deliver in $1$ hour, we need to calculate $367.5\\div 3.5$.\n\nWe can write this division problem as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{367.5}{3.5}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the numerator and denominator by $10$, we get a whole number in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{367.5 \\times 10}{3.5 \\times 10} = \\dfrac{3,675}{35}\n$$\n\nSo the problem is equivalent to $3,675 \\div 35$.\n\nNext, we proceed to use long division:\n\n| $1$$0$$5$$35\\,$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$5$$1$$7$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$0$$0$$1$$7$$5$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$7$$5$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n367.5\\div 3.5 = 105\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the pump would deliver $105$ liters of water every hour." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142729 1069 "e-2307" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142729 1071 "What is the value of $73.71 \\div 5.4$?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142729 1072 #uuid "5cc37af5-51d9-553e-a6a0-666b990139cc" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142729 1074 "We can write this division problem as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{73.71}{5.4}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the numerator and denominator by $10$, we get a whole number in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{73.71 \\times 10}{5.4 \\times 10} = \\dfrac{737.1}{54}\n$$\n\nSo the problem is equivalent to $737.1 \\div 54$.\n\nNext, we write down the problem using long division notation, where we keep the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $54$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 1}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe also bring the decimal point up into the quotient, placing it directly above the dot in the dividend:\n\n| $\\:\\,\\! \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid \\mid}} \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}\\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}$$54$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 1}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow, сarrying out the long division process as usual, we get:\n\n| $1$$3$$\\!\\!\\!. 6$$5$$54$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\,. \\,} 1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$4$$1$$9$$7$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$6$$2$$3$$5$$1$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$2$$4$$2$$7$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$7$$0$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n73.71 \\div 5.4 = 13.65\\,\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142730 1069 "e-2339" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142730 1071 "What is the greatest common factor of $3$ and $8$?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142730 1072 #uuid "616a4060-1176-5664-953b-5db0b99e925c" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142730 1074 "Let's list the factors of each number. Then, we find the largest number that appears in both lists:\n\n- Factors of $3$: $\\quad {\\color{blue}1}, 3$\n- Factors of $8$: $\\quad {\\color{blue}1}, 2, 4, 8$\n\nWe conclude that the greatest common factor of $3$ and $8$ is ${\\color{blue}{1}}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142731 1069 "e-2340" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142731 1071 "What is the greatest common factor of $18$ and $27$?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142731 1072 #uuid "288afd84-b751-5dc1-97ef-7a4c96069256" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142731 1074 "Let's list the factors of each number. Then, we find the largest number that appears in both lists:\n\n- Factors of $18$: $\\quad 1, 2, 3, 6, {\\color{blue}9}, 18$\n- Factors of $27$: $\\quad 1, 3, {\\color{blue}9}, 27$\n\nWe conclude that the greatest common factor of $18$ and $27$ is ${\\color{blue}{9}}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142732 1069 "e-2355" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142732 1071 "Which symbols could replace the empty box below to make the statement true?\n$1 \\,\\,\\square\\,\\, -2$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142732 1072 #uuid "0ab5e75b-30f7-5297-8f07-937f51a3a0fa" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142732 1074 "First, we plot both numbers on a number line.\n\n![](images/f8/f8819b324419956daed0ae806c26ecf834e4201ba8b62dbfd318bb476505aca5.png)\n\nWe see that $1$ is to the right of $-2$. Therefore, $1$ is *greater than* $-2$.\n\nSo, the correct statement must be:\n$1 > -2$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142733 1069 "e-2356" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142733 1071 "Which symbols could replace the empty box below to make the statement true?\n$-6 \\,\\,\\square\\,\\, 0$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142733 1072 #uuid "aa73a3e7-a4bc-517b-a379-77812e38ddbe" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142733 1074 "First, we plot both numbers on a number line.\n\n![](images/9b/9b1e088d9a6fd1d795eac3cbc7083c5086cce98394d02e19ef63fe5305e12a24.png)\n\nWe see that $-6$ is to the left of $0$. Therefore, $-6$ is *less than* $0$.\n\nSo, the correct statement must be:\n$-6 < 0$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142734 1069 "e-2358" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142734 1071 "Which symbols could replace the empty box below to make the statement true?\n$-5 \\,\\,\\,\\,\\square\\,\\,\\, {-2}$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142734 1072 #uuid "262cbbd1-c10e-590a-afdd-8354593fac26" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142734 1074 "First, we plot both numbers on a number line.\n\n![](images/1a/1a3e04bbcf04c16b7b171357d1aa8322c21f17f242ab26b7fd4b288704ada1b4.png)\n\nWe see that $-5$ is to the left of $-2$. Therefore, $-5$ is *less than* $-2$.\n\nSo, the correct statement must be:\n$-5 < - 2$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142735 1069 "e-2362" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142735 1071 "Which of the following is true regarding numbers $A$ and $B$?\n\n![](images/db/db52d93586d2b804039bd9449d645dc5898b40de6d4b28dd5f31006836025ae5.png)\n\n1. $A < 0$\n2. $0 > B$\n3. $B < A$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142735 1072 #uuid "75dfb8dc-62cd-538e-9394-01a472911259" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142735 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The number $A$ is to the left of zero. Therefore, $A$ is *smaller than* $0$, which we write as\n$A < 0$.\n- Statement II is true. The number $B$ is to the left of zero. Therefore, $B$ is *smaller than* $0$, which we write as\n$B < 0$.\nThis is the same as saying that $0$ is *greater than* $B$, which we write as\n$0 > B$.\n- Statement III is false. The number $A$ is to the left of $A$. Therefore, $A$ is *smaller than* $B$, which we write as\n$A < B$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142736 1069 "e-2364" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142736 1071 "What temperature is shown on the thermometer?\n\n![](images/50/50bd31328da17519b21aab92789f66dcdb71475baffded557ff99dcc250b01ae.png)" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142736 1072 #uuid "a1409a37-c07d-54e9-9bda-d189ca134fcd" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142736 1074 "The fluid in the thermometer is at the same level as $-5$ on the scale, and the temperature is measured in degrees Celsius. Therefore, the thermometer shows $-5^\\circ\\textrm{C}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142737 1069 "e-2365" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142737 1071 "What is the opposite number to $4$?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142737 1072 #uuid "4bff1f60-94e2-5393-b594-eef7766997fc" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142737 1074 "The number $4$ is a distance of $4$ from zero, as shown on the number line.\n\n![](images/8f/8f2722fe9629599ea16e9be61aa45f8dcd6f2c9f1a34438fe8449c7eafe27aba.png)\n\nWhen we place a negative sign in front of a number, we're finding the **opposite** number. The opposite number has the same distance from zero, but is on the opposite side of zero:\n\n![](images/da/da2312461963013a0085d6bc2f111fdc983cab9365d02ccbb871c92050dadb58.png)\n\nTherefore, $-(4) = -4$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142738 1069 "e-2368" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142738 1071 "Which temperature is $5^\\circ\\textrm{C}$ warmer than $-3^\\circ\\textrm{C}$?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142738 1072 #uuid "a140ddce-f9eb-5eac-b18f-4c55c09e1848" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142738 1074 "Let's imagine $-3^\\circ\\,\\textrm{C}$ on a thermometer.\n\n![](images/90/908fbd08ba3e531dae52fcaec12ac19e86606fe70ec00b6375a1ac8bacd258b3.png)\n\nTo find a temperature that is $5^\\circ\\,\\textrm{C}$ warmer, the fluid needs to move up by $5$ units. This gives:\n\n![](images/6d/6d8f7e1689f318cf8a5ef3895a637f7b367708fedfb615c7e4ca4489e4855d4a.png)\n\nTherefore, $2^\\circ\\textrm{C}$ is $5^\\circ\\textrm{C}$ warmer than $-3^\\circ\\textrm{C}$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142739 1069 "e-2512" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142739 1071 "Alex has $m$ marbles, and Jason has $n$ marbles. The relationship between $m$ and $n$ is given by the equation\n$m = n + 10$.\nIf Jason has $15$ marbles, how many does Alex have?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142739 1072 #uuid "991274c6-8baa-58cf-87bd-cf351f2c2200" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142739 1074 "To find the number of marbles that Alex has, we substitute\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{n}}={\\color{blue}{15}}\n$$\n\ninto the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= n + 10 \\\\\nm &= 15 + 10 \\\\\nm &= 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Alex has $25$ marbles." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142740 1069 "e-2513" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142740 1071 "Find $p$, if $p = 4q - 1$ and $q = 6$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142740 1072 #uuid "a18dae86-ebd8-5638-82a4-4de08ac7e4fc" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142740 1074 "To find the value of $p$, we substitute\n\n$$\n\\color{blue}{q}=6\n$$\n\ninto the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np &= 4q - 1 \\\\\np &= 4 \\cdot 6 - 1 \\\\\np &= 24 - 1 \\\\\np &= 23\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142741 1069 "e-2514" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142741 1071 "Carmen is $6$ years younger than Antonio.\n\nIf $c$ represents Carmen's age and $a$ represents Antonio's age, what is the relationship between $c$ and $a$?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142741 1072 #uuid "fd613658-964f-56d6-8963-b880c9ab6e90" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142741 1074 "Suppose that Antonio is $18$ years old. Then Carmen is\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}18} - 6 = {\\color{red}12}\n$$\n\nyears old.\n\nIf Antonio is $19$ years old, then Carmen is\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}19} - 6 = {\\color{red}13}\n$$\n\nyears old.\n\nIf Antonio is $20$ years old, then Carmen is\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}20} - 6 = {\\color{red}14}\n$$\n\nyears old.\n\nAs we can see from the above examples, in order to find out Carmen's age, we must subtract $6$ from Antonio's age.\n\nSo, if Antonio is ${\\color{blue}a}$ years old and Carmen is ${\\color{red}a}$ years old, then we must have:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}a} - 6 = {\\color{red}c}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the relation between $c$ and $a$ is:\n\n$$\nc = a - 6\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142742 1069 "e-2515" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142742 1071 "Johana gets $8$ identical pieces of cake for each cake she makes.\n\nIf $p$ represents the number of pieces of cake and $c$ represents the number of cakes, then which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $c = 8p$\n2. $p$ is the dependent variable\n3. $c$ is the independent variable" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142742 1072 #uuid "b427ce00-45a4-53c7-9bc1-9da48c5701b0" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142742 1074 "First, let's construct a two-variable equation for our situation.\n\n- If Johana makes ${\\color{blue}1}$ cake, she gets $8 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 8$ pieces of cake.\n- If Johana makes $\\color{blue}2$ cakes, she gets $8 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 16$ pieces of cake.\n- If Johana makes $\\color{blue}3$ cakes, she gets $8 \\times {\\color{blue}3} = 24$ pieces of cake.$\\qquad\\ldots$\n- If Johana makes $\\color{blue}c$ cakes, she gets $8 \\times {\\color{blue}c} = 8c$ pieces of cake.\n\nTherefore, we get the equation\n$p = 8c$.\nThus, statement I is false.\n\nFrom the equation the number of pieces of cake ($p$) depends on the number of cakes ($p$) Johana makes. So, we have that\n\n- $p$ is the **dependent** variable, and\n- $c$ is the **independent** variable.\n\nThus, statements II and III are true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142743 1069 "e-8821" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142743 1071 "![](images/e6/e6d9bfaafe038e3629b0d2110dba000b003fce84009e948fd0f33cc493db63a5.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the statement below?\n\n$0$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142743 1072 #uuid "a81e0edb-8f1c-5238-9292-80648038da53" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142743 1074 "Let's compare the two shapes.\n\n- For the shape on the left:There are $3$ parts of the shape in total. Of them, $2$ parts are shaded. So, $\\dfrac{2}{3}$ of the shape is shaded.\n- For the shape on the right:There are $6$ parts of the shape in total. Of them, $4$ parts are shaded. So, $\\dfrac{4}{6}$ of the shape is shaded.\n\nBoth shaded regions have the same area. Therefore, the two fractions are equivalent, and we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing value is $\\color{blue}4$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142744 1069 "e-8822" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142744 1071 "![](images/c8/c802abdbf9969b22535548df5c0e554f9e6fd5539dc528c0ae27a88d4844f9ff.png)\n\nGiven the picture above, what fraction is equivalent to $\\dfrac{8}{10}$?" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142744 1072 #uuid "78d16d6f-cf95-5998-a10f-0aa032b87cf3" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142744 1074 "Let's compare the two shapes.\n\n- For the shape on the left:There are $10$ parts of the shape in total. Of them, $8$ parts are shaded. So, $\\dfrac{8}{10}$ of the shape is shaded.\n- For the shape on the right:There are $5$ parts of the shape in total. Of them, $4$ parts are shaded. So, $\\dfrac{4}{5}$ of the shape is shaded.\n\nBoth shaded regions have the same area. Therefore, the two fractions are equivalent, and we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{10} = {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{4}{5}}\n$$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142745 1069 "e-8823" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142745 1071 "![](images/94/94c0b0551d2f7ec745b30d8edd08a45257f6f3daf581798ee97b82ba277e5fee.png)\n\nThe fraction given below is equivalent to the fraction shown in the picture. What number is missing?\n\n$0$" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142745 1072 #uuid "0198caea-8d1f-5fdb-9f62-68530123150d" 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142745 1074 "There are $4$ equal parts in the given shape in total. Of them, $3$ parts are shaded. So,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nof the shape is shaded.\n\nSince we are looking for a fraction of the form $0$, we now draw another shape that has the same shaded area but is divided into $8$ equal parts. To do that, we subdivide each part into $2$ equal pieces:\n\n![](images/23/238f7318c49d063a62e048a5f47d61d20af26c6ee80b84029f07e0acec1c9f39.png)\n\nThe shape on the right is divided into $8$ equal parts. Of them, $6$ parts are shaded. So,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{8}\n$$\n\nof the shape is shaded.\n\nHence, we obtain\n$6$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $\\color{blue}6$." 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142746 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050317 13194139533362 17592186045585 true] [17592186142746 1038 #edb/ref 17592186143489 13194139533362 17592186